SYNTH-0001♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Hill♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the hospital for six weeks in 2019 after a series of complications from a routine knee replacement, and what I remember most is not the machines or the monitors but the one surgeon who would come in at 7am and actually sit down and talk to her. Not stand in the doorway, not glance at a screen. Sit down. That relationship is what I keep thinking about when I read that HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care.⏎⏎The question I want to address is what guardrails or conditions should accompany AI tools before they are deployed in direct patient care. My answer is that the guardrails being discussed are not adequate and in some cases they may not be possible to design well enough to matter.⏎⏎Here is what worries me most concretely. AI systems trained on historical data will inherit whatever was wrong with that history. We already know from published research, including a widely cited 2019 study in Science magazine by Obermeyer and colleagues, that a commercial algorithm used by hospitals across the country systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare spending as a proxy for health need. Black patients had been given less care historically, so the algorithm concluded they needed less care going forward. The system appeared to be working. It had metrics and validation scores. It was being sold to hospitals. And it was quietly making racial disparities worse.⏎⏎Now HHS wants to accelerate this. I understand the appeal. Clinicians are burned out. Administrative burdens are real. But I do not think the answer to physician burnout is to hand more decision-making to systems that cannot be held accountable when they are wrong.⏎⏎There is something that gets lost in the efficiency framing that I cannot quite put into words except to say this: when a doctor makes a mistake, there is a person you can ask why. You can look them in the eye. You can file a complaint. When an algorithm makes a mistake, the hospital says the vendor is responsible, the vendor says the hospital deployed it incorrectly, and the patient's family is left with nothing. I have seen this happen to people I know and it is infuriating in a way that is hard to explain to people who haven't experienced it.⏎⏎I am also genuinely skeptical about the workforce claims. Every technology that was supposed to free up time for care has, in practice, often just increased documentation expectations or been used to justify not hiring another nurse. EHRs were going to transform care and reduce administrative burden. Instead clinicians now spend more time on documentation than they did before, according to multiple surveys. Why would AI be different if the institutional pressures are the same?⏎⏎My specific request to HHS is to slow down. Require independent prospective validation, not just vendor-supplied testing. Require demographic breakdowns of error rates before any system is approved for use. Require that hospitals disclose to patients when AI is being used to inform their care or their insurance denials. Require that there be a named human being responsible for every AI-assisted decision, someone who cannot legally point to the algorithm as the reason they cannot explain a choice.⏎⏎I also want to say something about insurance claim denials specifically because I think that use case is particularly alarming and I notice it doesn't get as much attention as the clinical side. When insurers use AI to deny claims, they can process hundreds of thousands of denials with a review time that in some reported cases has been under a second per claim. Cigna was reported in 2023 to have used a system that allowed physicians to reject claims without reviewing individual files. That is not a hypothetical risk. That already happened. Accelerating AI adoption in that context is not improving care, it is industrializing the denial of care.⏎⏎I don't have a tidy way to end this except to say that I think the framing of this RFI is already pointing in the wrong direction. Asking how to accelerate adoption assumes adoption is the goal. I'd rather HHS ask first whether adoption in a given use case is actually good for patients, and I'm not confident that question is being asked with enough seriousness. The burden of proof should be on the technology, and right now the process for meeting that burden feels♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0002♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Johnson♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in 2021 after a medication error that nobody caught until her kidneys started failing. The error was caught, eventually, by a nurse who noticed something felt off. Not a dashboard. Not an algorithm. A person who had been watching her for three days and knew something had changed. I keep thinking about that whenever I read about how AI is going to transform clinical care and make it safer and more efficient.⏎⏎The question I want HHS to actually sit with is this: what happens to accountability when a machine is involved in a decision that harms someone? Because right now, if a doctor prescribes the wrong dose, there is a person who is responsible. A license. A name. You can ask them what they were thinking. With an AI recommendation that a clinician "approved," that chain gets murky very fast. The clinician says they were following the system. The hospital says the vendor validated it. The vendor says it was used outside its approved parameters. I have watched this happen already with prior authorization AI tools that insurance companies use to deny care, and it is genuinely hard to get anyone to own the decision.⏎⏎I understand the argument that AI could catch errors like the one that almost killed my mother. Maybe. But the reason that nurse caught the problem was not that she had access to better data. The data was right there in the chart. She caught it because she was paying attention to a human being over time. If you staff a unit with fewer nurses because you have an AI monitoring system, you may actually get worse outcomes even if the AI is technically more accurate than any individual nurse on any individual data point. The interactions matter. The presence matters. I'm not sure how you measure that or put it in a cost-benefit analysis, but that doesn't mean it isn't real.⏎⏎There is also something that bothers me about the framing of this whole RFI. It asks how HHS can accelerate AI adoption. The assumption is that faster adoption is better. But a lot of AI tools in healthcare right now are being deployed before anyone has really studied them in the populations where they're being used. The dermatology AI systems that have worse accuracy on darker skin tones is the famous example, but that problem is not limited to dermatology. Training data in American healthcare reflects decades of unequal access and documentation, and a system trained on that data is going to encode those inequities even if nobody intends it to.⏎⏎HHS should be asking harder questions about what independent, post-market surveillance of these tools looks like, not just how to get them deployed faster. Right now vendors self-report performance data, which is obviously not sufficient. If a pharmaceutical company told us to trust their own efficacy data without independent review we would laugh them out of the room, but somehow with AI tools the evidentiary bar is much lower.⏎⏎I also want to say something about administrative AI because I think it gets lumped in with clinical AI as if it's safer or less consequential. Prior authorization algorithms are administrative. They are also directly causing people to be denied chemotherapy and insulin. The people who used to make those decisions were not perfect, but they were human beings who could be reasoned with, appealed to, and held accountable by state insurance commissioners and courts. When an algorithm denies a claim, the appeals process is often designed to be so burdensome that most people give up.⏎⏎My specific ask is that HHS not treat acceleration as a goal in itself. Before any further regulatory action to speed adoption, the department should commission an independent review of post-market surveillance mechanisms for AI tools already in use in clinical and insurance settings, with particular attention to how adverse events are defined, reported, and investigated when AI is involved in the care pathway. The FDA has a framework for this with drugs and devices. There is no equivalent for most of the AI tools being deployed right now, including the ones making coverage decisions for Medicare patients.⏎⏎I don't know how HHS weighs all of this against the genuine potential benefits. I am not saying AI has no role ever. But right now the pressure is coming almost entirely from vendors and health systems that see cost savings, and♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0003♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Rodriguez♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔Association for Molecular Pathology♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Vincent's in Indianapolis in February 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that took nearly 36 hours to catch. During that time I watched nurses and doctors do something no algorithm can replicate: they argued with each other. They disagreed. They changed their minds when my mother, who was confused and frightened and not giving a clear history, finally said something offhand about a medication she'd stopped taking months before. That offhand comment changed her treatment. A pattern-matching system working off her chart would never have waited for it.⏎⏎I want to focus my comments on what the RFI describes as opportunities to accelerate AI adoption in clinical decision-making, because that's the area I find most troubling.⏎⏎The framing of this RFI assumes that speed of adoption is itself a goal. I don't think it is. Medicine is one of the few fields where being slower and more deliberate is often the right answer. The question isn't how do we get more AI into clinical care faster, it's whether we have any real evidence that doing so makes patients safer or healthier in the actual messy conditions of real hospitals, not in controlled pilots with well-curated data sets. The evidence on that is a lot thinner than the vendor presentations would suggest.⏎⏎What worries me practically is the deployment gap. A system can be validated in one academic medical center with a particular patient population and EHR configuration, and then sold to a community hospital in rural Georgia with completely different demographics, staffing ratios, and documentation practices, and nobody is systematically tracking whether it still works. The FDA's current framework for software as a medical device doesn't fully close this gap. Hospitals aren't equipped to audit these tools on their own. So what happens is the tool gets used, something goes wrong eventually, and then there's a long argument about whether the clinician deviated from the recommendation or followed it too closely, and the vendor points to the terms of service, and the hospital points to the vendor, and the patient or the patient's family is left holding nothing.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in healthcare at all. I work in health policy and I understand the arguments about diagnostic imaging and sepsis alerts and medication reconcilliation. But there's a difference between a narrow tool with a clear validation record and a defined use case, and the broader push to embed AI into clinical workflows in ways that change how decisions get made and who feels responsible for them. The second thing is what this RFI seems to be encouraging, and I think HHS should pump the brakes.⏎⏎The employment dimension also deserves more attention than the RFI gives it. When hospitals talk about AI reducing administrative burden, what they often mean in practice is that they want to do the same work with fewer people. That's not necessarily bad in every case but it's not neutral either. Medical coders, prior authorization reviewers, care coordinators, these are jobs held disproportionately by women and by workers without four-year degrees, and the displacement of those jobs doesn't get offset by the productivity gains that flow to health systems and insurers.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require prospective outcome tracking for any AI tool deployed in clinical or coverage decision contexts before offering any regulatory or reimbursement incentives for adoption. Not retrospective review after something goes wrong. Prospective, standardized, publicly reported data on whether the tool is actually doing what it claims in the population where it's actually being used.⏎⏎I'd also ask the Department to be honest about the fact that some of the pressure to adopt AI quickly is coming from vendors with significant financial stakes in this RFI's outcome, and that the framing of "accelerating adoption" reflects those interests more than it reflects♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0004♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Brown♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔Center for Democracy and Technology♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Brigham and Women's Hospital in Boston in February of 2023, and what I watched during those eleven days was not primarily a technical process. It was a series of human judgment calls made under pressure by people who knew her name, watched her face, and talked to our family at two in the morning when we were scared out of our minds. I keep thinking about that experience when I read about how HHS wants to "accelerate" AI adoption in clinical care, and honestly my stomach drops a little.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to AI adoption and what the department can do to remove them. I want to suggest that some of those "barriers" are actually there for good reasons, and that the framing of this whole exercise worries me. When you set up a process asking specifically how to speed something up, you've already decided the direction. You're not really asking whether to go, just how fast.⏎⏎Here is what I think gets lost in the efficiency argument. Medicine is not mostly an information retrieval problem. The nurses who took care of my mother were not primarily processing data points. They were watching her breathe, noticing when she seemed more agitated than the monitors showed, pushing back on a resident's order because something felt off to them based on thirty years of experience. I don't know how you encode that in a training dataset. And I'm genuinely uncertain whether anyone does, or whether the people selling these systems are being honest about that uncertainty.⏎⏎The bias issue is something I feel strongly about. These systems are trained on historical records, and historical records in American medicine are full of documented disparities in how pain is assessed, how symptoms are taken seriously, which patients get aggressive treatment and which get palliative pathways steered at them early. A 2021 study in JAMA looked at sepsis prediction algorithms and found meaningful differences in performance across racial groups, and that's one study about one condition. We don't actually know the scope of how these biases are baked in because the vendors often won't disclose their training data or their validation populations. HHS accelerating adoption before that transparency exists seems backwards to me.⏎⏎What concerns me almost as much as the clinical safety piece is what happens to the workforce. I have a friend who works as a medical coder, has done it for fifteen years, and she's been told her department is being "augmented" by AI tools that will let them handle twice the volume with the same headcount. That's not augmentation, that's a staffing cut that hasn't happened yet. And when errors occur in that environment, who is responsible? The coder who approved a code she barely had time to review? The software vendor? The hospital administrator who decided the productivity targets were achievable?⏎⏎The accountability question is the one I can't get past. In the current system, when something goes wrong in a hospital, there is at least a theoretical chain of human responsibility. It's imperfect and often fails patients badly, I know that. But at least it exists. When you insert an AI system into that chain, responsibility gets distributed in ways that are genuinely hard to trace. The clinician says she was following the system's recommendation. The hospital says the clinician should have exercised independent judgment. The vendor says the system was used outside its validated parameters. Patients and families end up in a maze.⏎⏎I'm not saying technology has no role in medicine. I'm saying HHS should be asking much harder questions before it starts clearing regulatory pathways and building reimbursement incentives designed to push these tools into clinical settings faster. The question shouldn't be "what barriers can we remove" but "what safeguards do we need before any of this scales."⏎⏎And frankly I'm not sure the current post-market surveillance infrastructure for software as a medical device is anywhere close to adequate for what's being contemplated here. The FDA's Digital Health Center of Excellence has been working on this for years and the framework still has enormous gaps, particularly around algorithm updates and how to handle drift in model performance over time without triggering a full re-review.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0005♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Nelson♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔American Pharmacists Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in March of 2023, and I watched what good clinical care actually looks like. Her attending physician, Dr. Okafor, spent maybe forty minutes with her on one of those days just sitting and asking questions, not about her labs, but about her life, her fear of being a burden, the fact that she hadn't told her sister how sick she was. That conversation changed her treatment plan. No algorithm would have found that. I don't think any algorithm could.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS is looking at ways to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my first reaction was genuinely just dread. Not because I'm opposed to technology in general, but because I've spent enough time in hospitals and clinics to know that the thing people are trying to automate is often the thing that cannot be automated without losing something essential.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to AI adoption, and I want to answer the question slightly differently: some of those barriers exist for good reason and HHS should think very carefully before removing them.⏎⏎Here's what concerns me most. When a hospital or an insurer deploys an AI tool for clinical decision support, or for prior authorization, or for flagging high-risk patients, there is an almost immediate diffusion of accountability. The clinician can say the algorithm recommended it. The hospital can say the vendor built it. The vendor can say the training data was the problem, or that the user didn't follow the deployment guidelines. I have seen this happen already with prior authorization tools. In 2023 a Senate investigation found that UnitedHealth's nH Predict algorithm was denying post-acute care claims at a rate that bore essentially no relationship to the clinical judgment of the treating physicians, and the company's defense was more or less that the tool was a guide, not a decision-maker. That is exactly the kind of accountability gap that gets people hurt.⏎⏎And I think the safety argument is being systematically underweighted in this conversation. A tool that performs well on average can still fail badly for specific patients, and medicine is full of patients who are not average. Elderly patients with multiple comorbidities, patients with rare genetic conditions, patients whose symptoms don't fit the textbook presentation, patients whose records are incomplete because they've moved or been uninsured for years. These are exactly the patients who get hurt by pattern-matching systems trained on data that didn't include them in sufficient numbers. I keep thinking about the well-documented problems with pulse oximetry in patients with darker skin tones, which took decades to fully acknowledge despite being a relatively simple device. AI systems are far more complex and the bias is far harder to see.⏎⏎I'm also worried about what AI does to the clinician workforce over time. The argument that AI reduces burnout is plausible in narrow cases, but the same tools can easily become productivity monitors, standardization mechanisms, and justification for staffing cuts. If a hospital can demonstrate that an AI flags 85% of deteriorating patients before a nurse would, that creates pressure to run thinner nursing ratios. That's not hypothetical. There are already hospital systems piloting "AI-assisted" monitoring as part of arguments to state regulators that minimum staffing ratios are unnecesary.⏎⏎HHS asks what it can do to accelerate adoption. My answer is that acceleration is not obviously the right goal right now. What would actually serve patients is rigorous post-market surveilance requirements, mandatory disclosure to patients when AI is involved in a clinical or coverage decision, and clear rules about who is legally liable when an AI-influenced decision causes harm. None of those things require slowing down beneficial innovation, but they do require treating AI in healthcare as something that needs to earn trust rather than something that should be assumed trustworthy until proven otherwise.⏎⏎The RFI doesn't ask directly about liability frameworks, but I'd urge HHS to coordinate with whoever does have jurisdiction over that question because the gap there is real and it's going to matter before very long.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0006♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Taylor♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔Carbon Health♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Advocate Lutheran General Hospital in Park Ridge, Illinois in February of 2023. She had a septic infection that the attending physicians initially thought was a straightforward UTI. What saved her was not a pattern-matching algorithm. It was a night nurse who noticed that her breathing had changed in a way that didn't match any of the numbers on the monitors, who called the resident, who called the attending, who made a judgment call at 2am based on thirty years of watching people get sick. That chain of human attention and human responsibility is exactly what I'm afraid gets broken when we start routing clinical decisions through systems that nobody can fully explain and nobody is clearly accountable for.⏎⏎I want to address specifically the question of what HHS should do to "accelerate" AI adoption in clinical settings, because I think the framing of this RFI is itself the problem. The whole document assumes that faster adoption is the goal and asks only how to remove the obstacles. But the obstacles are sometimes there for good reasons. Validation requirements, liability frameworks, physician oversight rules - these aren't just bureaucratic friction. They exist because healthcare errors kill people and someone needs to be answerable when that happens.⏎⏎What worries me practically is the insurance side of this. AI-driven prior authorization systems are already being deployed by major payers and there is documented evidence that some of these systems have denial rates that far exceed what physician reviewers would produce. A ProPublica and CBS News investigation in 2023 found that UnitedHealth's nH Predict algorithm was flagging patients for discharge from rehabilitation facilities at rates that internal reviewers couldn't justify clinically. That's not theoretical harm. Those are real patients who got less care because an algorithm said so and because the system made it difficult for anyone to push back effectively. Accelerating AI adoption in that context doesn't mean better care. It means faster, cheaper, and harder to contest.⏎⏎There's also something I keep coming back to which is the question of who actually benefits from moving quickly here. The vendors benefit. Large hospital systems that can afford to buy and implement these tools benefit, at least in terms of cost reduction. I'm genuinely uncertain whether patients benefit on average, and I'm pretty confident that patients in under-resourced settings, rural hospitals, federally qualified health centers, places that can't afford the good implementations and will end up with whatever's cheapest, those patients may end up worse off. I don't see this RFI grappling seriously with that distributional question.⏎⏎I'm not saying no AI tools should ever be used in healthcare. I'm saying that "accelerate" is the wrong verb. The right question is how do we ensure that any tool that influences a clinical or coverage decision has been validated in populations that look like the patients who will actually be affected by it, and that there is a real human being whose professional license is on the line if it goes wrong. Right now neither of those conditions is reliably met.⏎⏎HHS should be requiring much more rigorous post-market surveillance for AI tools that are already deployed, not looking for ways to lower the bar for new ones. The FDA's current framework for software as a medical device has significant gaps when it comes to adaptive algorithms that continue to change after they're cleared, and the agency has acknowledged this. Before the department encourages broader adoption, it should close that gap.⏎⏎I also want to say something about administrative AI because this RFI keeps implying that clinical AI and administrative AI are separable and that administrative uses are lower risk. They're not always. Scheduling algorithms that triage urgency, documentation tools that summarize patient histories for physicians who then don't read the underlying notes, coding tools that determine what diagnoses get submitted to payers - all of these have downstream effects on care. A physician who is reviewing an AI-generated summary of a patient's chart instead of reading the chart is not in the same epistemic position as one who read it themselves. That matters and I don't think it's being taken seriously enough in how these tools are being categorized and regulated.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0007♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Thomas♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔University of Texas at Austin Dell Medical School♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that got missed for almost 36 hours. I'm not going to pretend I know exactly what went wrong. But I know this: the thing that finally caught it was a nurse who had been on that floor for nineteen years and noticed something was off about how my mother was breathing. Not a number on a screen. A person who had seen enough sick people to know when something wasn't adding up.⏎⏎I bring this up because the framing of this RFI bothers me. The question being asked is essentially how do we speed this up, and I think that's the wrong starting point. The question should be whether we should be doing this at all, or at least whether we should be doing it as fast and broadly as the vendors and health systems are pushing for.⏎⏎Medicine is not primarily an information problem. I know that's not a popular thing to say right now, but I believe it. A patient who comes in terrified and can't describe their symptoms clearly, or an elderly person with no family present who doesn't really understand what the doctor is asking, or someone whose chart is full of errors from a previous provider who didn't really know them -- none of that is a pattern matching problem. It's a relationship problem. A judgment problem. And when you put an AI layer between the clinician and the patient, even if the AI is technically accurate, you are changing what the encounter is. You are making it more transactional. The clinician starts managing recommendations instead of actually thinking. I've read enough about automation bias to know that people tend to follow what the machine says even when they shouldn't.⏎⏎The bias issue is also something I want to flag specifically. There's documented research -- the Obermeyer et al. study from 2019 in Science is probably the most cited example -- showing that widely deployed commercial algorithms systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because they used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients had been historically denied care and therefore had lower costs. That algorithm was used on millions of patients before the problem was identified. Now we're talking about deploying AI much more broadly, much faster, with much less scrutiny. The fact that one tool got caught doesn't mean the others will.⏎⏎What really worries me though is the insurance side. The question of AI in clinical care is partly a question about what counts as medically necessary and who decides. If insurers are using AI to generate denial recommendations, and they are, and those systems are opaque, and the clinician who wants to appeal has to fight through a prior authorization process that is already exhausting, adding AI into that chain makes accountability worse not better. There's no one to argue with. There's no one who made the decision. There's just a score.⏎⏎I'm also not persuaded by the argument that AI will reduce clinician burnout. That argument is convenient for hospital systems that want to reduce staff. The Electronic Health Record was also sold as something that would make clinicians' lives easier. Ask any physician what they think of their EHR documentation burden now. The tools that promised to free up time have mostly added more data entry, more compliance checkboxes, more things to click through. I don't see why AI tools deployed in a similar institutional context would behave differently.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require pre-deployment validation studies that are specific to the patient population and care setting where a tool will actually be used, not just the population where it was trained. It should require that any AI used in coverage or denial decisions be subject to independent audit. It should require that clinicians have a documented, functional way to override AI recommendations without it affecting their performance metrics or generating additional paperwork.⏎⏎But honestly my larger concern is that this RFI is being conducted in a way that treats acceleration as the default and caution as the thing that needs to be justified. I'd like to see HHS ask a different question: what evidence do we require before allowing AI to influence clinical decisions, and who bears the burden of proof. Right now it feels like the burden is on patients and skeptics to prove harm after the fact, which is backwards.⏎⏎The nurse who noticed something wrong with my mother's breathing had no algorithm. She had experience and attention and the professional obligation to trust her own judgment. I don't know how you quantify that or whether an AI system trained on a million sepsis cases would have caught what she caught. Maybe it would have. But maybe it would have flagged fifty other patients that day as higher priority and she would have been in a different room.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0008♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Baker♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Inova Fairfax Hospital in 2022 after a sepsis diagnosis that almost got missed entirely, and what saved her was a night-shift nurse who noticed she was shivering in a way that didn't match her chart. Not an algorithm. A person who had been doing that job for sixteen years and recognized something she couldn't have put into a data field. I've been thinking about that a lot since I heard about this RFI.⏎⏎What bothers me most about the framing of this whole effort is the assumption that the problem in American healthcare is insufficient automation. That the answer to a system that already feels impersonal, rushed, and transactional is to add more tools that process people as inputs and outputs. My mother, during those eleven days, was scared and sometimes confused and occasionally said things that didn't make sense because she was running a fever of 103. An AI system trained on patient records would have had a very hard time with her. The nurse knew to discount certain things she said and weight other things more heavily, and that judgment came from being a human being in a room with another human being.⏎⏎I understand there are people who will say AI is just a tool, that clinicians will still make the final call, that this is about reducing administrative burden not replacing doctors. I've heard that argument and I don't entirely dismiss it. But I've also watched what happens when institutions get a new tool that promises efficiency. The tool gets used more broadly than intended, the humans who were supposed to remain "in the loop" get stretched thinner because the tool is supposedly handling things, and within a few years the original caution is forgotten. That's not speculation, that's just how healthcare organizations respond to cost pressure.⏎⏎The specific question I want to address from this RFI is the one about reimbursement structures that could accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings. My concern is that if Medicare and Medicaid start reimbursing AI-assisted diagnosis or AI-assisted prior authorization review at higher rates, or if payers start preferring or requiring AI tools as a condition of contracts, you will create a financial incentive that runs way ahead of any evidence base. We already have a problem in this country where medical devices and software get deployed at scale before we really understand their failure modes. The FDA's 510(k) clearance process has been criticized for decades for allowing devices to reach patients without rigorous clinical testing, and AI diagnostic tools present a similar or worse problem because their failures can be subtle, correlated across large populations, and invisible until something goes badly wrong.⏎⏎The bias issue is real and I don't think it gets taken seriously enough in these conversations. Training data in American healthcare reflects decades of undertreatment of Black patients, dismissal of women's pain complaints, and underrepresentation of elderly patients with multiple comorbidities in clinical trials. A system trained on that data will reproduce those patterns at scale, and it will do so with an air of objectivity that makes the bias harder to see and harder to challenge than when a human doctor does the same thing. At least when a human is biased you can confront them.⏎⏎I work in social services, not medicine, so I don't have a technical background here. But I've watched a lot of vulnerable people navigate healthcare systems and what they need is more human contact, not less. The populations who already get the worst care, elderly people, people with disabilities, people who don't speak English as a first language, people with serious mental illness, are exactly the populations who will be hardest for AI systems to serve well and easiest for institutions to justify deprioritizing once a machine has flagged them as complicated or low-probability-of-good-outcome.⏎⏎I really think HHS should be asking harder questions about who benefits from accelerating this adoption. Vendors benefit. Some large health systems with capital to invest benefit. It's less clear to me that patients benefit, and it's pretty clear to me that frontline healthcare workers don't benefit in any way that actually improves their working conditions rather than just adding a layer of machine oversight to their already overloaded days.⏎⏎There's also something I keep coming back to about accountability. When an AI system contributes to a wrong diagnosis or a wrongful denial of coverage, who is responsible? The hospital will say the clinician should have caught it. The clinician will say the tool gave them bad information. The vendor will say the tool was used outside its validated parameters. I've seen this dynamic play out already with prior authorization AI tools that insurance companies have been using to deny claims, and there are lawsuits pending right now, including one involving UnitedHealth Group's use of an AI model with a reported 90% error rate on post-acute care denials, that should give everyone pause before we decide the answer is more AI faster.⏎⏎The administrative burden argument is the one I find most persuasive for AI use, honestly. If AI can genuinely reduce the time clinicians spend on documentation without changing what gets documented or how it gets used, that seems worth exploring carefully. But "carefully" is doing a lot of work in that sentence, and I'm not confident that's how it will go.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0009♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Clark♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔University of Chicago Center for Health and the Social Sciences♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Advocate Lutheran General Hospital outside Chicago in March of 2023, and I watched what good nursing actually looks like up close. It is not pattern matching. Her nurse, a woman named Darlene who had been doing this for twenty-two years, caught something in my mother's breathing that the monitors weren't flagging. She couldn't have told you exactly what she noticed. She just knew. That is not a data point. That is not something a training set captures.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS is looking for ways to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my first reaction is honestly a kind of dread. Not because I think technology is bad, I use it constantly, but because "accelerate adoption" sounds like a decision that has already been made and the comment period is more of a formality. I hope that's wrong.⏎⏎The question I want to address is the one about what regulatory actions HHS can take to support AI use in clinical settings. My answer is that before you do anything to accelerate this, you should require proof that it works for the patients who are already underserved, not just proof that it works on average. Average performance in healthcare is a misleading number. A system that is 94% accurate sounds impressive until you realize what it means to be in the other 6% when you're having a stroke or your chest pain gets coded as anxiety because you're a Black woman in her forties and that's what the historical data looks like.⏎⏎There's a lot of talk about AI reducing clinician burnout, and I don't doubt that some administrative uses might genuinely help. But I am worried, and I think this is a legitimate worry, that "reducing burden on clinicians" becomes a justification for thinner staffing. If an AI tool can flag deteriorating patients, a hospital administrator might decide you need fewer nurses per floor. That is not a hypothetical. Hospitals have been doing this kind of math for decades, and they will do it again. The efficiency gains get captured by the institution, and the risk gets distributed onto patients.⏎⏎I'm also not sure who is accountable when an AI recommendation turns out to be wrong. This is the part that keeps me up at night more than anything else. If Darlene makes a call and it's the wrong call, there is a person, a license, a process. If an algorithm from a vendor whose contract is buried in a hospital's procurement system makes a recommendation that a tired resident follows without questioning, and something goes wrong, the chain of responsibility just dissolves. The vendor says the clinician should have used their judgment. The hospital says the vendor's tool was validated. The clinician says they were following the system. Nobody is responsible and the patient is just gone.⏎⏎I think HHS should require, before any acceleration happens, mandatory prospective studies in real clinical environments with diverse patient populations, not retrospective validation on cleaned datasets. And those studies should be publicly available, not proprietary. The FDA's 510(k) pathway for software as a medical device is not sufficient for tools that are making or influencing clinical decisions in real time, and HHS knows this.⏎⏎One more thing that I don't see discussed enough: the administrative AI applications. Insurance companies are already using algorithmic tools to deny prior authorizations, and there is documented evidence from the Senate Finance Committee's 2023 investigation into Medicare Advantage plans that denial rates from AI-assisted review systems were dramatically higher than from human reviewers, and that many of those denials were later overturned on appeal. That's not efficiency. That's obstruction dressed up as automation. If HHS is serious about patient welfare, the RFI should have asked specifically about that use case and whether it should be restricted rather than expanded.⏎⏎I realize this comment is focused more on caution than on answering the specific questions in the RFI the way a policy expert would. I'm not a policy expert. But I sat with my mother for eleven days and I watched what care actually requires, and I don't think the people designing these systems have spent much time in an ICU at 3am trying to figure out if someone's breathing has changed.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0010♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Adams♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Regions Hospital in St. Paul in February of 2023, and I can tell you with absolute certainty that what got her through it was not pattern recognition. It was a night-shift nurse named Delores who noticed something was off about the way my mother was breathing even though the monitors said everything was fine. Delores had been doing this for twenty-two years. She called the attending at 3am. My mother had a pulmonary embolism that the automated early warning system had not flagged. So when HHS asks how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my first reaction is something between a laugh and a cold fear, because I am not sure the people writing this RFI have spent enough time thinking about what gets lost.⏎⏎I want to focus on two things specifically. One is the question of what AI actually does to clinical judgment, and the other is who benefits economically when hospitals adopt these tools. I am not going to pretend I have no understanding of the other side. I know clinicians are burned out, I know documentation is crushing, and I know there are radiology contexts where AI has shown real accuracy gains. I am not a complete skeptic. But the way this RFI is framed worries me, because it treats adoption as the goal rather than as a means to an end, and that framing tends to produce policy that pushes speed over scrutiny.⏎⏎On clinical judgment: medicine requires something that I do not think AI can replicate, which is the ability to hold uncertainty without resolving it prematurely. A good clinician can look at a patient and say I don't know what's wrong yet, and sit with that, and keep looking. AI systems are built to produce outputs. They are trained on historical data, which means they reflect past decisions, including past mistakes and past biases. The ProPublica investigation into the COMPAS recidivism algorithm is not a healthcare story, but it is a story about exactly what happens when you trust a scoring system over human judgment and don't audit it carefully enough. Healthcare already has its own version of this problem. The pulse oximetry data is one example that's gotten more attention lately: devices that were less accurate on patients with darker skin tones, validated on populations that didn't represent everyone, deployed for decades before anyone really pushed back. AI systems trained on EHR data from institutions that have historically undertreated Black patients or undertreated women's pain are going to encode those patterns. And the output will look authoritative. That is the dangerous part.⏎⏎On the economic question, I keep thinking about what prior authorization AI means in practice. Insurance companies are already using algorithmic tools to deny claims faster. There was a ProPublica and CBS News investigation published in late 2023 that found a former Cigna medical director reviewed and denied over 60 patient claims in one day, guided partly by algorithmic recommendations. Sixty in one day. The question of whether that's a human decision or an AI decision starts to feel like a semantic argument at that point. HHS asking how to accelerate this is, in that context, asking how to give those systems more legitimacy and more speed, not more accountability.⏎⏎I understand that's not the only use case. But the RFI doesn't draw a hard enough line between tools that assist clinicians in ways clinicians can verify and override, and tools that are being used to remove clinicians from the loop or to reduce the time clinicians spend per patient. Those are categorically different things and they should not be treated as different points on a spectrum of adoption. One is augmentation and the other is replacement, and the reimbursement structures HHS designs will determine which one wins in practice.⏎⏎There's also something that I think gets underweighted in these policy conversations, which is what happens to the healthcare workforce. Radiologists in training, medical students learning to read an ECG, nurses developing clinical intuition over years of practice - all of that expertise develops through repetition and through making judgments and getting feedback. If AI handles the straightforward cases and humans only see the edge cases, the pipeline for developing that judgment collapses over time. We don't have good research yet on what that does to the profession over twenty years, and I am not sure HHS should be pushing adoption faster than that research can keep up.⏎⏎The question of accountability is where I want to end, though I realize this is getting long. When Delores called the attending at 3am, there was a chain of human accountability that I could follow. I knew who made the call, I knew who was responsible, and if something had gone wrong I would have known who to ask. With AI-assisted care, that chain gets murky in ways that are not just philosophical. Vendors say the tool is a decision support aid, not a decision maker. Clinicians say they were following the recommendation. Hospitals say they deployed it according to the vendor's instructions. This is not a hypothetical breakdown; it is already happening in malpractice litigation around AI-assisted diagnostic tools. HHS should be thinking much harder about that accountability gap before it designs incentives to make these tools more widespread.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0011♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Thompson♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Cedar Rapids in March of 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that her primary care doctor had missed twice. What saved her, ultimately, was a hospitalist who sat with her for forty minutes, noticed she was holding her left side in a way that didn't match the chart, and ordered an additional scan that hadn't been on any protocol. I think about that a lot when I read about AI being positioned as the solution to diagnostic gaps in medicine.⏎⏎Nobody is asking whether we lose something when that kind of attention gets replaced, or even supplemented, by a system that is fundamentally pattern-matching on historical records. My mother is an 74-year-old woman with atypical symptom presentation who had never been hospitalized before. There is no meaningful training set for her. The whole point is that the doctor had to throw out the pattern.⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the question this RFI poses about what regulatory actions HHS can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, because I think that framing is already pointing the wrong direction. The question assumes acceleration is the goal. I'd argue the department should be asking whether deployment should happen at all in certain contexts before it asks how to speed things up.⏎⏎The safety issues here are not hypothetical. The literature on sepsis prediction algorithms, for instance, includes the Epic Sepsis Model, which was studied independently by researchers at UCSF and published in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021. They found it missed more than two thirds of sepsis cases that clinicians caught and generated an enormous number of false alerts. Hospitals were already using it widely before that study came out. That is the pattern I am worried about: tools get deployed, they seem impressive in a vendor demonstration or a pilot, and by the time rigorous independent evaluation happens the system is already embedded in workflow and people have started deferring to it.⏎⏎And I do think people defer. That is not speculation. There's research on automation bias going back decades, and it is particularly concerning in environments where clinicians are exhausted and understaffed, which describes most American hospitals right now. If a system flags something or doesn't flag something, that changes behavior even when clinicians know they are supposed to override it. The liability and accountability structure in most institutions makes clinicians reluctant to document that they acted against the recommendation of a deployed tool. So in practice, the tool's judgment starts to dominate even when the nominal policy says the human is in charge.⏎⏎I'm genuinely worried that the people most harmed by this will be patients who already have the hardest time advocating for themselves. Elderly patients. Patients with limited English. Patients with mental illness. Patients in rural hospitals where staffing is thin and there is pressure to rely on automated systems precisely because there aren't enough people. These are also the patients whose data is probably least well represented in whatever training sets these algorithms were built on.⏎⏎I understand there's an argument that AI could help with administrative burden and that maybe that's lower risk than clinical decision support. But honestly I'm skeptical of that too. Insurance prior authorization is an administrative function and we already know that automated denial systems are being used to override physician judgment at scale. Cigna was reported in 2023 to have had medical reviewers spending an average of 1.2 seconds per denial decision. That is not a human reviewing a claim. That is a human providing cover for an automated recommendation. Calling it "AI-assisted" doesn't change what it is.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require that any AI tool used in a clinical or coverage decision context be subject to independent prospective validation before deployment, not just post-market surveillance that vendors can largely manage themselves. The FDA's current framework for software as a medical device doesn't really capture the ongoing drift that happens when a model gets used in populations and settings that weren't part of the original validation. And I don't see anything in this RFI that suggests HHS is seriously grappling with that problem rather than looking for ways to get out of the way of industry.⏎⏎I'm not opposed to technology in medicine in some blanket way. I just think the burden of proof should be on the people deploying these systems to show they work for real patients in real settings, not on patients and clinicians to prove after the fact that something went wrong. The way this RFI is written, the working assumption seems to be that adoption is being slowed by unnecessary friction and that HHS's job is to remove it. I think the friction is sometimes doing important work and we should be careful about what we're removing.⏎⏎There's also no serious discussion in the framing of this RFI about♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0012♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Lopez♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Riverside University Health System in Moreno Valley in February 2023. I watched the nurses and doctors work with her. What I noticed, more than anything, was how much of what they did was not about data. It was about her. The way she got scared when more than two people came into the room at once. The way she couldn't explain where it hurt but her face changed. The way one particular resident, a young woman who couldn't have been more than 28, figured out that my mother was refusing to eat because she was embarrassed, not because she wasn't hungry. No algorithm would have caught that. I don't know how you'd even begin to train one to catch it.⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the question about what barriers HHS should remove to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, because my answer is that I think HHS is asking the wrong question. The question shouldn't be what's slowing AI down. It should be whether we actually want AI making or influencing clinical decisions at a faster pace than we can understand what it's doing.⏎⏎What concerns me is the framing of this whole RFI. It assumes the answer is yes, we want more AI, and now we're just working out details. But the case for going slower is serious and I don't think it's being taken seriously enough. Medicine is full of situations where a system that works well on average fails badly for a specific person. A Black patient with kidney disease, an elderly woman with atypical cardiac symptoms, someone who doesn't speak English and can't describe their pain accurately. These aren't edge cases, there are tens of millions of people in those situations. AI tools trained on historical data will inherit whatever biases were already in that data, and the problem is that an AI system looks authoritative even when it's wrong. A doctor who is wrong can be questioned. A score or a flag from an AI system tends to become the floor of the conversation rather than one input among many.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the insurance side of this, because the RFI mentions administrative functions. Insurers are already using automated systems to deny claims faster than any human reviewer could have processed them. UnitedHealth's use of an AI model called nH Predict to deny post-acute care for Medicare Advantage patients was the subject of a federal lawsuit in 2023, and reporting suggested its denial rate was far higher than what individual medical reviewers would have determined. That is a real, documented example of AI being used not to improve care but to cut costs at patients' expense. HHS accelerating AI adoption in administrative functions without serious guardrails would very likely produce more of that, not less.⏎⏎I understand there are genuine uses for AI that might help clinicians. I'm not saying the technology is worthless. But the way this RFI is structured, asking for ways to remove barriers and accelerate adoption, puts HHS in the position of a promoter rather than a regulator. The question of who is responsible when an AI-influenced decision harms a patient is genuinely unresolved. Vendors disclaim liability. Hospitals say they're using tools that were cleared by FDA. Clinicians say they were following what the system recommended. That chain of non-responsibility is already a problem in a small number of cases. Scaling up AI use before that accountability question is answered seems like a serious mistake.⏎⏎What I would ask HHS to do is pause the acceleration framing entirely and instead ask: what would it take to make any AI tool used in a clinical or insurance decision provably safe for the specific populations it will be used on, with a named human who is legally accountable for its outputs?⏎⏎That is a harder question and it would slow things down considerably. That's probably the point.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0013♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Lewis♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's Hospital in Kansas City in February of 2023, and what got her through it was not a pattern-matching algorithm. It was a night-shift nurse named Delia who noticed something was off in how my mother was holding her left arm and flagged it to the attending before the chart said anything was wrong. That observation came from thirty years of sitting with sick people. I don't know how you train a model on that.⏎⏎I'm responding specifically to the question about what barriers HHS should address to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, because I think the framing of that question is already wrong. The assumption seems to be that the barriers are the problem and adoption is the goal. What if the barriers exist for good reasons? What if some of them are just called "medicine"?⏎⏎The thing that worries me most, honestly, is not any single AI tool. It's the cumulative pressure. Hospitals are already understaffed and under financial pressure. When a vendor comes in and says their system can reduce documentation time by 40% or flag deteriorating patients before nurses notice, administrators hear "we can cut FTEs." I've talked to nurses in Missouri and Illinois who are already being told that AI-assisted monitoring means their floor can run with fewer people overnight. That is not a hypothetical future concern. That's happening now. The AI doesn't replace a nurse on paper, it just creates the conditions where the hospital feels justified in not replacing the one who left.⏎⏎There's also a bias problem that I don't think gets taken seriously enough. The datasets these systems train on reflect decades of unequal care. Black patients have historically been undertreated for pain, undertested for certain conditions, seen less frequently. A system trained on that history will reproduce it, and it will do so with a confidence score attached, which makes it harder to argue with than a human clinician whose reasoning you can at least question out loud. I'm not a researcher, I don't know all the technical details, but I've read enough to know that ProPublica documented this in the criminal justice context with the COMPAS tool back in 2016, and there's no obvious reason healthcare AI is immune to the same dynamics.⏎⏎What I'd ask HHS to consider is this: before accelerating anything, require that any AI system used in a clinical or insurance decision context be subject to independent auditing for disparate impact by race, income, disability status, and age. Not vendor self-certification. Independent auditing. And require that the results be public. If the system can't pass that, it shouldn't be deployed regardless of how well it performed in a controlled trial at an academic medical center.⏎⏎The insurance piece especially concerns me. Prior authorization is already a nightmare. My father-in-law's pulmonologist in Topeka spent something like four hours last year fighting an automated denial for a CT scan that was pretty clearly indicated. The insurer's system flagged it as not meeting criteria. Nobody could explain exactly which criteria or why. If AI gets embedded deeper into that process, and I have every reason to think it will because it saves insurers money, the accountability problem gets worse not better. At least with a human reviewer you can theoretically appeal to someone. With an algorithm making the first-pass decision, and then a human rubber-stamping it because the queue is too long to actually review each case, you've just added a layer of false legitimacy to a denial process that was already pretty opaque.⏎⏎I understand there are genuine use cases where AI may help, I'm not saying every application is bad. But HHS is asking how to accelerate adoption, and I think the more responsible question is how to ensure that whatever gets adopted actually helps patients and doesn't just help the financial interests of the institutions deploying it. Those are not the same thing, and right now there's very little in the regulatory structure that distinguishes between them.⏎⏎The 510(k) clearance pathway for software as a medical device has been criticized pretty extensively for not requiring clinical evidence of benefit, just substantial equivalence to something already on the market. That seems like a gap that matters a lot if we're talking about AI tools that influence diagnosis or treatment decisions for millions of people.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0014♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Allen♔♔New York♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Regions Hospital in St. Paul in February of 2023, and during that time the thing that mattered most was not the monitoring equipment or the lab results. It was the night nurse who noticed she seemed more confused than her numbers suggested and pushed for a second opinion on her medication dosing. A computer looking at her chart would have seen stable vitals. A human being saw something else. That distinction is the whole argument, as far as I'm concerned.⏎⏎What worries me about this RFI is that HHS seems to be asking how to go faster, not whether to go at all. The questions are framed around acceleration, adoption, removing barriers. There is almost no language about what happens when these tools are wrong, or who is responsible, or what patients lose when clinical judgment gets quietly replaced by a recommendation engine that a busy hospitalist learns to rubber-stamp because they have fourteen other patients.⏎⏎I want to say something about the bias question because I think it gets underplayed in these kinds of policy conversations. The data that trains these systems comes from decades of medical practice that was itself biased. There is documented evidence that pain in Black patients has historically been undercoded, undertreated, underbelieved. If you train a sepsis prediction model or a pain management algorithm on that history, you are not correcting for bias, you are compressing it into software and then calling it objective. The model will look authoritative. It will generate numbers and confidence intervals. Nobody in the room will feel comfortable saying the number is wrong. That is actually more dangerous than the original human error, because at least a human clinician can be questioned, can change their mind, can be shamed or sued or retrained.⏎⏎The employment piece also deserves more attention than I've seen in most of these discussions. I keep reading that AI will reduce clinician burnout by handling documentation. Maybe. But I work in healthcare administration and I can tell you the conversations I've heard in the last two years are not mostly about reducing burnout. They are about FTE reduction. They are about whether you still need a human coder or a human prior authorization specialist once you have a tool that does it faster. And prior authorization is already a nightmare for patients. Automating it does not make it more humane. It makes the denial faster and the appeal process more opaque, because now you are arguing with a system that cannot tell you why it decided what it decided except in terms that no patient can interpret.⏎⏎I am genuinely uncertain whether there is any version of AI in clinical care that I would feel comfortable with, and I realize that probably sounds extreme. I am not opposed to technology. I use the same EHR systems everyone else does, I understand the value of decision support for drug interactions, things like that. But there is a meaningful difference between a system that flags a potential adverse drug reaction and a system that generates treatment recommendations or triages patients or decides who gets approved for a procedure. The first one is a tool. The second one is practicing medicine, and I don't think we've had an honest public conversation about whether we want software doing that.⏎⏎The accountability gap is the thing I keep coming back to. When my mother's nurse intervened, she was accountable. Her license was on the line. Her professional judgment was on the line. She had skin in the game in the most literal sense. When an algorithm makes a recommendation and the clinician follows it and the patient is harmed, the accountability just sort of dissolves. The vendor says the clinician should have used their judgment. The clinician says they were following standard of care based on available tools. The hospital says they implemented the system according to guidance. Nobody is actually responsible in the way that matters, which is the way that would have prevented the harm in the first place.⏎⏎I think HHS should be asking much harder questions about liability before any of this goes further, and I would also want to see independent validation requirements that are not funded or designed by the vendors themselves. The current framework for software as a medical device is already struggling to keep up with what's being deployed, and adding acceleration on top of that♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0015♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Brown♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔Harvard Medical School Department of Biomedical Informatics♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that got missed on her first ER visit. She's fine now, but what I keep thinking about is what actually saved her: a night nurse who noticed something was off about the way she was holding her arm, who asked a question that wasn't on any checklist, and who pushed back on the attending until someone ordered a second blood panel. That nurse had been doing her job for 22 years. She knew my mother was scared and wasn't describing her symptoms accurately because she didn't want to be a bother. No algorithm would have caught that. I don't know how you train a model on "patient is downplaying pain because she doesn't want to inconvenience anyone."⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the questions HHS is asking about what actions the department can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, because I think the framing of this RFI is itself the problem. The question isn't how do we accelerate this, it's whether we should be accelerating it at all and who bears the risk when we get it wrong.⏎⏎The safety question gets glossed over too quickly in most of these conversations. AI systems trained on historical patient data will inherit whatever biases were baked into how care was actually delivered, which means they will systematically underperform for Black patients, for low-income patients, for people with disabilities, for people whose conditions don't fit the typical presentation because their demographics were underrepresented in whatever training set the vendor used. There's documented evidence of this already. A widely cited 2019 study in Science found that a commerically used healthcare algorithm was less likely to refer Black patients for extra care because it used cost as a proxy for need, and Black patients historically receive less expensive care even when they're sicker. That wasn't a fringe product. It was being used on tens of millions of patients. Now imagine that kind of bias embedded in AI tools that are making triage recommendations or flagging patients for follow-up, and imagine the liability is diffuse enough that nobody is clearly responsible when outcomes diverge along racial lines.⏎⏎And that's the piece that I think really needs to be addressed before HHS does anything to accelerate deployment: accountability. Right now, when an AI system makes a bad recommendation and a clinician follows it and a patient is harmed, the legal and institutional responsibility is genuinely unclear. The vendor says the clinician should have exercised judgment. The hospital says they followed the vendor's guidance. The clinician says the system presented the recommendation with high confidence and there was no practical way to override it in the workflow. The patient or their family is left with no clear path to recourse. That's not a hypothetical scenario, it's already happening with prior authorization AI tools that insurance companies are using to deny claims automatically. There have been lawsuits. There was a congressional inquiry in 2023 into UnitedHealth Group's use of an AI model called nH Predict to deny post-acute care claims at a rate that multiple courts found violated Medicare Advantage rules. That's the real world version of AI in healthcare, not the demo version.⏎⏎What I'd actually want HHS to do is slow down and require that any AI tool used in a clinical or coverage decision context be subject to prospective clinical validation in the specific population where it will be used, not just the population it was trained on. I'd want mandatory disclosure to patients when AI played a role in a coverage decision or a clinical recomendation (I know there are existing transparency provisions but they're not being enforced in any meaningful way). And I'd want a moratorium on AI-driven prior authorization until there's a clear legal framework establishing that insurers using these tools are fully liable for outcomes, not partially liable, not "shared" liability with some software company in a jurisdiction with favorable terms.⏎⏎The efficiency argument for AI keeps getting made as though efficiency is a neutral good in healthcare. It's not. Efficiency in a for-profit healthcare system often just means finding faster ways to deny care or reduce staff. The hospitals and insurers who are most eager to adopt AI are frequently the same ones with the worst records on patient outcomes and worker treatment.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0016♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Wilson♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔Epic Research♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in March of 2023, and what I kept watching was not her monitors but the nurses. The way a nurse would walk in, take one look at her, and know something was off before any machine said so. That kind of perception is not a dataset. I don't know what you call it exactly, but I know it saved her life at least once when her pressure readings were technically stable and everything was "within normal range" and a nurse named Delia just didn't like the way she was breathing and called the resident anyway. No algorithm flagged that. A person did.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS is looking at ways to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, my honest reaction is dread. Not because I'm against technology in general, I use it constantly, but because I think there's a real confusion happening between what medicine is and what it looks like from the outside. From the outside it looks like information processing. Lots of data, lots of decisions, lots of patterns. Feed it into a model, get better outputs. But from the inside, from the patient side of the bed, medicine is something else entirely. It's someone being accountable to you. Personally accountable, in a way that matters.⏎⏎What I want to focus on here is the question of who bears responsibility when something goes wrong, because I don't think HHS has grappled seriously with that. When a physician makes a mistake, there is a chain of accountability. Licensing boards, malpractice law, hospital credentialing, peer review. It's imperfect but it exists. When an AI system makes a mistake, that chain becomes a maze. The hospital says it followed the vendor's guidelines. The vendor says the model performed within its stated parameters. The clinician says she was relying on the tool's recommendation. The insurer says coverage decisions were made by the system, not a human. And the patient is left with an injury and no one who is clearly, unambiguously responsible. I have read about this happening already with AI-driven prior authorization denials. UnitedHealth's nH Predict tool was the subject of a class action lawsuit in 2023 precisely because it was allegedly denying post-acute care claims at a rate that didn't match clinical reality, and patients and families couldn't get a straight answer about who had actually made the decision. That's not a hypothetical. That already happened.⏎⏎I'm also skeptical of the argument that AI will reduce clinician burnout. Maybe it will for some tasks. But I think there's an equal or greater chance that it becomes a justification for cutting staff, increasing caseloads, and then pointing to AI tools as evidence that the remaining clinicians can handle more. We've seen this in other industries. Technology gets adopted, productivity expectations go up, and workers end up doing more with less while the efficiency gains flow somewhere else. There's no reason to think healthcare is immune to that dynamic, and there are specific reasons to think it might be worse, because the costs of getting it wrong are borne by patients who are often not in a position to complain effectively.⏎⏎The populations I worry about most are elderly patients with complex comorbidities, people with limited english proficiency, people with psychiatric conditions that affect how they present symptoms, and people who are simply frightened and don't give a clean history. These are exactly the patients for whom standardized pattern-matching is least reliable. They're also, not coincidentally, the patients who are most likely to be in settings where cost pressures are highest and where AI adoption might be most aggressively pushed.⏎⏎I'm not asking HHS to ban research or stop all development. But the question this RFI is asking, what can we do to accelerate adoption, assumes that acceleration is the right goal. I don't think it is. I think the right goal is careful, limited, thoroughly validated deployment in very specific use cases with robust human override requirements and genuine liability clarity before any of this scales. The word "accelerate" in the title of this request worries me more than anything else in it.⏎⏎Also I want to flag that the comment period for this RFI closes on what seems like a fairly short timeline given the scope of what's being asked, and a lot of the people most affected by these decisions, patients, bedside nurses, home health aides, don't have lobbyists or policy teams monitoring federal registers.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0017♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Mitchell♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in 2019 after a sepsis diagnosis that was initially missed for almost 36 hours. What eventually caught it was a nurse who noticed something was off about how she was breathing and pushed back on the attending. Not a score, not an algorithm. A person who had been doing that job for twenty-two years and had a feeling. I keep thinking about that when I read materials like this one, because everything in this RFI seems to treat the "problem" of clinical care as fundamentally a data problem, and I don't think it is.⏎⏎I want to address the question about what regulatory actions HHS should take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, because I think the framing of the question itself is the problem. The question assumes acceleration is the goal. I'd argue the opposite: HHS should be looking at where to put the brakes on, or at minimum build in much longer evaluation periods before any of this gets normalized.⏎⏎Here is what concerns me most practically. AI tools in healthcare are being validated in specific hospital systems, often large academic medical centers with relatively clean and complete EHR data, and then sold to community hospitals and rural clinics where the patient population is different, the data is messier, and the staff don't have the same resources to catch errors when the tool gets something wrong. The FDA cleared a sepsis prediction algorithm a few years back that performed well in the original study, and then a 2021 analysis in Nature Medicine found it missed more than two thirds of sepsis cases in real-world deployment and generated a huge number of false positives. That's not a minor discrepancy. That's a tool that could genuinely make care worse while giving everyone the impression it's making care better. I don't know how HHS accelerates adoption in that environment without significantly increasing patient risk.⏎⏎There's also the insurance piece that I think gets underplayed. When the RFI talks about administrative functions, it's including prior authorization and coverage decisions. Insurers are already using algorithmic tools to generate denial recommendations at speeds no human reviewer could match. UnitedHealth was sued in 2023 specifically over an AI system that was allegedly overriding physician judgment to deny post-acute care for elderly patients. That case is still working through the courts as far as I know. Calling that an administrative efficiency is, I think, a pretty sanitized way to describe what's actually happening to real patients. My concern is that once HHS signals it wants to accelerate AI adoption broadly, that signal gets used by payers to justify exactly this kind of automation, and there's no clear line between clinical AI and administrative AI in the way the rule seems to imply.⏎⏎I also think the workforce question is being handled too gently in this RFI. Hospitals are already using AI adoption as cover for staffing decisions. If a documentation tool can generate a clinical note in thirty seconds, the argument becomes that you need fewer people doing documentation, which sometimes means fewer people on the floor noticing things. The nurse who caught my mother's sepsis wasn't doing something that shows up in an efficiency metric.⏎⏎What I'd actually want to see from HHS is a mandatory post-market surveillance requirement for any AI tool used in clinical decision support, with public reporting of real-world performance data broken down by patient demographics. Not the validation data from the original study. What it actually does in practice, in different kinds of hospitals, over time. That doesn't exist right now in any consistent way, and I don't see how you can responsibly talk about acceleration when you don't have that infrastructure in place.⏎⏎I also think there should be explicit rules about when AI-generated outputs can and cannot be the basis for insurance coverage decisions, and what level of human review is actually required, not just nominally present.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0018♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔White♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔Illumina♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in 2021 and I watched what good nursing care looks like up close. The nurses knew when she was in pain before she could say it. They noticed she wasn't eating right, they caught a medication interaction that the overnight resident missed, and one of them sat with her for twenty minutes at 2am just because she was scared. None of that was in a chart. None of that was a data point. I have been thinking about that experience constantly since I heard HHS was soliciting comments on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, and I have to say my reaction is pretty close to alarm.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to AI adoption and what HHS can do to remove them. I want to suggest that some of those barriers exist for good reasons and the department should think hard before treating them as obstacles to be cleared away.⏎⏎Here is what concerns me most. When you push AI into clinical decision-making, you don't just add a tool. You change who is responsible. The clinician who used to make a call based on their training and their read of the patient now becomes someone who either agrees with the algorithm or has to justify overriding it. That's a real shift in accountability and I don't think it goes in a good direction. If the AI recommends a course of treatment and the doctor follows it and the patient is harmed, who is answerable? The vendor will say the doctor should have used clinical judgment. The hospital will say the vendor's product was validated. The doctor will say the system flagged it as low risk. That's not a hypothetical, there have already been cases where algorithmic tools used in sepsis prediction and in prior authorization decisions produced bad outcomes and the accountability question was genuinely murky.⏎⏎And the prior authorization piece is actually where I want to spend most of my comment because I think it gets less attention than the clinical side. Insurers are already using AI to automate denials. Cigna made headlines in 2023 when it came out that their system was rejecting claims at a rate that made it basically impossible for any human reviewer to have actually looked at the cases, something like 1.7 seconds per claim on average. That is not AI helping anyone. That is AI being used to put distance between a corporation and a decision that harms a patient, while making the decision look like it came from a neutral technical process rather than a cost-cutting incentive. HHS accelerating AI adoption without specifically and forcefully addressing this use case would be, in my view, irresponsible.⏎⏎I know there are people who will say AI can reduce burnout, flag drug interactions, help with documentation. I'm not completely dismissing that. But the documentation argument in particular feels like a bait and switch to me. Clinicians are drowning in paperwork because of billing requirements and liability concerns and insurance prior auth demands, and AI that helps document more efficiently doesn't fix any of those underlying problems, it just makes it slightly more bearable to work inside a broken system. That's not nothing, but it's not the same as improving care.⏎⏎The populations I worry about most are elderly patients, patients with psychiatric conditions, patients who don't speak English well, and patients in rural areas who may be seeing providers who have less training and fewer resources to push back when an AI recommendation seems off. These are also, not coincidentally, the populations whose data is most likely to be underrepresented in whatever training sets these tools were built on. A system trained mostly on data from large academic medical centers in the northeast is going to perform differently in a critical access hospital in rural Mississippi and there is not nearly enough scrutiny of that gap in how these tools get validated before deployment.⏎⏎I don't think HHS should ban AI outright. I'm not sure that's even a coherent ask. But I do think the framing of this RFI, which is oriented almost entirely around acceleration and adoption, gets the emphasis wrong. The question shouldn't be how do we remove barriers to AI in healthcare. It should be how do we make sure that when AI is used, someone is genuinely accountable for the outcome, the tool has been validated in the actual setting where it's being deployed, and there is a meaningful way for patients and clinicians to understand and contest what the system is recommending.⏎⏎On that last point, contestability, I don't see nearly enough attention in current discussions. If a patient is denied coverage or a treatment is recommended based in whole or part on an algorithmic output, they should have a right to a plain-language explanation of how that output was generated and a real human review process that isn't just a rubber stamp. Right now that's often not the case and the systems are opaque enough that even the clinicians using them don't fully understand what's driving the recommendations.⏎⏎I'm also genuinely worried about what happens to the healthcare workforce. Not just job losses, though that's real, but the devaluation of expertise that happens when institutions start treating clinical judgment as something that needs to be checked against a machine. There's a nurse I know who has been doing wound care for 30 years. She can look at a wound and tell you things that no photograph captures. If her hospital starts requiring algorithmic sign-off on wound care protocols, what message does that send her? And if the algorithm is wrong, does she have the institutional support to say so, or does she face pressure to defer?⏎⏎I realize I've been somewhat all over the place here. The short version of my comment is: please do not treat acceleration as the goal. The goal should be patient safety, clinician accountability, and equitable outcomes, and AI may or may not serve those goals depending on how carefully it's implemented, where, by whom, and with what oversight structures in place. The RFI as written makes me nervous because it seems to assume the answer is yes and is just asking how to get there faster.⏎⏎One more thing that I haven't seen addressed anywhere in the background materials: what happens to data that gets fed into these systems? If a hospital uses an AI documentation tool and that tool's vendor is also selling analytics services to insurers, there is a potential conflict that patients are not being told about and that I don't think current consent frameworks are equipped to handle.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0019♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Baker♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔MIT Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence Laboratory (CSAIL)♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February of 2023, and I watched what good nursing looks like up close. I watched a nurse named Darlene notice that my mother's breathing had changed in a way that wasn't showing up on any monitor yet. She didn't run an algorithm. She just knew, from years of sitting with sick people, that something was shifting. That kind of knowledge doesn't get captured in a training dataset.⏎⏎I'm responding specifically to the questions HHS is asking about what regulatory actions might accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, and my answer is: please don't. Or at least slow down considerably and think harder about what you're actually building.⏎⏎The framing of this RFI bothers me. It asks how to accelerate adoption, not whether to accelerate it. That's a significant assumption buried in the question itself. There are real reasons to be skeptical that faster is better here.⏎⏎What worries me most, and I keep coming back to this, is the accountability problem. Right now if a doctor makes a wrong call, there's a person who made that call. They can be questioned, disciplined, sued, or simply asked to explain themselves to a grieving family. When an AI system contributes to a bad outcome, who answers for it? The hospital says they were following the vendor's guidance. The vendor says the hospital deployed it outside its validated parameters. The clinician says the screen said one thing and they trusted it. I've seen how hard it already is to get straight answers after a medical error. Adding a machine into that chain of custody for decisions seems like it makes accountability significantly worse, not better.⏎⏎There's also something that doesn't get said enough in these conversations, which is that AI in healthcare isn't being proposed primarily to help patients. It's being proposed because it might reduce costs and increase throughput. I understand why hospitals and insurers care about that. But "reduce costs" in healthcare often means fewer nurses per patient, fewer minutes per visit, fewer second opinions. If AI tools let administrators justify cutting staff because the machine is handling more of the cognitive load, the people who pay for that are patients, specifically the ones who most need human attention and are least able to advocate for themselves.⏎⏎I've read some of the research on AI diagnostic tools and the accuracy numbers can look impressive until you dig into which populations were in the training data and which weren't. There was a widely cited study, I believe published in Science around 2019, that found a commercial algorithm used by millions of patients was significantly less likely to refer Black patients for additional care compared to equally sick white patients, specifically because it used prior healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs. That's not a corner case. That's a fundamental problem with what happens when you optimize a model on historical data in a system that already has inequities baked in. I don't have confidence that we've solved that problem. I don't think the vendors have solved it either, they've just gotten better at talking about it in their marketing materials.⏎⏎I want to be honest that I don't think every use of AI in healthcare is wrong. Scheduling software and billing code assistance probably aren't going to hurt anyone. But the RFI seems to be pointing toward much more consequential applications, diagnostic support, treatment recommendations, insurance prior authorization. Those are places where errors have real consequences for real people.⏎⏎The prior authorization piece in particular. Insurers are already using automated systems to deny claims at scale, and patients and doctors have almost no visibility into how those decisions get made. Giving that process more AI infrastructure before there are serious transparency and appeals requirements in place seems like exactly the wrong order of operations. My neighbor spent four months fighting a denial for a medication her rheumatologist had prescribed, and she never once got a straight answer about what criteria the insurer was applying or who had actually reviewed her case.⏎⏎I think HHS should be asking different questions. Not how do we accelerate adoption, but what harms are already occurring from existing deployments, what recourse do patients have when AI contributes to a bad outcome, and whether the current legal and regulatory framework is even capable of assigning responsibility in these situations. I suspect the answer to that last one is no, and that seems like something that should be sorted out before the technology is further embedded into clinical workflows.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0020♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Moore♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔Natera♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Regions Hospital in St. Paul in February of 2023, and I want to be honest that this experience shapes everything I'm about to say. The care she received was extraordinary not because of any technology but because a pulmonologist named Dr. Reyes sat with our family for forty minutes on a Tuesday night and talked us through what was happening in language we could actually understand. He was tired. He probably had six other patients. But he stayed, and he answered every question, and he looked my mother in the eye. I keep thinking about what that interaction would have looked like if half his attention was going to reviewing AI-generated flags and alerts and risk scores on a dashboard somewhere.⏎⏎That's the thing HHS doesn't seem to be asking in this RFI. The questions are all about how to accelerate adoption, how to reduce barriers, how to align reimbursement with AI use. There's no question about what we lose. There's no question about whether some of what AI is replacing was actually doing important work that we can't quantify.⏎⏎Medicine is not just an information matching problem. I know that sounds obvious but I think the whole framing of this RFI treats it like one. Patients are scared and confused and sometimes they can't describe their symptoms clearly, and they present differently depending on who's in the room and whether they trust that person. A clinician reading body language, picking up on something a patient said offhandedly, noticing that the family member in the corner looks exhausted and frightened - that is not inefficiency waiting to be optimized. That is the actual work. And there's good reason to think that layering AI recommendations into that process pushes it toward something more transactional, where the clinician starts unconsciously anchoring to the machine's output rather than doing their own independent assessment.⏎⏎There's also the bias question, which I don't think gets taken seriously enough. AI systems trained on historical health data are trained on decades of undertreatment of Black patients, dismissal of women's pain reports, inadequate care for people on Medicaid versus private insurance. The 2019 Obermeyer study in Science - which I'm sure HHS is familiar with - showed that a widely used commercial algorithm was systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients historically received less care even when equally sick. That's not a bug that got fixed and went away. That's a structural feature of what happens when you train systems on real-world data from a healthcare system that has never treated everyone equally.⏎⏎And I worry a lot about the insurance side of this, maybe even more than the clinical side. The RFI mentions administrative functions almost as an afterthought, but prior authorization denials powered by AI are already affecting real patients right now. UnitedHealth's use of an algorithm called nH Predict to deny post-acute care claims was the subject of a class action lawsuit filed in 2023, with plaintiffs alleging the algorithm had a 90 percent denial rate and was overriding physician judgments. That's not a hypothetical future harm. That's happening while HHS is asking how to accelerate adoption.⏎⏎What I'd want HHS to actually do is slow down and require that any AI system used in a clinical or coverage decision context be subject to mandatory prospective bias audits before deployment - not retrospective reviews after harm has already occurred. And I'd want there to be a clear legal rule that when an AI system contributes to a denial or a clinical recommendation that causes harm, the vendor and the institution share liability. Right now the liability structure is so diffuse that nobody is really accountable. The clinician says they were following the system, the hospital says the vendor validated it, the vendor says it was used outside the approved indication.⏎⏎I'm also genuinely worried about what this does to the workforce over the next decade. Not just job losses, though that's real, but the slower erosion of clinical judgment and expertise that happens when residents and nurses and even experienced physicians start relying on algorithmic recommendations as a crutch. Skills atrophy. If a radiologist spends ten years reviewing AI-flagged images rather than doing independent reads first, what happens to their ability to catch the thing the AI missed?⏎⏎The RFI asks about what regulatory actions HHS could take to accelerate AI adoption. My answer is that HHS should instead be building a much more robust framework for when AI use in healthcare is actually appropriate and safe before any acceleration happens. The pace right now is being set by vendors with financial incentives, not by evidence of patient benefit. And the reimbursement proposals being floated would essentially pay providers to adopt tools that have not been rigorously validated in the specific populations and settings where they'd be used.⏎⏎I recognize there are probably some narrow applications where AI genuinely helps - certain imaging analyses, maybe some administrative scheduling functions that don't involve clinical judgment. But the RFI isn't asking about narrow applications. It's asking how to make AI adoption broadly faster, and that framing itself is the problem.⏎⏎One more thing that keeps nagging at me: once these systems are embedded in the EHR workflow, they are almost impossible to remove. Epic and Cerner and the major vendors build these tools into the core interface. A hospital that signs a five-year contract with an AI vendor is not going to be in a position to pull that tool after two years even if evidence of harm starts accumulating, because the whole clinical workflow has been reorganized around it. The switching costs become a reason to keep using something even when you have doubts about it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0021♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Hill♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's Medical Center in Milwaukee in February of 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that her primary care doctor had nearly missed twice. What saved her was not an algorithm. It was a second-year resident who walked into the room, looked at her, looked at my father's face, and said something is wrong here that the numbers aren't telling me. That resident ordered a blood culture panel that wasn't in any protocol. I keep thinking about what happens to that instinct when we hand everything over to a system that was trained on historical data and optimizes for the average patient.⏎⏎I want to focus my comments on the question of how HHS is thinking about clinical safety review before these tools are deployed, and also on the workforce question, because I don't think the RFI takes the employment and deskilling concern seriously enough.⏎⏎On safety: the framing of this RFI is almost entirely about acceleration. The word "accelerating" is right there in the title. But healthcare AI tools are already being deployed faster than the evidence supports. A 2023 review in JAMA found that the overwhelming majority of AI tools approved or cleared by the FDA had been validated on data from a single institution. Single institution. That means a tool trained mostly on patients from, say, a large academic hospital in the northeast will get deployed in a rural federally qualified health center in Mississippi and nobody will have any systematic way to know whether its performance holds up. Hospitals are buying these products because vendors are good at sales and because administrators see labor cost savings, not because there's clear evidence the tool works in their specific patient population.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem and I don't want to just name it and move on because I think people have started treating it like a checkbox issue. The bias in these systems isn't always obvious. It doesn't show up as the tool refusing to treat Black patients. It shows up as the tool consistently undertriaging them, or recommending watchful waiting where it would recommend intervention for someone else, because the training data reflected decades of undertreament and that undertreament looks, to the algorithm, like a different baseline. The tool is just learning what happened. It doesn't know that what happened was wrong.⏎⏎The workforce piece is something I feel strongly about and I don't think I'm being a luddite here. The argument that AI will reduce clinician burnout is plausible in narrow cases but it's also exactly what every labor-displacing technology promises before it displaces labor. Radiology is already seeing consolidation driven partly by AI-assisted reads being used to justify fewer radiologists covering more images faster. That's not burnout reduction. That's speedup. And when something gets missed, who is accountable? The radiologist who reviewed 400 studies that day because the AI was supposed to flag anything serious? The vendor who sold a tool with a 94% sensitivity rate in the trial population but never disclosed what the false negative rate looks like for patients over 80 with comorbidities?⏎⏎I think HHS should require, before any AI clinical decision support tool can be marketed to Medicare and Medicaid participating institutions, a prospective multi-site validation study that includes the specific demographic and geographic populations the tool will actually be used with. Not a retrospective chart review. An actual prospective study. And the results should be publicly posted, not buried in a 510(k) submission that takes a FOIA request to access.⏎⏎The administrative AI question bothers me too, maybe even more in some ways, because I think people assume that because it's "just" prior authorization or coding or scheduling, the stakes are lower. They aren't. Prior authorization denials cost people their medications, their surgeries, their lives sometimes. Automating denials with AI doesn't make them more accurate. It makes them faster and harder to contest, because now you're arguing with a black box and the insurance company can say the tool made the recommendation, not the human reviewer. That's a feature for the insurer. It is absolutely not a feature for the patient sitting at home trying to figure out why their insulin pump request was denied for the third time.⏎⏎I recognize there are genuine potential benefits here and I'm not saying never. I'm saying the burden of proof should be on the people deploying these systems, not on the patients and workers who have to live with the consequences. Right now that burden is basically inverted.⏎⏎One more thing I didn't see addressed clearly in the RFI: what happens to existing contracts when a tool is later found to have systematic errors? Are institutions required to disclose adverse events attributable to AI recommendations the same way they report other adverse events? Because right now I don't believe there's any consistent requiremnet for that and that seems like a pretty significant gap before we start talking about acceleration.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0022♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Jackson♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔American College of Emergency Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the hospital for six weeks in 2022 with a complicated post-surgical infection, and I watched what it looked like when a care team actually functioned as a care team. Her wound care nurse, a woman who had been doing that job for over twenty years, caught something one afternoon that the attending had missed because she knew my mother, had been watching her for days, and noticed that her affect was slightly off in a way that didn't show up in any chart. That observation led to a sepsis workup that probably saved her life. I keep thinking about what happens to that kind of judgment when you start routing clinical decisions through a system that has never met my mother, cannot read her face, and was trained on data from institutions that may not look anything like the hospital in Fresno where she was being treated.⏎⏎The question HHS is asking about accelerating AI adoption is framed as though the main barriers are bureaucratic and the solution is to remove friction. But I think the friction is sometimes doing real work. When a physician has to stop and think through a diagnosis rather than confirm a machine recommendation, that pause is not inefficiency. It is medicine.⏎⏎What concerns me most, and I'll be honest that I'm not sure exactly how to articulate this, is that the push toward AI in clinical settings feels like it is being driven more by what AI can do technically than by any clear evidence that it improves outcomes for real patients in real settings. The studies I've seen cited tend to come from controlled environments or from the vendors themselves, and the populations studied often don't reflect the diversity of patients who show up at safety-net hospitals. There was a widely discussed study, I believe it was published in Science around 2019, that found a commercial algorithm used by millions of patients was systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients had historically received less care even when equally sick. That kind of bias doesn't announce itself. The system just keeps outputting numbers that look authoritative.⏎⏎I want to address specifically the question about what regulatory actions HHS could take to accelerate adoption, because I think the framing of that question is itself worth pushing back on. Acceleration assumes we know where we are going and that the main problem is speed. I don't think we know enough yet to be accelerating. We should be requiring much more rigorous post-market surveillance of AI tools already deployed in clinical settings. We should be asking who is liable when an AI-assisted diagnosis is wrong and the patient is harmed, because right now the answer to that question is genuinely unclear and that unclarity protects vendors more than patients. Hospitals can say the vendor's tool was approved. Vendors can say the hospital deployed it outside the validated use case. Clinicians can say they followed the recommendation they were given. The patient's family is left trying to figure out who to hold accountable and finding that responsibility has been distributed so widely it effectively disappears.⏎⏎There is also the employment piece, which I think gets dismissed too quickly in these discussions as being about workers protecting turf. It isn't only that. When you reduce staffing ratios because an AI system is supposedly handling part of the cognitive load, you are making a bet that the system won't fail in ways that require human intervention. And when it does fail, there may not be enough experienced humans around who remember how to catch the problem without the tool.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place anywhere in medicine. I'm saying HHS should stop treating acceleration as the default goal and start asking harder questions about what safeguards need to be in place before any of this gets embedded deeply enough that reversing it becomes practically impossible. Once hospital systems build their workflows around a particular vendor's platform, and that vendor gets acquired or changes its pricing model or gets hacked, the institution is stuck in a way that affects patient care directly. That dependency risk is real and I have not seen it taken seriously in any of the materials HHS has put out so far.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0023♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Wright♔♔California♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February of 2023, and I watched nurses spend more time documenting in the EHR than they spent at her bedside. I am not opposed to technology on principle. But what I keep coming back to, reading through this RFI, is the question nobody seems to want to ask directly: what problem are we actually trying to solve, and for whom?⏎⏎The push to "accelerate AI adoption" in clinical care treats speed of adoption as obviously good. I don't think that's obvious at all. When my mother was in the ICU, what mattered was that her nurse noticed she was more agitated than her vitals suggested, and made a judgment call that turned out to be right. That is not a pattern matching problem. That is a human being paying attention to another human being in a way that cannot be cleanly separated from the fact that they were both human. I worry that we are building systems that will make it easier to replace that kind of attention with something that looks like attention but isn't.⏎⏎On the specific question of what regulatory actions HHS should take, I want to focus on two things.⏎⏎First, the reimbursement question. The RFI asks about reimbursement structures that could support AI adoption. My concern is that reimbursement incentives will do what reimbursement incentives always do, which is push the behavior that gets paid for regardless of whether it's actually better for patients. If CMS creates billing codes or incentive payments tied to AI tool deployment, hospitals will deploy AI tools. That does not mean patients will be better off. The evidence base for most clinical AI tools is genuinely weak. A 2023 review in the BMJ looked at hundreds of AI diagnostic tools and found that the vast majority had never been tested in a real clinical environment outside the institution that built them. Paying for deployment before we have that kind of external validation seems backwards.⏎⏎Second, and this is the one I feel more strongly about, the administrative and insurance side. The RFI frames AI in insurance decisions somewhat gently, but what we're really talking about is prior authorization automation. United Healthcare's use of an AI model called nH Predict to deny post-acute care claims was the subject of a class action lawsuit filed in 2023. The lawsuit alleged the model had a 90% overturn rate on appeal, meaning that when humans reviewed the denials, they were wrong nine times out of ten. That is not efficiency. I don't know what to call it exactly but it is not efficiency.⏎⏎There's also something that bothers me about the framing of this whole RFI, which is the assumption that the main barriers to AI adoption are regulatory friction and slow reimbursement. That might be true for some vendors. But for clinicians and patients, the barriers might include things like: the tools don't work well enough, the tools introduce new liability questions nobody has answered, the tools are trained on data that underrepresents certain populations, and nobody has figured out who is responsible when an AI recommendation contributes to a bad outcome. Those aren't friction problems. They're substantive problems, and accelerating past them doesn't make them go away.⏎⏎I think HHS should be asking hospitals and health systems to demonstrate clinical benefit, not just deployment, before any new reimbursement pathways are created. And honestly the prior authorization AI question probably needs Congressional attention not just HHS guidance because the agency's authority to regulate insurer decision-making tools is genuinely limited and I'm not sure a request for information is the right vehicle for addressing something with this much♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0024♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Nguyen♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔UnitedHealth Group♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023 and what I remember most is how much it mattered that her night nurse, a woman named Deb, actually knew her. Knew that she got agitated when the lights came on suddenly. Knew that she'd stop answering questions if she felt rushed. That kind of knowing doesn't come from a chart. It doesn't come from a pattern-matching system trained on a million other patients. It came from Deb sitting with her and paying attention.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read about this push to accelerate AI in clinical care, because what I hear underneath the language about efficiency and adoption is an assumption that medicine is basically an information problem and that better information processing equals better care. I don't think that's true, and I think HHS should be extremely cautious before using its regulatory and reimbursement authority to push AI into clinical settings faster than we actually understand what we're doing.⏎⏎The question I want to respond to is the one about what HHS can do to encourage adoption. My answer is: less than you think, and more slowly than you're planning.⏎⏎Here's what worries me concretely. AI tools can perform well in the research setting where they were validated and then behave very differently in actual deployment, in community hospitals with incomplete records, with patient populations that don't look like the training data, with overworked staff who don't have time to second-guess a recommendation that comes out of a system the hospital paid a lot of money for. There was a widely reported case with an algorithm used by hospitals across the country that was systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and costs are a reflection of access, not illness. That kind of bias doesn't show up until someone looks for it, and most institutions aren't looking. The system appeared authoritative. It was producing numbers. People trusted it.⏎⏎What I'd actually want HHS to do is slow down on the reimbursement incentives side. If you start paying more for AI-assisted care or building AI use into quality metrics before we have real post-deployment surveillance requirements, you're going to push hospitals to adopt tools that haven't been adequately studied in their specific contexts. You'll get adoption without accountability.⏎⏎The employment piece also doesn't get talked about enough honestly. I've heard the argument that AI will reduce clinician burnout by handling administrative tasks, and maybe that's true in some narrow cases. But the same tools can just as easily be used to justify reducing support staff, narrowing the scope of what nurses and social workers and patient navigators do, or automating prior authorization denials in ways that are even harder to appeal than the current system. Insurance companies already use algorithmic tools to deny claims at scale, and patients have almost no recourse when that happens. Accelerating AI adoption in that context means accelerating a process that is already failing people.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no role anywhere ever. I'm saying the framing of this RFI, with its emphasis on acceleration, feels like it's already decided the answer and is just looking for input on the details. The question of whether we should be moving this fast in this direction, whether some parts of medicine should stay slower and more human and more accountable even if that costs something in efficiency, that question isn't really being asked here and it should be.⏎⏎Deb retired in April. I don't know who replaced her or whether that unit has a new staffing model.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0025♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Moore♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that her primary care physician's office had flagged as "low risk" using an automated triage tool. I'm not saying the tool caused her near-death. I genuinely don't know. But nobody could tell me what it had recommended or why, and that uncertainty has stayed with me ever since.⏎⏎What I want to say to HHS is this: the question you're asking in this RFI is framed wrong. You're asking how to accelerate AI adoption. That framing assumes acceleration is the goal. I'd ask you to slow down and think about whether embedding these systems more deeply into clinical care is actually something patients have consented to, structurally, as a society. We haven't had that conversation.⏎⏎Medicine works because a specific human being is accountable to you. Your doctor can lose their license. They can be sued. They can look you in the eye when they've made a mistake. When an algorithm gets it wrong, who do you call? The vendor will say the hospital misconfigured it. The hospital will say the clinician should have overridden it. The clinician will say they were following protocol. I've seen this exact argument play out, not in my mother's case but in a lawsuit I read about involving an insurers AI-driven prior authorization system that denied a post-surgical rehabilitation stay and the patient deteriorated at home. No one was held accountable in any meaningful way.⏎⏎The bias problem is also not theoretical. There's documented evidence that some sepsis prediction algorithms perform worse on Black patients because the training data reflected historical disparities in who got admitted to ICUs and when. If you accelerate deployment of these tools without fixing that, you are accelerating inequity. Faster is not better here.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the employment question because I think HHS is probably not thinking about it hard enough in this RFI. Medical coders, care coordinators, prior authorization nurses, patient navigators -- these are real jobs that real people with healthcare training do, and they do them with judgment that comes from talking to actual patients. My cousin is a care coordinator in Tulsa and she's already watching her department shrink. The hospital tells the staff it's "efficiency." What it actually is, is that an AI tool now handles the first pass on about 60% of the cases she used to manage, and they didn't backfill the two positions that left. So now she's managing higher complexity cases with less collegial support and more documentation burden because the AI outputs require human sign-off that takes longer than just doing the thing herself.⏎⏎That's the reality. Not the demo. The demo always works.⏎⏎I'm honestly not anti-technology as a general matter. I use electronic health records, I think telehealth saved my father-in-law from missing a medication adjustment during COVID. But there's a difference between tools that support clinical judgment and systems that supplant it or that quietly shift decision-making authority to vendors who have no clinical training and no accountability to patients.⏎⏎The question I'd actually want HHS to answer is: what happens when one of these systems is wrong in a way that harms thousands of patients before anyone notices? The FDA's premarket review process for software as a medical device is still catching up, and post-market surveillance for AI tools is basically voluntary right now. That's not a regulatory framework that warrants acceleration. That's a regulatory framework that warrants caution.⏎⏎I'd urge HHS to require independent clinical validation before any AI tool can be used in diagnostic or treatment recommendation contexts, and I mean validation in the specific patient population where it will be deployed, not just on some benchmark dataset from a research hospital in 2019.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0026♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Miller♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Francis Medical Center in Peoria in February of 2023, and during that time I watched three different attending physicians spend a combined maybe forty minutes actually at her bedside. The rest of the time, someone was looking at a screen. I'm not blaming those doctors personally - they were stretched impossibly thin - but what I keep thinking about when I read proposals like this one is that we are trying to solve a human problem by adding more technology, and I am genuinely not sure that makes sense.⏎⏎The question I want to address is the one about what barriers HHS should remove to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. My answer is: please slow down and think harder about whether acceleration is the right goal at all.⏎⏎Medicine is not primarily an information processing problem. I know that sounds like something a Luddite would say, and I want to be clear that I am not opposed to technology in general. But when my mother couldn't explain where her pain was because she was on oxygen and frightened and 74 years old, what helped was a nurse who had been doing this for twenty years and who knew from watching her face that something had changed before any monitor showed it. I don't know how you train an AI on that. I don't know how you even measure it.⏎⏎The safety arguments here seem underappreciated in this RFI. We know from published research - I'm thinking of work that came out around 2019-2021 on sepsis prediction algorithms - that tools which look impressive in controlled validation studies can perform much worse when deployed at scale in real hospitals with messier data. The Epic deterioration index, for example, generated so many alerts at some institutions that nursing staff started ignoring them, which is probably worse than having no alert at all. That's alarm fatigue, and it's a well documented problem, and yet we're talking about adding more AI-generated signals into already overwhelmed clinical environments.⏎⏎What worries me just as much is the accountability question. When an AI system recommends a course of treatment and the clinician follows it and the patient is harmed, who is responsible? The vendor will say the clinician should have exercised independent judgment. The hospital will say they implemented the tool as specified. The clinician will say they were following best practices by using the approved decision support. I have watched something like this play out with my mother's billing - not AI exactly, but an automated prior authorization system that denied a medication her cardiologist prescribed, and it took nine days and two appeals to get it approved. Nobody at the insurance company seemed to be actually responsible for that decision. Nobody had looked at her chart. And this is the direction we want to go with clinical care itself?⏎⏎I understand there are genuine workforce shortages and that AI might help with documentation burden or scheduling. I'm not saying every use is equally problematic. But this RFI is asking what barriers to remove, and I think the better question is what guardrails to build before any further deployment happens. At minimum: independent pre-deployment validation requirements that aren't funded by the vendor, mandatory disclosure to patients when AI tools influenced their diagnosis or treatment, and some mechanism - I don't know exactly what it would look like legally - for holding vendors liable when their systems contribute to patient harm rather than letting that liability dissolve.⏎⏎The way this RFI is framed assumes that faster adoption is better, and that the main challenges are technical and regulatory friction. I think the main challenges are ethical and structural, and removing friction without addressing those first seems like it could cause real harm to real people. People who are already scared and vulnerable and not really in a position to push back on whatever the system tells their doctor.⏎⏎I should also mention - and I realize this is a tangent but I think it matters - that the communities most likely to be harmed by poorly validated AI systems are the same communities that already receive worse care. Training data for these systems skews toward larger academic medical centers, toward patients with better documented health histories, toward English speakers. A model trained mostly on data from well-resourced hospital systems and then deployed in a rural critical access hospital in, say, eastern Kentucky is not the same model anymore in any practical sense.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0027♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Martinez♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February 2023, and in that time I watched maybe a dozen different clinicians try to piece together what was happening to her. What made the difference wasn't any software. It was a night-shift nurse named Denise who noticed that my mother kept turning her head to the left when the pain spiked, something she'd been doing her whole life when she was scared, and who flagged that as meaningful. No algorithm trained on discharge summaries would have known that. I don't know how you encode that.⏎⏎I'm responding here specifically to the questions about what regulatory actions HHS should take to accelerate AI adoption, because I think the framing of the question itself is the problem. "Accelerating adoption" presupposes that adoption is good and the only issue is pace. That's not an obvious truth. It's a policy choice dressed up as momentum.⏎⏎The safety argument against rushing this is serious and I don't think HHS has grappled with it honestly. AI systems trained on historical health data inherit every inequality and bad habit baked into that data. A tool trained mostly on records from well-insured, well-documented patients at large academic medical centers is going to perform differently on a 68-year-old diabetic woman in rural Appalachia whose records are scattered across four different systems and who has been systematically undertreated for pain her entire life because of documented provider bias. The system will look confident. It will spit out a recommendation. And the clinician, who is overworked and maybe hasn't slept, will be tempted to defer to it. That's not a hypothetical. The 2019 Obermeyer study in Science showed that a widely-used commercial algorithm was systematically underestimating how sick Black patients were, and that system had been running quietly for years before anyone checked. HHS accelerating adoption without mandatory pre-deployment bias audits and post-deployment surveillance requirements would be, in my view, genuinely reckless.⏎⏎There's also the question of what happens to the clinician relationship when AI becomes standard of care. I keep thinking about this because it's not just about accuracy. A patient who knows that a machine generated their treatment recommendation experiences their care differently. They may not complain less. They may not be less satisfied on whatever survey the hospital is using. But something real has changed about whether they feel seen. I'm not sure how to quantify that and I suspect that's partly why HHS isn't asking about it.⏎⏎The employment piece matters too and I'd be suspicious of anyone who dismisses it quickly. When hospitals talk about AI reducing administrative burden, what they frequently mean is that they plan to reduce the number of people performing those tasks. Medical coders, prior authorization staff, even some diagnostic roles, those are real jobs held by people who understand the clinical and human context of the work they do. A prior auth reviewer who spent ten years as a floor nurse brings something to that job that a language model does not. The efficiency gains get captured by the institution or the vendor. The costs get distributed onto patients and workers.⏎⏎I do want to be fair here. I understand there are genuine problems, physician burnout is real, diagnostic errors cost lives, administrative waste is staggering. I'm not saying the status quo is fine. But the answer to those problems might not be AI, or might be a much more limited and carefully regulated version of AI than what the industry is currently pushing. The fact that a technology can help in some circumstances doesn't mean we should accelerate its spread into all circumstances before we understand the risks.⏎⏎What I'd actually like HHS to do is require that any AI tool used in a clinical decision-support role be required to disclose to the patient that it was used, in plain language, before or at the point of care. Not buried in a consent form. Not technically disclosed somewhere in the EHR. Actually told to the patient. I'd also like to see mandatory reporting when an AI recommendation preceded an adverse outcome, similar to how we report medication errors. Right now there's no systematic way to even know how often AI is contributing to harm because nobody is required to track it.⏎⏎The other thing worth saying is that "clinical care" is being used very broadly in this RFI and I think that's deliberate. Prior authorization decisions are not really clinical care. They are coverage decisions made by insurers, and AI is already being used to deny claims at scale. Cigna reportedly used an AI system in 2023 that allowed physicians to deny claims after spending an average of 1.2 seconds reviewing each case. That is not clinical decision support. That is automation of denial. If HHS is genuinely interested in patient outcomes, that use of AI deserves much more scrutiny than the RFI currently gives it, and I'd argue it shouldn't be accelerated at all.⏎⏎I'd also want to know more about who is actually asking HHS to move faster on this and whether those parties have a financial interest in the answer, which♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0028♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔King♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔Carnegie Mellon University Block Center for Technology and Society♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in 2019 after a medication interaction that three different physicians missed on rounds. I'm not telling that story to argue against technology. I'm telling it because what finally caught the problem was a night nurse who noticed my mother was holding her jaw funny and asked her about it. That nurse had been on shift for ten hours. She was tired. She had six other patients. And she still picked up on something that no chart, no monitor, no alert system flagged. I think about that a lot when I read materials like this RFI.⏎⏎The question HHS is asking here is essentially: what can we do to speed up AI adoption in clinical care? And I want to push back on the premise before I get to any specifics, because the framing assumes that faster adoption is the goal and the only question is how to remove friction. That worries me. Some of the friction is there for a reason.⏎⏎What I want to address is the safety question and what I'd call the accountability question, because I think they're related in ways the RFI doesn't quite grapple with.⏎⏎On safety: healthcare is genuinely different from other domains where AI has shown promise. A recommendation engine that gets it wrong shows you a bad movie. A clinical decision support tool that gets it wrong can contribute to a missed diagnosis, a delayed treatment, an unnecessary procedure. The difference matters. And the problem isn't just that AI systems can be wrong, it's that they can be wrong in ways that are hard to detect and that look authoritative. Clinicians are already under enormous time pressure. If a tool gives a confident-seeming output, the realistic response in a busy ED or a short primary care visit is often going to be to accept it rather than interrogate it. That's not a failure of the clinician. That's just how cognitive load works.⏎⏎There's also a validation problem that I don't think gets enough attention. A system might be validated on data from academic medical centers in the northeast and then deployed in rural critical access hospitals with completely different patient populations, documentation practices, staffing ratios. The performance gap between those settings can be enormous. I've read studies suggesting that some sepsis prediction models show AUROCs that drop by 0.10 to 0.15 or more when moved outside their training environment. That's not a small margin. And there's no systematic requirement right now that I'm aware of that forces ongoing post-deployment monitoring in the way that, say, a new drug would require post-market surveillance.⏎⏎The accountability piece is maybe even harder. When a clinician makes an error, there is a person. There is a license. There is a malpractice system, however imperfect. When an AI system contributes to a bad outcome, the question of who is responsible becomes genuinely murky. The vendor says the clinician was supposed to exercise independent judgment. The hospital says the vendor represented the tool as validated. The clinician says they were following the system's recommendation because they had no reason to doubt it and no time to second-guess every output. That's not a hypothetical. That pattern is already showing up in litigation and in incident reports.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in healthcare. I'm genuinely not sure where I land on all of this. There are narrow applications, like flagging certain imaging findings for radiologist review, where the evidence seems more solid. But the push to accelerate adoption broadly, across clinical care and administrative functions and insurance decisions, feels like it's running ahead of the evidence and ahead of the governance structures we'd need to make it safe.⏎⏎HHS should be asking not just how to speed this up but whether certain uses should require demonstrated real-world performance data before deployment, whether there should be mandatory incident reporting when AI tools are involved in adverse events, and who bears liability when the tool and the clinician and the institution all point at each other.⏎⏎The administrative side is worth mentioning separately because I think it gets treated as lower stakes and it isn't. Prior authorization denials affect real patient care. If those decisions are being shaped or automated by AI systems trained on cost-minimization objectives, the consequences for patients can be just as serious as a clinical error. And the people on the receiving end of those denials, often already sick, often already dealing with a lot, have even less ability to push back against an opaque algorithmic decision than they would against a human one.⏎⏎I'd really like to see HHS focus this effort on governance, transparency requirements, and liability clarity before talking about incentives for faster adoption. The incentives for adoption are already enormous on the vendor side.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0029♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Gonzalez♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔Society of Critical Care Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's Medical Center in Milwaukee in February 2023, and not once did I feel like the nurses and doctors who cared for her were moving too slowly or making decisions that needed to be replaced by a computer. What I saw was people thinking hard about a complicated situation, talking to us, changing their minds, asking questions, and sometimes just sitting with her when she was frightened. I keep thinking about that when I read about efforts to bring AI into clinical care, because I genuinely cannot see what problem is being solved for patients like her.⏎⏎The question I want to address is the one about what barriers HHS thinks it should remove to accelerate AI adoption. My answer is that before removing barriers, someone should ask whether the barriers exist for good reasons. Informed consent processes, documentation requirements, liability frameworks, these aren't just red tape. They are the residue of things that went wrong. If AI tools are being held back by requirements that they demonstrate safety and effectiveness in real clinical populations before wide deployment, that is not a barrier. That is the point.⏎⏎What worries me most is that the push to accelerate adoption seems to assume that speed is a value in itself here. But consider what happened with early sepsis prediction algorithms. A study published in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021 looked at Epic's sepsis prediction tool and found it performed significantly worse in real-world conditions than the company had claimed, generating large numbers of false alarms that nurses described as exhausting and distracting. The hospital systems that deployed it weren't doing anything wrong exactly, they were trying to save lives, but the gap between the validation data and actual performance was wide enough to cause real harm. That's one documented example of a well-funded, widely used system falling short, and there are more.⏎⏎There's also the question of who pays for these tools and who benefits from them. Hospitals and insurers are under enormous financial pressure, and AI gets sold to administrators as a productivity solution. I have no way to verify this from the outside, but I strongly suspect a significant portion of the AI deployment happening right now in health systems is driven less by clinical evidence and more by the desire to justify leaner staffing or to speed up prior authorization denials. That's not care improvement. When AI is used to automate the denial of claims, which UnitedHealth Group has already faced lawsuits over related to its nH Predict tool, the patient doesn't experience that as innovation. They experience it as getting told no by something they can't argue with.⏎⏎I want to be honest that I'm not opposed to technology in medicine as a general principle. I understand that there are things like imaging analysis where machine tools may genuinely help. But there's a difference between a specific, validated, narrowly scoped tool used as one input among many, and a broad institutional push to put AI into clinical workflows across the board before we understand what we're doing.⏎⏎The depersonalization concern is real and I don't think it gets taken seriously enough in these policy conversations. When a clinician is managing an AI dashboard and reviewing flagged cases rather than talking to a patient, something has shifted. The patient may not even know it happened. And if something goes wrong, the trail of accountability gets very murky very fast. The clinician says they followed the recommendation, the hospital says the vendor was responsible, the vendor says the clinician should have used judgment. That loop doesn't resolve cleanly and in the meantime a patient or their family is left without a clear answer about what happened or why.⏎⏎HHS should be asking vendors to provide disaggregated performance data by race, age, insurance status, and geography before any tool is used in a federally funded setting. There should be mandatory reporting when an AI system contributes to an adverse outcome, similar to how medical device adverse events get reported to the FDA. Right now I don't think that infrastructure exists in any consistent way and the absence of it means we're flying somewhat blind about the actual harm being caused.⏎⏎I'm also not sure the administrative AI argument is as safe as proponents suggest. Administrative work in healthcare isn't just form-filling. A billing specialist who has worked in oncology for fifteen years has contextual knowledge that shapes how they code, what they flag, what they question. Replacing that with automated systems trained on historical coding patterns embeds whatever errors and biases existed in those patterns, and then removes the human who might have caught them.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0030♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Garcia♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔Cotiviti♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's Medical Center in Kansas City in February of 2023, and during that time I watched a parade of residents, nurses, and attending physicians piece together what was happening with her through conversation, observation, and a kind of slow collaborative reasoning that no system could have replicated. One of the nurses noticed something about how my mother was holding her arm that led to a conversation that led to a revised diagnosis. That did not come from a dataset. I keep thinking about that when I read materials like this RFI, which seems to assume that the main obstacle to better healthcare is that we haven't yet figured out how to feed enough data into enough algorithms.⏎⏎The question HHS is really asking here, underneath all the language about "accelerating adoption," is whether the federal government should be actively pushing AI into clinical care. My answer is no, and I want to focus specifically on the question of what regulatory actions HHS might take, because I think the framing of this RFI is backwards. You are asking how to speed this up. I think you should be asking whether it's ready at all and who bears the cost when it isn't.⏎⏎There is a study from 2021, published in the New England Journal of Medicine, that looked at a widely used commercial algorithm for identifying patients who needed additional care. The algorithm was systematically underestimating the needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients had historically received less care, so the model learned to predict less care for them. This wasn't a fringe or obscure finding. It was published prominently and the algorithm was in use at hundreds of hospitals. That is not a problem you solve by moving faster. That is a problem that should make everyone pause and ask how many other systems are doing something similar right now, in production, in ways we haven't measured yet.⏎⏎I'm also genuinely worried about what happens to accountability when things go wrong. Right now, if a physician makes a bad call, there is a human being who is responsible. They can lose their license, they can be sued, they can be required to explain their reasoning. When an AI system contributes to a bad outcome, the hospital blames the vendor, the vendor says the hospital deployed it incorrectly, and the patient or their family gets nothing except a complicated legal situation. HHS should be requiring much clearer frameworks for liability before any acceleration happens, not after.⏎⏎The employment piece matters too, even though it tends to get dismissed as special pleading from workers who don't want to adapt. Hospitals have already been cutting medical coders, billing staff, and administrative roles where AI tools have been deployed. Some of those people had years of contextual knowledge about their institution's patient population, their insurance quirks, their documentation patterns. That knowledge doesn't transfer to the algorithm. It just disappears. And then when the algorithm produces errors, there's nobody left who knows enough to catch them.⏎⏎I understand that HHS sees real promise here and I'm not saying every AI application in medicine is worthless. But the specific question of what regulatory and reimbursement actions the Department should take to accelerate adoption, I think the honest answer is: pump the brakes. Require prospective clinical validation in diverse populations before any reimbursement incentives attach to a tool. Require clear disclosure to patients when AI played a role in their diagnosis or treatment recommendation. Establish liability rules that don't let vendors hide behind end-user agreements when their products cause harm.⏎⏎And honestly, I'd want to know what the post-market surveillance looks like. Because right now there's essentially no systematic mechanism for catching a problem like the one in that NEJM study at scale, before it affects tens of thousands of patients. The FDA's approach to software as a medical device has been evolving slowly and the current framework still has pretty significant gaps in how it handles AI tools that update continuously or that get used outside their original validated♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0031♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Hall♔♔New York♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Advocate Christ Medical Center in Oak Lawn, Illinois in February of 2023, and what got her through it was not data. It was a night nurse named Denise who noticed something was off about how my mother was holding her left arm, mentioned it to the attending, and that turned out to matter. That kind of observation is not in any training dataset. It is not a pattern that a system learns from a million prior cases. It is a person paying attention to another person in a room at 2 in the morning.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS is looking at ways to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my immediate reaction is: slow down. Not because I am against technology in the abstract, but because I think the framing of "acceleration" is exactly the wrong frame for this particular domain.⏎⏎The question I want to address is essentially the one about what barriers HHS should remove to help AI move faster into clinical settings. My answer is that some of those barriers exist for good reasons and removing them would be a mistake.⏎⏎Here is what concerns me most. When a hospital or insurer says they are implementing AI to improve care, what that often means in practice is that they are implementing AI to reduce costs and staff. I am not being cynical for the sake of it. There is a real pattern here. UnitedHealthcare's use of an algorithm called nH Predict to deny post-acute care claims was documented extensively in 2023, and a federal lawsuit alleged the system had a 90% overturn rate on appeal, meaning the AI was wrong nine times out of ten when patients pushed back. That is not a demonstration of technology improving care. That is technology being used to make it harder for sick people to get what they need. And the people most affected are elderly Medicare Advantage enrollees who often don't have the energy or resources to appeal.⏎⏎If HHS accelerates AI adoption without first putting hard rules in place around this kind of use, I think we will see more of it, not less. The economic incentive is too strong. Insurers and large hospital systems have every reason to use these tools to cut costs and shift decisionmaking into systems that are difficult to audit and even harder to challenge.⏎⏎I also want to say something about what gets lost when AI becomes the default layer between a patient and a clinical decision. Medicine involves a lot of ambiguity. Patients don't always describe their symptoms accurately. They leave things out because they're embarrassed or scared or they don't think it's relevant. A good clinician reads between the lines. An AI system trained on structured data does not read between the lines, it reads the lines, and if the lines are incomplete or misleading the output can be confidently wrong in a way that a human clinician would have caught because they would have asked a follow-up question or noticed the patient seemed to be holding something back.⏎⏎I am not saying there is no possible role for any kind of decision-support tool anywhere in medicine. I can see arguments for narrow, well-validated uses in things like radiology or flagging drug interactions. But the framing of this RFI treats acceleration as inherently good and barriers as inherently bad, and I don't think that is right. Some barriers are just called standards. Some slowness is called caution. The question is not how fast we can move but whether the people bearing the risk of moving fast are the same people who benefit from it, and right now they are not.⏎⏎The patients who will be harmed by a badly deployed AI tool are not the executives who approved the contract or the vendors who sold the system.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0032♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Harris♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was admitted to a hospital in Cleveland in February 2023 with what turned out to be a relatively straightforward case of sepsis caught early. She recovered fine. But during her stay, a nurse mentioned almost in passing that one of the systems they were using had flagged her as "low acuity" based on her intake data, and that flag had briefly deprioritized some follow-up checks. The nurse corrected it. But she only caught it because she'd been doing this for nineteen years and something felt off. That's the thing nobody in this RFI seems to want to grapple with directly: the catches only work if the humans are still paying full attention, and you cannot pay full attention when you've been told a system is handling it.⏎⏎I want to speak specifically to the questions HHS is asking about barriers to adoption, because I think the framing of the whole RFI gets things backwards. The question shouldn't be "what's slowing AI down" as though the slowness is obviously a problem. Sometimes friction is the system working. Hospitals requiring review, insurers needing human sign-off, clinicians pushing back on algorithmic recommendations - that's not inefficiency. That's accountability in action, and it's one of the few things standing between patients and errors that scale catastrophically.⏎⏎The bias problem is real and I don't think HHS is taking it seriously enough in how this RFI is written. Training data in healthcare is a mess. It reflects decades of unequal treatment, undercoding for certain populations, overdiagnosis in others. A 2019 study published in Science - the Obermeyer study - found that a widely used commerical algorithm was systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients historically received less care, so they cost less, so the algorithm thought they were healthier. That's not a fixable edge case. That's the fundamental problem with using historical data in a system that produced inequitable history. Deploying more AI faster doesn't solve that. It scales it.⏎⏎What also concerns me, and I don't see it addressed anywhere, is the staffing question. The pitch for AI in healthcare keeps saying it will reduce burnout and let clinicians focus on what matters. Maybe. But hospitals are also laying off coders, billing specialists, prior authorization reviewers, and other staff while simultaneously rolling out AI tools to cover those roles. I'm not saying automation is inherently wrong but I am saying that when the same administrators making staffing cuts are also buying AI systems, I'm skeptical that patient care is the primary consideration. And those workers who get displaced - many of them have contextual knowledge about patients and institutions that doesn't transfer to any database. A prior auth reviewer who's worked a regional Medicaid plan for a decade knows things about that population that no model trained on claims data is going to replicate.⏎⏎The other thing HHS should think hard about before accelerating anything is what happens when these systems are wrong and someone gets hurt. Right now if a clinician makes an error there's a relatively clear path: malpractice liability, licensing boards, institutional review. If an AI system contributes to a bad outcome the accountability chain just dissolves. The vendor says the clinician should have overridden it. The hospital says they followed the vendor's implementation guidelines. The clinician says they trusted the system because they were told to. The patient or family is left with nothing. That's not a hypothetical, that's already happening in prior authorization denials where AI tools are rubber-stamping rejections and nobody can explain the reasoning or appeal it to a human who understands the case.⏎⏎HHS should be asking how to preserve human judgment, not how to replace it more efficiently. And specifically, I'd want to know what the department's plan is for ensuring that "AI oversight" roles don't just become checkbox positions where one clinician is nominally responsible for reviewing hundreds of AI decisions per shift with no real capacity to scrutinize any of them.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0033♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Miller♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔Emory University Rollins School of Public Health♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in 2019 after a sepsis diagnosis that got missed for almost 36 hours. The nurses caught it eventually. A nurse who had been on her feet for twelve hours, who knew my mother's face, who noticed that something just seemed off even though the numbers weren't screaming yet. I keep thinking about what happens to that kind of judgment when the system around it is designed to route everything through an algorithm first.⏎⏎That's really what I want to address here, because I think HHS is framing this whole conversation wrong. The question isn't "how do we accelerate AI adoption in clinical care." The question should be "under what circumstances, if any, should clinical judgment be delegated to or filtered through automated systems, and who bears responsibility when it goes wrong." Those are different questions and the second one is much harder.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to adoption, which tells you where the Department's head is already at. What about barriers created by adoption? What about the clinician who now has to explain to a patient why the insurance company's AI denied a prior authorization, when she can't actually get anyone to tell her what criteria the system used? That's happening right now, not hypothetically. UnitedHealth's use of an algorithm called nH Predict to make post-acute care coverage decisions became the subject of a federal lawsuit, and internal documents suggested the denial rate was dramatically higher than what physicians were recommending. That's not a barrier to adoption. That's what adoption already looks like in practice.⏎⏎I understand there are use cases where AI assistance sounds genuinely helpful, and I'm not trying to pretend radiology flagging tools or appointment scheduling software are the same as automated denial systems. But the problem is that once you normalize the infrastructure, once hospitals have integrated these systems and vendors have contracts and reimbursement codes are built around AI-assisted billing, the scope creeps in directions that are very hard to reverse. The least consequential uses become the justification for the more consequential ones.⏎⏎There's also something that bothers me about the employment angle that doesn't get talked about honestly. Medical coding, prior auth review, care coordination, a lot of administrative work in healthcare is done by people who understand the human context of what they're processing. They know that a patient who lists "no fixed address" on intake forms needs a different kind of follow-up. They know that a referral from certain zip codes in their city means the patient probably can't afford the specialist copay and someone should flag that. That contextual knowledge doesn't appear in training data. It's not in the EHR. When you automate those roles, you don't just eliminate jobs, you eliminate the humans who were doing invisible, informal work that the metrics never captured.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require that any AI system used in clinical or coverage decisions be subject to prospective bias audits by independent reviewers, not just the vendors, before deployment. And there should be a clear, enforceable pathway for patients and clinicians to appeal decisions made with AI involvement and get an explanation in plain language. Right now that doesn't exist in any consistent way. "The system recommended" is not an explanation.⏎⏎I'd also ask HHS to think carefully about the difference between AI tools that assist clinicians and AI tools that constrain them. A tool that surfaces information is different from a tool that gatekeeps a decision. The reimbursement and regulatory frameworks being built right now don't clearly distinguish between those two things, and that ambiguity is going to be exploited.⏎⏎My mother's nurse didn't have a protocol for what she noticed. She had experience and she trusted herself enough to push.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0034♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Hall♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Francis Hospital in Hartford in October 2022, and I watched nurses and doctors make judgment calls that no algorithm could have made. She was presenting with symptoms that didn't fit the textbook picture of what was actually wrong with her, and it took a physician who knew how to read her face, who asked my father about how she'd been sleeping, who noticed something about how she was holding her arm, to get to the right answer. I keep thinking about what would have happened if there had been an AI system flagging her chart and pushing the team toward the more statistically likely diagnosis. I genuinely don't know that she'd be here.⏎⏎I want to focus on what I understand to be one of the core questions in this RFI, which is about what actions HHS can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. My answer is: please slow down instead.⏎⏎The safety argument against moving fast here is real and I don't think it gets taken seriously enough. These systems are trained on historical data, and healthcare data is full of the biases and gaps that have always plagued medicine. There's already documented evidence that some AI tools perform worse on Black patients, on women presenting with cardiac symptoms, on patients whose records are fragmented because they moved around or lacked insurance. When those disparities show up in a human clinician's decisions, we can at least ask the clinician to explain themselves, we can report them, we can sue them. When it's baked into a vendor's model, who's accountable? The hospital says they followed the vendor's guidance. The vendor says the hospital deployed it wrong. The patient just got a bad outcome and nobody can really say why.⏎⏎There's also something I'm not sure how to articulate exactly but it's about what medicine is supposed to be. When I was sitting in that waiting room in Hartford I wasn't waiting for an algorithm to process my mother. I was trusting that there were human beings in that building who had taken an oath and who were going to use everything they knew to help her. That relationship, where another person is genuinely responsible for your care, is not a nice-to-have. It's the whole thing. And I worry that if we keep layering AI tools into the process, we slowly shift clinicians into a role where they're mainly checking whether they agree with the machine, and over time the machine's judgment starts to feel more authoritative than their own, and we end up with people who are trained to defer rather than to think.⏎⏎I also want to flag the insurance side of this because it keeps getting folded into the broader AI-in-healthcare conversation but it deserves its own scrutiny. Automating prior authorization denials with AI is not a patient safety improvement. It is a cost-cutting tool dressed up in the language of efficiency. UnitedHealth's use of an algorithm called nH Predict to deny post-acute care claims, which generated a class action lawsuit in 2023, is exactly the kind of thing that HHS should be looking at very carefully before deciding that more AI in administrative functions is a good idea. The people harmed by that were elderly patients who were told their inpatient rehabilitation was no longer medically necessary, based on a model that apparently had a 90% error rate when those denials were appealed.⏎⏎That number needs to sit with people at HHS who are thinking about acceleration. Ninety percent.⏎⏎The employment piece is also something I think about. Medical coders, prior auth nurses, care coordinators, the people who do the actual work of navigating patients through a complicated system - those jobs may look administrative but they require deep knowledge of how specific patients, specific insurers, and specific facilities actually behave. An AI system doesn't have a relationship with the case manager at Aetna who knows that a particular appeal needs to be framed a specific way. That tacit knowledge is going to disappear if we automate those roles, and we won't notice until the system starts failing in ways we can't diagnose.⏎⏎I recognize there are people who genuinely believe AI will reduce burnout and improve access and catch things humans miss. I'm not saying those arguments are worthless. But I don't think HHS has done nearly enough to answer the question of what happens when these tools are wrong, who bears the cost, and whether the populations most likely to be harmed are the same populations that already get the worst care.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to adoption and how to remove them. I'd ask HHS to also think about whether some of those barriers exist for reasons that shouldn't be removed, and whether the pressure to move fast is coming more from vendors who want a market than from patients who want better care.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0035♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Hill♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔Consumer Reports♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Riverside University Health System in 2022 after a sepsis diagnosis that got missed for almost 36 hours. I am not blaming any individual nurse or doctor for that. They were overwhelmed, short-staffed, working a system that was already stretched past its limit. But I keep thinking about what would have happened if an AI tool had been in the loop during those 36 hours. Would it have caught the signs earlier? Maybe. Or would a clinician have deferred to a system that said "low risk" and felt even less urgency to push back on that assessment? I genuinely don't know. That uncertainty is exactly why I'm writing this.⏎⏎The question HHS is asking, essentially, is how can we speed this up. And I want to ask why speed is the right goal here at all. Medicine has been moving fast for decades, and a lot of what's gone wrong in American healthcare, the overtreatment, the fragmented records, the prior authorization nightmare, the burnout, came from moving fast and assuming technology would sort out the problems it created. I don't see a compelling reason to believe AI adoption will be different.⏎⏎What concerns me most is the accountability question. When a physician makes a wrong call, there is a person. There is a license. There is a relationship, however imperfect, between that clinician and the outcome. When an AI tool makes a wrong call and a clinician defers to it, who is accountable? The vendor says the doctor should have overridden it. The doctor says the tool said low risk. The hospital says they deployed it according to the vendor's instructions. I've seen this pattern play out already in the prior authorization context, where insurers use algorithmic tools to deny claims and then when you appeal, nobody can tell you exactly why the denial happened or who made the decision. That is already a serious problem and it exists in a context where the stakes are high but usually not immediately life-or-death. Moving that same dynamic into clinical decision support is a genuinely frightening prospect.⏎⏎There's also the staffing argument that I think deserves more scrutiny than this RFI seems to give it. The pitch is always that AI reduces clinician burnout by handling documentation, routine triage, that kind of thing. But hospitals are businesses, and if AI can handle documentation faster, the likely outcome is not that nurses and doctors get more time with patients. The likely outcome is that the hospital decides it needs fewer nurses and doctors. I don't think that's paranoia. It's just how cost pressures work in a system where labor is the biggest expense. And the people who will feel that most acutely are not the senior physicians at academic medical centers who are participating in these pilots. It's the home health aides, the medical coders, the patient navigators, the people doing jobs that look administrative but actually require understanding a patient's specific situation in ways that are hard to quantify.⏎⏎I want to be clear that I'm not saying no AI tool should ever be used anywhere in healthcare. That would be an unreasonable position. But the framing of this RFI, which is about acceleration and adoption, skips over some questions that I think should come first. Like: what is the process for removing a tool from use when post-deployment evidence shows it's causing harm in a subpopulation? How long does that take? Who triggers it? The FDA's current framework for software as a medical device has serious gaps around continuous learning systems, and I have not seen a satisfying answer to what happens when a model updates itself and the validation data from six months ago no longer reflects how the system actually behaves.⏎⏎The honest answer to this RFI, from my perspective, is that HHS should slow down and do the harder regulatory work before it starts clearing paths to faster adoption. Not because technology is bad, but because the infrastructure for accountability, the ability to audit decisions, to trace harms, to hold someone responsible, just isn't there yet and building it takes time that rushing deployment does not allow.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0036♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Davis♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔Included Health♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Riverside University Health System in Moreno Valley in 2022, and I watched what it actually looks like when a team of nurses and physicians tries to manage a critically ill patient with incomplete information, communication breakdowns, and exhausted staff. It was terrifying and imperfect and also deeply human in ways I'm still thinking about. The attending physician who finally figured out what was wrong with her did it partly by noticing that she was holding her left arm differently than the day before, something no chart entry captured. I don't know how you train a model on that.⏎⏎The RFI asks what HHS can do to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. My response to that question is: please slow down, and think harder about whether acceleration is actually the right goal here.⏎⏎What worries me isn't really the technology itself in some abstract sense. It's the combination of financial pressure, vendor salesmanship, and institutional impatience that tends to determine how these tools actually get deployed. A hospital system that is losing money on inpatient margins isn't going to buy an AI tool because it wants better outcomes. It's going to buy it because someone promised it could reduce nursing ratios or speed up prior authorization or flag patients for earlier discharge. The efficiency framing is almost always there from the start, and the safety validation is usually thinner than the marketing.⏎⏎There's a specific thing I'd ask HHS to focus on, which is the question of who bears liability when an AI-assisted recommendation turns out to be wrong. Right now the answer is essentially nobody, or more precisely, it gets diffused across the clinician who "should have caught it," the hospital that "properly implemented" the tool, and the vendor whose contract almost certainly includes language limiting their exposure. A nurse in Moreno Valley or anywhere else cannot practically push back against a system that her hospital has standardized around, especially if it's embedded in the EHR workflow. She clicks through the alert or the recommendation because the alternative is a documentation exception and a conversation with her supervisor. That's not informed human oversight. That's compliance theater dressed up as clinical judgment.⏎⏎The bias problem also doesn't get enough serious attention in conversations like this one. Training data in healthcare reflects decades of unequal access, differential treatment, and underdocumented populations. A sepsis prediction model trained primarily on data from large academic medical centers will not perform the same way at a safety-net hospital serving a predominantly undocumented or uninsured population, and the gap may not show up in aggregate accuracy statistics. It'll show up in the patients who get flagged late, or not at all. I haven't seen a realistic proposal for how HHS would require ongoing, site-specific performance monitoring in a way that's actually enforceable rather than just a box on a certification checklist.⏎⏎The administrative side of this worries me too, and I think people underestimate it. Insurance prior authorization is already a system that produces enormous harm through delay and denial, and there are vendors right now marketing AI tools to payers specifically to automate and scale that process. The fact that a denial is generated by an algorithm rather than a human reviewer doesn't make it more legitimate. It makes it harder to appeal, harder to understand, and harder to hold anyone accountable for. If HHS is thinking about where AI could cause serious near-term harm, that's where I'd look first, not at some hypothetical future clinical decision support tool.⏎⏎I keep coming back to the liability question because I think it's the one that determines everything else. Without a clear answer to "who is responsible when this goes wrong and a patient is harmed," every other safeguard is mostly aspirational. Right now vendors are writing contracts, hospitals are buying products, and the legal framework for any of this is♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0037♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Rivera♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔American Nurses Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023, and I watched a parade of residents stare at tablet screens while she tried to tell them her pain felt different than it had the day before. Nobody wrote that down in any way that mattered. I kept thinking: if you layer an AI recommendation system on top of that dynamic, what you get is not better care, you get faster dismissal. The machine said she was stable, so she was stable, end of conversation.⏎⏎I want to focus on the question of what "acceleration" actually means here, because I think HHS is framing this wrong. The RFI keeps using language about removing barriers and speeding adoption as though the barriers are the problem. Some of those barriers exist because clinicians, nurses, and patients pushed back on tools that did not work for them, or worked in the demo but fell apart in a busy ED on a Tuesday night. Calling that friction a barrier to be overcome gets things exactly backwards.⏎⏎The safety argument against rushing this is not abstract. There is a study out of Stanford from 2021 that found a widely-used sepsis prediction algorithm had a positive predictive value of only about 18 percent in real-world deployment, meaning more than 80 percent of the alerts it fired were false positives. Clinicians reported that they started ignoring the alerts. So the tool did not just fail to help, it actively trained people to tune out warnings. That is a concrete, documented outcome. And that was a single institution that actually studied it. Most hospitals do not have the resources or the incentive to do that kind of post-deployment audit, so they never find out whether their AI tools are working or not.⏎⏎What worries me about this RFI is that it is asking how to speed things up before there is any serious national infrastructure for finding out whether the things that have already been deployed are doing harm. The FDA has cleared hundreds of AI/ML-based medical devices over the past several years, many under the 510(k) pathway which does not require clinical trial evidence. Once those tools are in use, there is no mandatory adverse event reporting system specifically designed to catch AI-related failures. Clinicians can file MedWatch reports but most don't, and even if they did, the current system was not built to identify patterns that look like "the algorithm got it wrong" rather than "the device malfunctioned."⏎⏎I also want to say something about insurance AI, which the RFI treats almost as an afterthought. Prior authorization denials powered by AI are not a future risk, they are happening now. UnitedHealth's use of an algorithm called nH Predict to deny post-acute care claims was the subject of a federal lawsuit in 2023. The algorithm reportedly had a 90 percent overturn rate on appeal, which means it was denying claims that were almost always ultimately approved, just making patients and providers fight for them. That is not efficiency. That is obstruction wearing a technical costume.⏎⏎I understand there are genuine benefits in narrow, well-validated applications. Radiology AI that flags a possible fracture for a human radiologist to review is different from an algorithm that decides whether a 74 year old woman gets to stay in a rehab facility. HHS should be making that distinction much more clearly and building regulatory frameworks that treat those cases differently, not issuing a broad invitation to accelerate adoption across the board.⏎⏎The question of who bears the cost when AI gets it wrong also needs a real answer before any of this goes further. Right now the answer is basically: the patient. Sometimes the provider. Almost never the vendor. That is not a workable accountability structure for a domain where the stakes include whether someone lives or dies or goes bankrupt paying for care that was denied or delayed because a model made a bad prediction.⏎⏎I am not sure what the right reimbursement structure for AI tools should look like, but I am fairly sure that paying for AI-assisted care at the same rate as regular care, without any requirement to demonstrate that the AI component actually improved outcomes, is going to produce a race to the bottom where the incentive is to deploy the cheapest available tool and call it innovation. There is probably a version of a certification or coverage with evidence development pathway that could work here but I'd want to see very specific conditions attached, not just a general commitment to "real world evidence" which has historically meant whatever the vendor decides to collect.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0038♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Rivera♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Scripps Mercy in San Diego in February 2023, and I watched nurses and doctors make judgment calls that no algorithm could have made. She kept insisting she felt "wrong" in a way she couldn't describe, and one of her nurses — a woman who had worked that floor for sixteen years — pushed for an additional scan based on nothing more than that vague complaint and something she noticed about how my mother was holding her left arm. It caught a secondary clot that the monitoring systems had not flagged. I keep thinking about what happens when that nurse's intuition gets replaced by a dashboard telling her the patient's vitals are within normal range.⏎⏎The RFI asks broadly about what actions HHS can take to accelerate AI adoption, and I want to register something that probably won't be popular in this comment period: I think that framing is already the wrong one. The question isn't how to speed this up. The question is whether the Department has seriously grappled with what gets lost.⏎⏎Medicine isn't primarily an information processing problem. A patient who is scared, in pain, or cognitively impaired doesn't produce clean data. They contradict themselves, they minimize symptoms, they're embarrassed, they don't want to worry their family. The clinician's job involves reading all of that and holding it against years of experience with similar patients in similar situations. What AI systems do well — pattern matching across large datasets — is genuinely useful in some narrow contexts, like radiology image review or flagging drug interactions. I'm not saying there's zero role here. But the Department seems to be treating AI as a general-purpose improvement to clinical care, and I think that's a category error.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem, which I don't think gets taken seriously enough in these discussions. Training data in healthcare reflects decades of unequal care. Systems trained on that data will reproduce those inequalities, and the problem is that the outputs look authoritative. A clinician might push back against a biased colleague. It's much harder to push back against a number generated by a model that nobody in the room fully understands. A 2019 study published in Science found that a widely used commercial algorithm for allocating healthcare resources was systematically underestimating the needs of Black patients by a significant margin — this wasn't a fringe system, it was being used on approximately 200 million people per year. That study is now five years old and I have no confidence the industry has actually fixed the underlying problem.⏎⏎What worries me about the economic dimension is that AI in healthcare is being marketed as a way to address clinician burnout and staffing shortages, but there's an obvious financial incentive for hospitals and insurers to use it instead as a justification for thinner staffing. Those aren't the same thing. If an AI system can flag deteriorating patients, that's genuinely helpful — but only if you still have enough nurses on the floor to respond. The technology doesn't create capacity, it just reshuffles where attention goes. I've talked to travel nurses in three different states and the pattern I hear is that documentation burdens have increased, not decreased, as these tools get added.⏎⏎On the insurance side I feel even more strongly. Automated prior authorization denials are already causing documented patient harm. HHS should be restricting the use of AI in coverage decisions, not accelerating it. When an algorithm denies a claim, nobody is accountable. The patient gets a letter. There's a phone number. The physician submits an appeal. The system generates another denial. This is happening right now, not in some hypothetical future.⏎⏎I understand there are genuine benefits in specific, narrow applications and I'm not asking HHS to prohibit all use of these tools. But I would ask the Department to consider whether "accelerating adoption" should be the frame at all, versus something more like "establishing conditions under which specific applications can be proven safe and equitable before deployment." The difference matters. Right now vendors are deploying systems in hospitals before those systems have been validated in anything like real-world conditions, and the regulatory infrastructure to catch problems after the fact is nowhere near adequate.⏎⏎There's no equivalent of the FDA's clinical trial requirement for most AI tools used in clinical workflow. A hospital can start using a risk stratification algorithm tomorrow without ever having to demonstrate it works better than what it replaces or that it doesn't produce disparate outcomes for different patient populations. That gap is♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0039♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Baker♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Regions Hospital in St. Paul in March of 2023 and I sat with her through a lot of that. What I remember most is not the monitors or the medications. It is the nurses who came in and talked to her like a person, who noticed when she was scared even when she couldn't say so, who adjusted things based on watching her face. I keep thinking about what that would have looked like if the people in that room were primarily responding to what a dashboard told them to do.⏎⏎That's what I want HHS to actually sit with before it moves forward with any of this. Not the efficiency numbers. Not the vendor presentations. The question of what happens to the texture of care when clinical judgment gets routed through a recommendation engine.⏎⏎I'll focus my comments on the question about what regulatory or reimbursement actions HHS should take, because that's where I think the real damage can happen fast.⏎⏎The concern I have is that reimbursement incentives are going to do the work that no one wants to say out loud. If CMS starts paying more for visits that use AI-assisted diagnostics, or if insurers get permission to use algorithmic prior authorization systems without meaningful human review, then hospitals and practices don't really have a choice. They adopt the tools or they fall behind financially. That's not acceleration, that's coercion dressed up as modernization. And the institutions most likely to adopt fast are also the ones with the least margin to absorb a bad outcome when the system fails on an unusual patient.⏎⏎There's a real problem with how these tools get validated and then deployed. A model gets tested on data from, say, academic medical centers with relatively complete records and decent socioeconomic diversity in the test set, and then it gets sold to a rural critical access hospital in Nebraska where the patient population, the documentation habits, and the staffing ratios are all completely different. The FDA clearance or whatever approval pathway it went through doesn't really address that gap. And the clinicians on the ground often have no idea what the model was actually trained on or what its failure modes look like. They just see a score or a flag and they're expected to act on it or document why they didn't.⏎⏎That accountability structure is backwards. Right now the burden is on the clinician to override the machine and justify it. It should be on the vendor and the institution to prove the tool is appropriate for the specific population being served before it ever enters the workflow. I don't see how HHS accelerating adoption gets us closer to that.⏎⏎I also want to say something about administrative AI because I think it gets treated as low stakes and it isn't. Prior authorization denials generated or assisted by AI systems are already affecting patients right now. There was a report last year about UnitedHealth's algorithm denying post-acute care claims at a very high rate for elderly patients on Medicare Advantage, and the internal target approval threshold was apparently baked into the system in a way that made appeals difficult to win. That's not a hypothetical future harm. Speeding up the adoption of that kind of tool is not a neutral act.⏎⏎The HHS framing in this RFI assumes the question is how to accelerate. I think the prior question is whether acceleration in this domain is even appropriate given how little we understand about cumulative effects on care quality, workforce, and patient trust. The reimbursement levers especially should not be pointed in this direction until there's a much more robust framework for post-market surveillance of clinical AI tools in real-world settings, not just the settings where they were originally validated.⏎⏎I'm not sure what the right answer is on the research side, honestly. But on the regulatory and reimbursement side, the ask is straightforward: do not create financial incentives that push adoption faster than oversight can follow. And do not allow algorithmic prior authorization to expand without requiring a specific, auditable human review step for any denial.⏎⏎The form on this RFI said comments were due by♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0040♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Thomas♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Riverside University Health System in Moreno Valley in 2022, and I watched what good nursing looks like up close. The nurses knew when she was anxious before she said a word. They adjusted things based on how she was sitting, what her husband's face looked like in the corner, whether she'd eaten. None of that is in a dataset anywhere. I keep thinking about that when I read about AI being rolled out to help clinicians make decisions, and I get genuinely worried that HHS is treating this as a question of how fast to go rather than whether to go at all.⏎⏎The RFI asks specifically about what regulatory actions could accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. My honest answer is that HHS should be asking the opposite question first. Before you think about accelerating anything, you need to ask what happens when these systems are wrong and what the patient can actually do about it. Right now the answer seems to be: not much. The vendor blames the hospital's implementation. The hospital blames the vendor's training data. The clinician says they were just using a tool as a reference. The patient or their family is left with a bad outcome and a chain of accountability that nobody can follow. That is not a hypothetical. That is the structure that gets built in when you move fast and figure out liability later.⏎⏎There is also the question of whose data trained these systems. The AI tools being marketed to hospitals right now were almost certainly built on records from large academic medical centers, which means they reflect the demographics, documentation habits, and disease patterns of patients who were already well-served by the system. Applying those models to a safety-net hospital in rural Mississippi or a community clinic serving recent immigrants is not the same thing as applying them where they were validated, and I don't think anyone really knows how much that gap matters in practice. A 2019 study in Science found that a widely used commercial algorithm systematically underestimated health needs in Black patients because it used cost as a proxy for need. That was before generative AI, before large language models, before any of the current wave. The bias problem has not gotten simpler.⏎⏎On the employment question, which the RFI sort of gestures at without really engaging with it: I think the honest version of what many health systems want from AI is fewer medical coders, fewer care coordinators, fewer people doing prior authorizations, and eventually fewer nurses per shift because the monitoring is "automated." That is a cost-cutting strategy dressed up as innovation. I'm not saying every administrator thinks that cynically, but the incentive is there and HHS should not pretend otherwise. When you make it easier for an insurer to deploy AI for claims adjudication, you are making it easier to deny care with less human review, and we already have a crisis of people getting necessary procedures denied by reviewers who spend eight minutes per case.⏎⏎I don't think I'm anti-technology. I have a smartphone. My kids use computers for school. But there is something different about a system that is making inferences about whether you are sick enough to be admitted, or whether your pain is real, or whether you need a specialist. Those decisions require someone who can be called to account in a way a model cannot.⏎⏎One more thing that I don't think gets enough attention: once these tools are embedded in the EHR workflow, clinicians stop questioning them not because they trust them but because they are tired and the path of least resistance is to go with what the system suggests. There's a term for it, automation bias, and there is actually decent research showing it increases error rates in high-stakes decision environments. Embedding AI into clinical workflow doesn't just change what tools people use, it changes how people think, and probably within a few years in ways that would be very hard to reverse even if the outcomes data turned out to be bad.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require that any AI tool used in a clinical or insurance decision context publish its validation data broken down by race, age, insurance status, and the specific care setting where it was tested, before deployment, not after. And there should be a real mechanism for frontline clinicians to flag when a tool seems to be steering them wrong, not just a comment box that nobody reads.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0041♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Rivera♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔American Health Information Management Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Piedmont Atlanta Hospital in March 2023 after a stroke, and what I watched during those eleven days has shaped everything I think about this question. The nurses knew her. Not her chart — her. They noticed when she was frightened versus when she was in pain, even when she couldn't speak clearly enough to explain the difference. That distinction mattered for how they treated her. No algorithm was going to pick that up, and I don't think HHS should be working to build one that tries.⏎⏎I want to focus on what I think is the most underexamined part of this RFI, which is the question of what happens to accountability when AI is embedded in clinical decision-making. Not the technical question of whether the models are accurate enough. The accountability question. Because right now, if a physician makes a wrong call, there is a person who made that call. They have a license. They can be questioned, disciplined, sued. That is uncomfortable but it is also the structure that makes patients able to trust the system at all. What happens when a physician says, and I have already heard this from doctors I know, "the AI flagged it as low risk so I didn't pursue it further"? Who is responsible then? The hospital that purchased the tool? The vendor? The clinician who deferred? This isn't a hypothetical. A 2023 study published in JAMA Internal Medicine looked at sepsis prediction algorithms deployed across multiple hospital systems and found significant rates of both false positives and false negatives, and in interviews, nurses reported feeling uncertain whether to override the system's recommendations. That uncertainty is not a bug that better training will fix. It is a structural problem with putting a machine in the middle of a judgment that a human being needs to own.⏎⏎There is also something I keep coming back to about the economics of all this that the RFI framing mostly skips over. The pitch for AI in healthcare is that it will reduce clinician burnout and improve efficiency. Maybe. But efficiency for whom? Insurers have already started using AI-assisted prior authorization systems, and the practical effect has not been to make authorization faster or fairer for patients. It has been to process denials at a scale and speed that no human review board could match. UnitedHealth's use of an AI model to adjudicate post-acute care claims became the subject of a Senate investigation in 2023 precisely because the denial rates were dramatically higher than what individual medical reviewers had been producing. That is not a coincidence. That is the system working exactly as the people who bought it intended. HHS should be extremely cautious about any acceleration that makes that kind of automated denial more normalized or harder to challenge.⏎⏎I am genuinely not sure what the right answer is for something like AI-assisted radiology reads in a rural hospital with one radiologist covering four counties. I can see the argument there. But that specific use case is quite different from deploying ambient AI documentation tools in every exam room, or letting AI systems flag patients as high or low utilization risk, or using predictive models to guide staffing decisions. The RFI treats all of these as part of one big "AI adoption" question, but they are not the same thing and they should not be evaluated by the same framework.⏎⏎What I would ask HHS to do, at minimum, is require that any AI system used in a clinical or coverage decision context be subject to mandatory adverse event reporting the same way a medical device is. Right now there is no systematic way to know when an AI tool contributed to a bad outcome. Hospitals are not reporting it. Vendors certainly aren't volunteering it. And without that data, any claim that these tools are safe and effective in real-world deployment is just not verifiable. The FDA's Digital Health Center of Excellence has been working on this for years and still hasn't produced a workable post-market surveillance framework for AI-based clinical decision support. Accelerating adoption before that infrastructure exists seems like exactly the wrong sequence.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0042♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Adams♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Riverside University Health System in Moreno Valley in 2022, and what I remember most is not any particular test or procedure. It's the pulmonologist who came in on a Sunday morning, sat down without being asked, and spent forty minutes with her while she cried and tried to explain what she was afraid of. He wasn't consulting a dashboard. He was listening to a frightened 71-year-old woman who couldn't always find the right words. I don't think an AI system, no matter how well it performs on benchmark datasets, would have known what to do in that room.⏎⏎That's where I keep getting stuck when I read about accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. The framing assumes the problem is speed and efficiency, that medicine is slowed down by human bottlenecks that algorithms can clear. But a lot of what makes care work isn't a bottleneck. It's the thing itself.⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the question HHS is asking about what actions the Department can take to support AI adoption in clinical settings, because I think the question is being asked backwards. Before asking how to accelerate adoption, someone should be asking what happens when it goes wrong and who is accountable. Right now there is no good answer to that. A hospital deploys an AI tool from a vendor, the vendor trained it on data from a different population, a clinician follows the recommendation because it appears in their workflow and they're already seeing 22 patients that day, and the outcome is bad. Who is responsible? The clinician who deferred? The hospital that deployed without adequate validation? The vendor who licensed the product? In practice, all three point at each other and the patient or their family is left with nothing but a malpractice case that will take years to resolve.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem, and I don't think HHS is taking it seriously enough in how this RFI is framed. AI systems trained on historical clinical data will reproduce historical clinical inequities. A model trained mostly on data from well-resourced academic medical centers is going to perform differently for patients at a rural safety-net hospital in Mississippi or a tribal health clinic in South Dakota. "Performs well on average" is not a safety standard that protects anyone specific.⏎⏎What I'd actually want HHS to consider is a moratorium on using AI in prior authorization and coverage denial decisions until there is real independent auditing of those systems. Cigna made headlines in 2023 for a process in which reviewers were reportedly spending an average of 1.2 seconds per denial. That's not AI yet, but it's the same logic, treating human judgment as an inefficiency to be optimized away. Adding AI to that process doesn't fix the problem, it entrenches it and makes it harder to challenge because now there's a model involved and the model is proprietary.⏎⏎I'm not someone who thinks technology is bad. I use electronic health records every day in my work as a medical interpreter and I've seen them help. But there's a difference between tools that support clinical relationships and tools that replace or override them. The push to "accelerate adoption" worries me because acceleration in medicine usually means someone somewhere decided the risk of going slow costs more than the risk of going fast, and that calculation is almost never made by the patient.⏎⏎The RFI asks about reimbursement mechanisms to support AI use. My honest reaction to that is: we don't have enough reimbursement for adequate interpreter services, for home health aides, for mental health visits, for primary care in underserved areas. The idea that CMS should be figuring out how to pay for AI tools before those gaps are filled is♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0043♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Wilson♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔Deloitte Health Sciences♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February of 2023, and I watched what it looks like when medicine works the way it's supposed to. Her pulmonologist, Dr. Anand, sat with our family for forty minutes on a Tuesday night explaining why he was overriding what the monitoring software was flagging. He knew her. He knew she'd had an atypical presentation of everything her whole life. He made a call that the numbers alone would not have supported. She walked out of that hospital. I don't know what happens in that same room if the institutional pressure is to defer to the algorithmic recommendation, if the doctor feels he needs a documented reason to go against the tool, if the tool's output goes into the chart as a kind of soft authority that shapes every subsequent decision.⏎⏎That's the thing I keep coming back to when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. The framing of "acceleration" worries me more than almost anything else in this request. The question is not whether AI can do impressive things in controlled settings. The question is what gets lost in the spaces between the data points, and whether we're moving fast enough to even notice what we're losing before it's gone.⏎⏎I want to address specifically the question of what regulatory or reimbursement actions HHS might take to speed adoption. My honest view is that the current reimbursement incentives are already distorted in ways that favor technology purchases over staffing, and adding AI reimbursement pathways on top of that will make things worse, not better. Hospitals are not neutral actors here. They are under enormous financial pressure and they will use AI tools in whatever way reduces labor costs fastest. That is not a cynical reading, it's just what the incentive structure produces. We already saw this with the rollout of Prior Authorizations automation, where insurers deployed algorithmic denial systems, and reporting from ProPublica and others showed denial rates increasing sharply at certain payers almost immediately after those systems were deployed. The AI didn't improve care. It improved throughput on the denial side of the ledger.⏎⏎I'm also genuinely uncertain about who bears liability when an AI recommendation contributes to a bad outcome. I've asked this question to three different people who work in health policy and gotten three different non-answers. That ambiguity is not a minor regulatory gap. If the clinician defers to the tool and the patient is harmed, is the clinician liable? The hospital? The vendor? If there's no clear answer, then the tool effectively operates without accountable oversight, which is a strange thing to be accelerating into one of the most high-stakes domains in human life.⏎⏎There's also the question of what this does to training. If residents and nurses and physicians assistants start their careers in environments where AI is generating the first-pass assessment, the differential, the documentation summary, do they develop the same diagnostic instincts? I genuinely don't know the answer to that but I think it should be studied seriously before we normalize these workflows, not after.⏎⏎HHS should be asking what guardrails need to be in place before broader adoption, not how to remove barriers to adoption. The two questions lead to very different regulatory postures and I'd argue the second one is the responsible starting point given where we actually are with clinical AI validation right now. Most of these tools have been validated on narrow retrospective datasets and then deployed into much messier real-world conditions. The FDA's own 510(k) pathway for software as a medical device has been criticized repeatedly for not requiring prospective clinical evidence before clearance, and that's the regulatory environment we're being asked to accelerate into.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0044♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Torres♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔American Hospital Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento for six weeks in 2019 after a series of strokes, and what kept her alive and what kept our family sane was not any algorithm. It was a night nurse named Dolores who noticed that my mother's left hand was doing something unusual and flagged it to the neurologist before the next event happened. That is not a story about data. That is a story about a human being paying attention in a way that no system trained on labeled outcomes would have caught, because nobody had ever labeled what Dolores saw as a warning sign. I keep coming back to that when I read materials like this one, which seems to assume that the main barrier to better healthcare is insufficient automation.⏎⏎The RFI asks what regulatory actions HHS can take to accelerate AI adoption, and I want to respond to that framing directly because I think the framing itself is the problem. Accelerating adoption treats speed as the goal. But medicine has historically been very cautious about adopting new interventions, and for good reason. We run clinical trials. We require evidence. We have post-market surveillance. We do not simply deploy something because it performs well in a demonstration or because a hospital CFO sees a cost reduction opportunity. The fact that AI tools are being discussed under an "acceleration" framework rather than an evidence and safety framework tells me that this process is being driven more by industry enthusiasm than by patient welfare.⏎⏎There are real documented cases of AI tools causing harm. The sepsis prediction algorithm deployed at several major health systems, including one widely reported situation involving the Epic Sepsis Model around 2021, was shown in a study published in JAMA Internal Medicine to have substantially lower sensitivity than what had been claimed, and clinicians reported that it generated so many alerts that staff began ignoring them. That is not a success story that needs to be accelerated. That is a cautionary tale about what happens when you move fast in clinical settings.⏎⏎What concerns me about the insurance and administrative side is actually just as serious, maybe more so because it gets less attention. When insurers use AI to make prior authorization decisions, the practical effect is that a system trained on historical claims data is making coverage judgments that affect real people's access to care. And when something goes wrong, who is responsible? The insurer says the algorithm flagged it. The vendor says the client configured it wrong. The patient has no idea what happened or how to appeal it. I have seen this play out with a family member's coverage denial in 2022 and the appeals process was genuinely baffling. Nobody could explain what triggered the denial. That is not a hypothetical concern.⏎⏎I am not saying technology has no place in medicine. I use a continuous glucose monitor and I think it has genuinely helped me. But there is a difference between a device that gives me information about my own body and a system that is making or shaping clinical decisions for a population, at scale, in ways that individual patients and clinicians cannot see or contest.⏎⏎The workforce question is also being treated too lightly here. The argument that AI will reduce clinician burnout sounds good but it also could mean fewer nurses, fewer medical coders, fewer people in roles that currently require human judgment and provide middle-class employment in communities across the country. Those jobs are not just economic abstractions. They are also part of the care infrastructure. When you replace a medical coder with an algorithm you lose someone who knew how to call a physician's office and get clarification on a confusing note. That local knowledge disappears.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require prospective clinical validation in the specific care settings where a tool will be deployed before any federal reimbursement or regulatory pathway facilitates its use. Validation done in academic medical centers should not be assumed to transfer to rural critical access hospitals or federally qualified health centers. And any AI tool used in prior authorization decisions should be required to produce an explanation that a layperson can actually understand, with a clear human point of contact for appeals, not just a general customer service number that routes to another algorithm.⏎⏎I would also ask HHS to consider funding independent audits of tools that are already deployed rather than spending resources on accelerating the deployment of new ones. We do not actually have a good picture of what is already in use, how it is performing, or where it is failing quietly.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0045♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Sanchez♔♔California♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023, and what I keep thinking about when I read discussions like this one is how many times the nurses caught things that were not in any chart. A medication interaction that the system flagged as low priority. The way she was holding her left arm. The fact that she kept asking for her sister, who had died in 1987, which turned out to be a sign of something the attending needed to know about immediately. None of that was in the data. None of it would have been findable by a pattern-matching tool trained on discharge summaries and lab values.⏎⏎I want to address the question this RFI asks about what barriers HHS should try to remove to accelerate AI adoption, because my honest answer is that the question itself assumes the conclusion. The premise seems to be that adoption is good and slowness is a problem to be solved. But there is another way to read the current pace of adoption, which is that clinicians and patients are appropriately cautious, and that this caution is itself a form of safety signal that regulators should pay attention to rather than try to engineer away.⏎⏎The safety concerns here are not hypothetical. There's been documented work showing that some commonly used clinical algorithms, including the sepsis prediction tools that became widespread around 2020 and 2021, generated so many false positives in real hospital settings that nursing staff started ignoring the alerts entirely. That is a known failure mode. The tool gets embedded, the alert fatigue builds up, and eventually the system that was supposed to save lives becomes background noise. And that's before you even get into the question of whether the underlying training data encoded racial or socioeconomic disparities that the developers either didn't notice or disclosed in ways that didn't reach the bedside.⏎⏎What I'm more worried about than any single bad tool is the structural shift that happens when AI recommendations become the default. Once a system is in place, the human clinician who disagrees with it has to actively justify that disagreement. The burden of proof flips. And in a busy emergency department or an understaffed rural clinic, how often does someone have the time or the standing to push back? This is especially true for newer clinicians, who may not have the experience to know when to trust their gut over a confidence score. The organizational pressure to defer to the tool will be real and it will be quiet and nobody will be able to point to the moment it started.⏎⏎I also think the employment question is being handled very carefully by everyone who stands to profit from AI adoption and very carelessly by everyone else. The argument that AI will free clinicians from documentation burdens so they can spend more time with patients sounds good. But the same tools that reduce documentation time also make it easier to quantify exactly how many patients a provider sees per hour, which makes it easier to argue that you need fewer providers. That argument has already been made explicitly in some insurance contexts, where automated prior authorization systems have been used to process denials at a scale that would be impossible with human reviewers. UnitedHealth's subsidiary, Navihealth, faced litigation in 2023 specifically over allegations that an AI tool was being used to override physician judgments about post-acute care in ways that cut off coverage prematurely. That is not a distant hypothetical.⏎⏎HHS should be asking harder questions about accountability before it asks about acceleration. When an AI-assisted diagnosis is wrong, who is the patient supposed to sue? The clinician who followed the recommendation? The hospital that deployed the tool? The vendor who sold it and whose model weights are proprietary? The answer right now is genuinely unclear, and that ambiguity is not a technical problem waiting to be solved by better documentation standards. It reflects something real about what happens to responsibility when you distribute decision-making across a system that no single person fully understands or controls.⏎⏎I don't have a clean regulatory proposal here. I'm not a lawyer or a policy expert. But I do think HHS should be very careful about treating "removing barriers to adoption" as self-evidently good, because some of those barriers are there for reasons that took a long time to learn and could take even longer to relearn if we get this wrong.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0046♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Nguyen♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔Humana♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Piedmont Atlanta Hospital in February 2023, and I watched her care team make decisions that no algorithm could have made. Her blood pressure numbers looked stable on paper but her nurse noticed she was gripping the bed rail differently than the day before, and that observation triggered a cascade of interventions that probably saved her life. I keep thinking about that when I read about what HHS is proposing here.⏎⏎The question I want to address is the one about barriers to adoption and what the department can do to remove them. My concern is that you're asking the wrong question. The barriers to AI adoption in clinical settings may not be barriers so much as appropriate friction, the kind of resistance that exists in a field where errors kill people and where judgment built over years of training is genuinely hard to replicate or replace. When a nurse notices something a monitor doesn't catch, that is not a workflow inefficiency to be engineered away.⏎⏎There is a real difference between using software to schedule appointments or process billing codes and using it to influence what a clinician does at the bedside, or worse, what an insurer decides to cover. I worry that HHS is treating these as the same category of thing. The administrative AI use cases get bundled together with clinical decision support and even diagnostic tools, and the whole package gets framed as acceleration. But the risks are completely different. An AI that makes a scheduling error is annoying. An AI that flags a prior authorization denial, or recommends against a particular treatment based on statistical patterns drawn from historically biased datasets, can hurt or kill the wrong patient.⏎⏎On the bias issue specifically: it's not hypothetical. There is published research going back to at least 2019 showing that widely used risk-scoring algorithms in American hospitals systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because they used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients had historically been denied access to care and therefore had lower costs not because they were healthier but because they had been underserved. If a system like that had been treated as authoritative, and in some places it was, the errors would have compounded over time while appearing statistically reasonable in aggregate.⏎⏎What I would ask HHS to consider is that removing barriers to AI adoption and building adequate safeguards are not the same project and may actually be in tension with each other. The institutions most eager to adopt AI quickly are often the ones with the most financial incentive to use it for cost reduction rather than care improvement. Staffing ratios get cut. Nurses get told the AI will catch what they might miss. The liability question is a mess because when something goes wrong nobody can quite explain whose fault it is, the vendor blames the implementation, the hospital blames the clinician, the clinician says they were just following the recommendation. That diffusion of accountability is genuinely scary to me, not in an abstract way but in the very concrete way of thinking about someone lying in a hospital bed and trying to figure out who is actually responsible for their care.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the employment angle even though it's maybe not the primary focus of this RFI. Healthcare workers, including nurses, medical coders, radiology technicians, and a lot of other people with specialized training, are looking at this moment with real anxiety. The argument that AI will free them from tedious tasks so they can focus on human connection has been made about every wave of workplace automation and it has a pretty poor track record. What usually happens is the tedious tasks go away along with some of the jobs, and the remaining workers are expected to supervise the machines on top of everything else they already do.⏎⏎The comment period closing date for this RFI doesn't give nearly enough time for the kind of input that would actually be useful, and I'm not sure a comment like mine, from someone who's not a vendor or a health system, will get the same weight as the submissions from companies that have a financial stake in the outcome.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0047♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Sanchez♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that was almost missed entirely. The nurse who caught it wasn't running an algorithm. She noticed my mother looked "wrong" even though her numbers were borderline acceptable. That observation, that human thing that has no clean name, is what I keep thinking about every time I read something about AI being the future of clinical care.⏎⏎HHS is asking specifically about what actions it can take to accelerate AI adoption. I want to answer that directly: the better question is whether acceleration is even the right goal. The framing of the RFI assumes we are behind on something, that there is a destination we are supposed to be reaching faster. But a lot of the pressure to adopt these tools comes from vendors who have a commercial interest in deployment, not from patients or frontline clinicians who are the ones actually bearing the consequences when things go wrong.⏎⏎What worries me most practically is the accountability gap. When an AI-assisted tool contributes to a bad outcome, who is responsible? The clinician who followed its recommendation? The hospital that bought the software? The company that trained the model on data that was already skewed by decades of unequal treatment? Right now I don't think there is a real answer to that question. And I don't think HHS has one either, at least not in this RFI. The Department keeps talking about guardrails and governance frameworks but those things tend to get written after systems are already embedded everywhere and the contracts are already signed.⏎⏎There's also the insurance side of this, which the RFI sort of dances around. AI is already being used by payers to automate prior authorization denials. UnitedHealth's nH Predict tool was the subject of a class action lawsuit in 2023 specifically because the denial rate for post-acute care was allegedly much higher than human reviewers would have produced. That's not a hypothetical risk. That happened. People lost access to rehab care they needed. And the response from the company was essentially that the tool was accurate at a population level, which is not a comfort to the individual patient who got denied.⏎⏎I'm not a Luddite about this. I use electronic records. I think some diagnostic support tools have genuine value in narrow, well-validated settings. But there's a big difference between a specific, audited tool used by a specialist who understands its limits and a broad push to normalize AI across clinical workflows on the theory that speed and scale are goods in themselves.⏎⏎The people who tend to be harmed when healthcare gets optimized for efficiency are not the people who get to testify at hearings or submit polished comments with legal citations. They are elderly patients in understaffed nursing homes. They are people in rural areas where the AI was trained on data from urban hospital systems that look nothing like their situation. They are patients who can't clearly describe their symptoms because English isn't their first language or because they have cognitive decline or because they are just scared and not performing their illness in the way the training data expected.⏎⏎HHS has real power here. It controls reimbursement. It controls certification. It could decide that AI tools used in clinical decisions need independent clinical validation before any Medicare or Medicaid funds flow through systems that rely on them. It could require that any AI-generated recommendation be disclosed to the patient as such. It could prohibit the use of AI-generated denial rationales in coverage decisions without a human reviewer who is actually accountable by name. Those would be meaningful steps. Instead the RFI reads like the Department has already decided the direction and is just gathering input to fill out the record before moving ahead.⏎⏎I don't know exactly where the line is. I'm genuinely uncertain about some of this. But the default should be caution, not speed, and right now the structure of this RFI doesn't reflect that.⏎⏎One more thing I keep coming back to: the comment period for this RFI is 60 days, which sounds like a lot until you realize that most of the organizations that will submit detailed technical responses have teams of people whose job it is to do exactly that. Individual patients and nurses and social workers are submitting comments like this one in the evenings after work.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0048♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Sanchez♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔Autism Society of America♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Dayton in February 2023, and I watched what it actually looks like when a human clinician figures out what's wrong with someone. It's not pattern matching. Her attending physician, Dr. Calloway, sat with her for forty minutes one afternoon asking questions that had nothing to do with her chart. He asked about her sleep, her appetite over the past year, what her house smelled like. That conversation led to a diagnosis that three prior tests had missed. I don't know how you train an AI on that. I genuinely don't.⏎⏎What concerns me about this RFI is that it's framed almost entirely around acceleration. The question HHS seems to be asking is how do we move faster, how do we reduce friction, how do we get AI into clinical settings more quickly. That framing worries me because it treats adoption speed as the goal rather than patient outcomes. Those are not the same thing.⏎⏎On the question of what regulatory actions HHS might take to support AI adoption in clinical care, my honest answer is that the department should slow down considerably and spend much more time on the liability and accountability questions before doing anything to encourage broader deployment. Right now if an AI system recommends a drug dose and the clinician follows the recommendation and the patient is harmed, it is genuinely unclear who bears responsibility. The clinician can point to the tool. The hospital can say the vendor was responsible for validation. The vendor can say the user deployed it outside the intended clinical context. That chain of diffused responsibility is already showing up in malpractice cases and it will get worse. I don't think HHS can address that through guidance alone. It probably requires legislation, but at minimum the department should not be actively encouraging deployment while that accountability gap remains open.⏎⏎The bias problem is also more serious than the promotional materials from AI vendors tend to suggest. Training datasets in clinical AI frequently reflect decades of unequal care. A 2019 study published in Science found that a widely used algorithm for managing care coordination systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients had historically received less care and therefore generated lower costs. The algorithm wasn't doing anything unusual. It was doing exactly what it was trained to do. The problem was baked into the training data in a way that was genuinely hard to see until someone looked carefully. Deploying more AI faster doesn't solve that problem. It scales it.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the employment question, which I notice tends to get minimized in these conversations. The argument is usually that AI will free up clinicians to do more meaningful work by handling administrative burden. Maybe. But hospitals are not going to absorb the efficiency gains and leave staffing levels unchanged. They will cut positions. Medical coders, prior authorization reviewers, radiology technicians, people who do intake and scheduling, those are real jobs held by real people, many of them women, many of them people of color, many of them without the credentials or resources to easily retrain for different work. The fact that some of those jobs are administrative does not make them unimportant. They are also often the jobs where the human context gets preserved. The person doing intake asks a question the form didn't include. The coder notices something inconsistent in the record. Those small human moments of attention matter in ways that are genuinely hard to quantify.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no legitimate uses in medicine at all. I can see the argument for narrow, well-validated tools in specific contexts, like flagging certain imaging findings for radiologist review where the evidence base is reasonably strong. But that is very different from the broad integration push this RFI seems to be contemplating. There is a real difference between a tool that assists a trained professional who retains full decision authority and a system that is gradually becoming the default source of clinical judgment in institutions that are also cutting staff and increasing patient loads.⏎⏎The pressure to adopt AI in healthcare is coming substantially from vendors who have enormous financial interest in deployment, and from administrators who see cost reduction opportunities, and neither of those groups is the same as the patients and frontline workers who will actually live with the consequences. HHS should be asking harder questions about who benefits from rapid adoption and who bears the risks, and those groups are not evenly distributed.⏎⏎One more thing I want to raise, though I'm not sure exactly where it fits in the RFI's question structure: the informed consent issue. Patients in most settings currently have no reliable way to know whether an AI system was involved in their diagnosis, their treatment recommendation, their insurance authorization, or their care plan. That seems like a basic transparency problem that should be addressed before any further expansion of use, not after. I've asked physicians at two different health systems in the past year whether any of the tools they use to support their decisions were AI-based and both times I got answers that were genuinely uncertain.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0049♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Clark♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔American Thoracic Society♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the hospital for six weeks in 2019 after a series of small strokes, and I spent a lot of that time watching nurses and doctors make judgment calls that no algorithm could have made. One of the hospitalists — Dr. Patel, at St. Luke's in Kansas City — spent forty minutes just talking with her about her living situation and her fear of being placed in memory care. That conversation changed her treatment plan. There was nothing in her chart that would have led a machine to that conversation. I keep thinking about that when I read about HHS pushing AI into clinical care.⏎⏎The question the RFI seems most focused on is what HHS can do to accelerate AI adoption, and I want to argue that the department should slow down instead, or at minimum stop treating acceleration as the obvious goal. Faster is not automatically better in medicine. Medicine has specific reasons for being slow and deliberate, because the costs of errors are not just financial. They fall on people who are already sick and scared and who often have no recourse when something goes wrong.⏎⏎What worries me most is the insurance and prior authorization side of this. There are already documented cases of AI-driven denial systems rejecting claims at rates that would be impossible for human reviewers to sustain. A ProPublica investigation in 2023 looked at how one major insurer was using automated review to reject post-acute care claims in bulk, sometimes in under a second per case. That's not a hypothetical risk. That's already happening. And the pressure HHS seems to be under from the tech industry is to make more of this easier, not less. If HHS accelerates AI adoption without putting serious brakes on the insurance side, the most concrete near-term effect may be more automated denials, not better care.⏎⏎There's also something I'm not sure HHS has fully grappled with, which is what happens to the clinicians themselves. Nursing and medical training is built on the idea that the professional is responsible for the patient. When you introduce a system that generates recommendations and the clinician is expected to either follow them or document why they didn't, you've subtly shifted the burden of proof. Now the human has to justify deviating from the machine. That's an inversion of how medical accountability is supposed to work, and it happens gradually, not all at once, so it's easy to miss.⏎⏎I'm also skeptical of the argument that AI reduces clinician burnout. Maybe for some tasks. But the administrative burden that causes the most burnout isn't the kind that AI handles cleanly. It's the documentation, the insurance fights, the phone calls to pharmacies, the prior auth denials. Those processes are messy and require someone who understands context and can push back. An AI system that drafts notes faster doesn't fix the fact that a nurse practitioner is spending two hours a day on the phone with insurance companies trying to get coverage approved for a patient who needs a medication they've been on for ten years.⏎⏎The RFI asks about what research HHS should support. My answer would be: fund research on what happens when these tools fail, not just when they work. Require post-deployment outcome tracking. Make it mandatory, not voluntary. Right now most AI vendors publish validation studies done in controlled settings on historical data, and then the tool gets deployed in a community hospital in rural Nebraska with completely different patient demographics and nobody tracks what happens next. That's the gap that matters.⏎⏎I don't think I'm anti-technology. I use technology every day. But there are domains where the human relationship is not incidental to the service being provided — it is the service. And I think healthcare is one of them. The efficiency gains from AI are real but they're also unevenly distributed, and the risks are not abstract. They fall hardest on elderly patients who can't navigate a portal, on people with mental illness whose presentations don't fit clean diagnostic categories, on patients who already distrust the healthcare system for very good historical reasons.⏎⏎One more thing I'd add, and I'm not sure where it fits in the formal comment structure: there should be some mechanism for patients to know when AI was involved in a decision about their care and to request a human review. Not just a buried disclosure in a consent form. An actual right, with teeth, that a patient or their family can invoke. I have not seen that addressed anywhere in the current RFI and it seems like the most basic protection you could put in place before any of this scales further.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0050♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Harris♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔Alliance for Aging Research♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that got delayed by almost 36 hours. Part of what happened, according to the attending who finally talked to us honestly, was that an early warning algorithm had flagged her as low-risk based on her vitals at intake, and the nursing staff was stretched thin and leaning on that score. I'm not saying the algorithm killed her — she survived — but I've thought about that a lot since then, and I think about it every time I see another announcement about how AI is going to "transform" healthcare.⏎⏎So when I saw this RFI I wanted to respond specifically to the question about what barriers HHS should remove to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. My answer is: please slow down and think about whether acceleration is actually the right goal here.⏎⏎The framing of this whole request bothers me. It assumes the problem is that AI isn't being adopted fast enough, and that regulatory barriers are getting in the way. But I don't think the evidence supports that framing. What I see instead is an industry that is moving very fast to deploy tools that have not been adequately tested in real-world conditions, in hospitals that are understaffed and under financial pressure, with patients who have no idea what systems are being used to make decisions about their care.⏎⏎The specific thing I want HHS to think about is what happens when these tools fail. Not in the aggregate, not on the benchmark dataset, but for one particular patient on one particular night. When an AI system gives a wrong recommendation, who is responsible? The clinician who followed it? The hospital that deployed it? The vendor who sold it? In my mother's case we never got a straight answer about whose call it was to rely on that score. That's not a technology problem, that's an accountability problem, and adding more AI to more decisions makes it worse not better.⏎⏎I've read some of the arguments about how AI can help with clinician burnout and I understand the appeal. Doctors and nurses are exhausted. I've seen it. But I keep thinking that "reducing burden" can very easily become a justification for thinner staffing and more automation of decisions that genuinely require a human being to stop and think. The economics of this are not neutral. Hospitals and insurers have strong financial incentives to use AI to cut costs, and "AI-assisted care" can be a polite way of saying "fewer nurses per patient."⏎⏎There's also something that doesn't get talked about enough which is the effect on patients who are already disadvantaged. My mother is white, english-speaking, has a daughter who asks a lot of questions, and she still nearly fell through the cracks. What happens to elderly patients with dementia, or people who don't speak English, or people in rural hospitals that bought a cheap system because it was all they could afford, when an algorithm gets their risk score wrong? The training data for most of these systems reflects decades of unequal care delivery. You can't just train a model on biased historical data and expect it to produce equitable outcomes.⏎⏎I'm not asking HHS to ban AI research or say that no tool can ever be useful. I'm asking the agency not to treat the removal of regulatory safeguards as an obvious good. Some of those barriers exist for reasons. The 510(k) process for medical devices, whatever its flaws, at least creates some expectation that a product has been evaluated before it gets used on patients. A lot of AI tools in clinical use right now are not going through that level of scrutiny, and HHS seems to be contemplating making that easier rather than harder.⏎⏎What I'd actually want to see is mandatory disclosure to patients when AI is being used in their care, real pre-deployment validation requirements that include performance on underrepresented subgroups, and clear liability rules so that "the algorithm recommended it" is not a defense. None of those things are in this RFI as far as I can tell, which♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0051♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Sanchez♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in 2019 after a misdiagnosed pulmonary embolism, and what saved her was not a decision support tool. It was a night-shift nurse who noticed she was holding her left arm differently and flagged it to the attending. That kind of observation doesn't get captured in a training dataset.⏎⏎I want to focus my comments on the question of what HHS describes as "accelerating adoption" and what that framing gets wrong from the start. The word accelerating is doing a lot of work here. It presupposes that speed is good, that the main obstacle to AI in clinical settings is regulatory friction rather than unresolved questions about whether these tools actually help patients. That framing worries me more than any specific provision.⏎⏎Here is what I keep coming back to: medicine is not primarily an information-matching problem. It never has been. A patient who comes in describing chest tightness might be having a cardiac event, or might be terrified about a custody hearing happening tomorrow, or might be describing something she has already been told twice is anxiety and is now scared to bring up again. The clinician who picks up on that context is doing something that I do not believe current AI systems can replicate, and more importantly, something that gets crowded out when clinicians are busy managing AI-generated alerts, documentation tools, and recommendation queues. There is real evidence that alert fatigue from existing clinical decision support systems has led to missed warnings. Adding more AI-generated signals does not obviously solve that problem.⏎⏎The bias question also deserves more direct attention than this RFI gives it. Algorithms trained on historical clinical data will inherit whatever treatment disparities existed in that data. A 2019 study published in Science found that a widely used commercial health algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it used cost as a proxy for need, and cost reflected unequal access rather than actual illness burden. That algorithm was already deployed at scale before the problem was identified. HHS is now asking how to accelerate deployment of similar tools. I would ask instead: what is the post-market surveillance infrastructure that would catch something like that in the future, and does it currently exist? I don't think it does, not in any robust form.⏎⏎The employment question is one I don't see addressed clearly in the RFI at all. Administrative staff in hospitals and clinics, medical coders, prior authorization reviewers, scheduling coordinators, these are not highly paid positions but they are real jobs held by real people, many of them women, many of them in communities that don't have a lot of other options. When insurers and hospital systems talk about using AI to "streamline" administrative functions, they mean replacing those workers. I am not opposed to efficiency in principle but I think the people whose jobs disappear deserve more than a sentence about workforce transition in a federal RFI.⏎⏎What I would ask HHS to do, specifically and concretely, is to impose a mandatory pre-deployment validation requirement for any AI tool used in clinical decision-making, including prior authorization, with public disclosure of performance data broken down by race, age, disability status, and insurance type. Not voluntary disclosure. Required. And I would ask for a moratorium on AI-assisted prior authorization denials until that infrastructure exists, because right now patients are being denied care by systems that nobody can fully audit and nobody is clearly responsible for when they get it wrong.⏎⏎I realize some of this sounds like I'm opposed to technology generally. I'm not. I use the patient portal, I think telehealth expanded access in important ways. But there is a difference between tools that give patients and clinicians more capability and tools that shift decision-making into systems that are opaque, hard to challenge, and ultimately owned by vendors whose interests are not the same as patients' interests. The RFI keeps describing AI as something that can be "responsibly adopted" but the burden of demonstrating that responsibility should be on the deploying institution and the vendor before deployment, not reconstructed after harm occurs.⏎⏎One more thing that I couldn't find addressed anywhere in the RFI: who is liable when an AI tool contributes to a diagnostic error? The clinician who followed its recommendation? The hospital that licensed it? The vendor who sold it? Because right now the contracts I've seen, and I've seen a few through my work in patient advocacy, typically include vendor indemnification clauses that push all liability back onto the provider. That needs to be addressed before any acceleration happens.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0052♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Rivera♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Regions Hospital in St. Paul in February of 2023, and during that time the thing that actually kept her alive — I believe this genuinely — was a night nurse named Doris who noticed something was off about how my mother was breathing and called the attending at 2am rather than waiting for the morning rounds. No algorithm flagged that. No dashboard. A person who had been doing this work for twenty-some years looked at another person and knew something was wrong. I keep thinking about what would have happened if Doris had been staring at a screen full of AI-generated risk scores instead of at my mother's face.⏎⏎I want to focus my comments on what I understand to be HHS's interest in accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings, and specifically the question of how AI can support clinical decision-making. My concern is that HHS is framing this almost entirely as a question of how to speed things up, not whether to, and I think that framing is doing a lot of work that should be examined more carefully.⏎⏎Medicine is not just an information problem. I know that sounds obvious but I'm not sure the people designing these systems actually believe it. A patient who comes in confused and frightened and unable to clearly describe their symptoms is not presenting the AI with bad data — they're presenting a clinician with a human being who needs to be read, not processed. The clinician's job in that moment involves body language, tone, family context, the way someone holds their arm, things that are not in the record and may never be. I'm skeptical that AI systems can capture that and I'm very skeptical that deploying them at scale won't quietly push clinicians toward treating the AI output as the thing they're responsible for rather than the patient.⏎⏎There's also an employment and power issue here that I don't think gets enough attention. When hospital systems and insurers start using AI to make or support decisions — and this includes prior authorization, which is already being automated in ways that are frankly alarming — the people who lose authority are the ones who were already closest to patients. Bedside nurses, social workers, community health workers. The people who gain authority are administrators and vendors. That's not speculation, that's already happening. UnitedHealthcare's use of an AI model called nH Predict to deny post-acute care claims was documented in reporting from late 2023, and a lawsuit alleged the system had a 90 percent error rate on appeal. That is the direction this goes when you move fast and the incentive structure is cost reduction.⏎⏎I want HHS to at minimum require that any AI used in clinical or coverage decisions be subject to independent validation before deployment, not validation by the vendor and not just once at launch but ongoing, because these systems drift. And I want to see serious attention paid to the question of who is accountable when an AI-assisted decision harms a patient. Right now the answer is essentially nobody. The clinician says they followed the tool's guidance, the hospital says they followed the vendor's instructions, the vendor says the clinician overrode their recommendation, and the patient or their family is left with a injury and no clear path to accountability.⏎⏎I understand there are genuine benefits being claimed here and I'm not saying technology has no place in medicine. But the RFI as written seems to assume the answer and ask only how to get there faster. There is no question that asks whether clinical AI is being oversold, whether deployment is outpacing evidence, or whether the communities most affected by algorithmic bias have been meaningfully consulted. The absence of those questions is itself a policy choice, and I'd ask HHS to reconsider it.⏎⏎Also, as a practical matter, who is going to have access to these tools? Rural critical access hospitals running on thin margins with overworked staff and aging IT infrastructure are not going to implement these systems the same way a large academic medical center in Boston will, and the assumption that accelerating adoption helps everyone equally doesn't hold up.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0053♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul♔Brown♔♔Kansas♔♔United States♔Evolent Health♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023 after a sepsis complication following routine gallbladder surgery. The nurses and the attending physician on her floor knew her. They knew she was anxious, that she had a history of underreporting pain because she grew up in a household where complaining wasn't allowed, that her oxygen numbers looked okay on paper but she wasn't okay. A monitor wouldn't have known that. No algorithm would have flagged it. Her nurse flagged it, at 2am, because she walked in and something felt wrong to her. I think about that a lot when I read materials like this one talking about accelerating AI adoption in clinical care.⏎⏎I want to address specifically the questions in this RFI about what HHS can do to support AI adoption in clinical settings, because my answer is: less than you're planning to, and more slowly than you want to.⏎⏎Medicine works because a human being is accountable to another human being. That's not sentiment. When my mother's condition deteriorated, there was a doctor whose name I knew, whose face I knew, who had to look us in the eye and explain what was happening and why. If an AI system had flagged or failed to flag something in that situation, who answers for it? The hospital points to the vendor. The vendor says the clinician should have overridden it. The clinician says the protocol required them to defer to the system's recommendation. I've read enough about how these liability questions actually play out in practice to know that nobody ends up being responsible. That diffusion of accountability is genuinely dangerous in ways that I don't think HHS is reckoning with seriously enough in this RFI.⏎⏎There's also the question of what happens to the data AI systems are trained on. Healthcare data in the United States reflects decades of unequal access and unequal treatment. Black patients have historically been undertreated for pain. Low-income patients have historically received less diagnostic workup for the same symptoms. Elderly patients get dismissed. If you train a system on that history, you're not getting an objective tool, you're codifying the biases of the past and giving them an authoritative-sounding output. And the problem is that when a human clinician is biased, you can sometimes identify it, confront it, work against it. When a system is biased, it looks like math.⏎⏎I'm also genuinely worried about what AI adoption means for the workforce, and I think this RFI is too optimistic about the "burnout reduction" framing. Every hospital administrator I've ever spoken to is primarily interested in cost. If AI can be used to justify having two nurses do the work of three, or to automate the prior authorization process in ways that make denials faster and harder to appeal, that's what it will be used for. The vendor materials will say "efficiency" and "support." What it will mean on the ground is thinner staffing and less human judgment at the bedside.⏎⏎I recognize there are applications of AI in medicine that seem genuinely useful and I'm not trying to pretend otherwise. Radiology image review where a second pass catches something a tired human missed, that's not obviously bad. But that's very different from embedding AI into clinical decision-making workflows across the board, or using it in insurance coverage determinations, which this RFI also seems to contemplate. Those are decisions that affect whether someone gets treated at all, and they should require a human being who can be held responsible.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require that any AI tool used in a clinical or coverage decision context be independently validated on populations that actually reflect the patients it will be used on, not just the populations that were convenient for training. That validation should be public, updated regularly, and not something the vendor controls. I've seen several AI tools marketed to hospitals where the published validation studies were done on patient populations from academic medical centers in large coastal cities, and those tools are being sold to rural critical access hospitals in places like western Kansas where the patient population is completely different demographically and clinically.⏎⏎The pace of adoption being pushed here feels like it's being driven more by industry enthusiasm than by any demonstrated evidence that patients are actually better off. I'd want to see HHS require post-deployment outcome tracking with real consequences for tools that don't perform as advertised, not just self-reported metrics from the institutions that have a financial interest in saying everything is going fine.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0054♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Clark♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Philadelphia in February 2023, and what I remember most is not the equipment or the medications but the pulmonologist who came in every morning, sat down, and actually talked to us. He knew her history not because a screen told him but because he had read her chart, spoken to her cardiologist, and listened to my father describe how her breathing had changed over the previous six months. I don't know how you build a machine that does that. I genuinely don't.⏎⏎What bothers me about this RFI is that the questions seem to start from the assumption that more AI in clinical care is obviously good, and the only real task is figuring out how to get there faster. The question about "barriers to adoption" treats skepticism as an obstacle to be removed rather than as a legitimate professional and moral position. Some of us think the barriers exist for reasons.⏎⏎The safety argument against rushing this keeps me up at night more than anything else. We already know that several widely used clinical algorithms embedded in hospital software were trained on data that systematically underrepresented Black patients, and the result was that Black patients in pain were scored as needing less intervention than white patients with the same condition. That wasn't a fringe finding, that was published in Science in 2019 by Obermeyer and colleagues and it affected tens of millions of patients. The response from the healthcare industry was not to slow down and audit everything else. It was basically to patch that one tool and keep moving. I have no confidence that accelerating AI adoption will go any differently. The systems that have bias baked in don't advertise it. They look authoritative. Clinicians defer to them because saying the algorithm is wrong feels like saying you know better than the data, and most people don't want to be that person, especially under time pressure in a busy ED.⏎⏎And then there is the insurance and prior authorization piece, which I think gets glossed over when people talk about AI in "clinical care" specifically. Prior authorization decisions are already being partially automated by vendors like Evicore and Navihealth, and there have been multiple lawsuits alleging that denial rates went up sharply when algorithmic review replaced human review. A STAT News investigation in 2023 found that one major insurer's AI tool denied claims at a rate that its own medical directors later acknowledged they could not individually review. That is not clinical care being improved. That is clinical care being overridden by a cost-cutting mechanism wearing a technical credential.⏎⏎The question I'd want HHS to actually answer before any of this moves forward is: who is legally responsible when an AI recommendation contributes to a bad outcome? Right now the answer is genuinely unclear, and I have talked to nurses who feel enormous pressure to follow what the system recommends because deviating from it without documentation creates liability exposure for them personally. So the clinician's judgment gets squeezed from both directions: the AI says one thing, the documentation requirements say cover yourself, and the patient in front of them is something more complicated than either.⏎⏎I don't think technology is the enemy. I use electronic health records, I think telemedicine expanded access meaningfully during the pandemic. But there's a difference between tools that support a clinician's judgment and systems that are positioned to replace or second-guess it at scale. The reimbursement incentives HHS controls could easily push institutions toward the second category, and I am not sure the people making these decisions have really sat with what that means for the patient who doesn't fit the model.⏎⏎The 2019 Obermeyer finding is from more than five years ago now and I'm not sure how many hospitals have actually audited their own systems against it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0055♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Lopez♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔Accenture Federal Services♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was diagnosed with early-stage ovarian cancer in February 2023 at a regional hospital in western Pennsylvania, and what saved her — I genuinely believe this — was a gynecologic oncologist who noticed something off about how she was describing her fatigue. Not in her chart. Not in her labs. In the way she talked. That doctor spent forty minutes with her on a Tuesday afternoon asking about things that had nothing to do with the clinical indicators. I think about that a lot when I read about AI being positioned as a solution to diagnostic gaps.⏎⏎The question I want to address here is the one about what barriers HHS should remove to accelerate clinical AI adoption, because my answer is that this framing gets things backwards. The question assumes the barriers are the problem. I think the barriers are often doing important work, and removing them without a much more rigorous public debate is a mistake we will not be able to undo easily.⏎⏎Medicine is not primarily an information retrieval problem. Patients lie, minimize, exaggerate, and contradict themselves. They're scared. They don't always know what's relevant. A physician sitting across from someone is doing something that can't be cleanly separated into a list of inputs and outputs — she's reading a person. An AI system trained on historical EHR data is, at best, finding patterns in what previous clinicians decided to write down and code correctly, which is already a filtered and imperfect record of what actually happened in those rooms. The biases baked into those records — racial, socioeconomic, gendered — don't disappear when you run a model on top of them. They get laundered. They get dressed up in the language of statistical confidence.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the insurance and prior authorization context specifically because I notice the RFI touches on administrative functions. There is already documented evidence — from ProPublica's reporting on Cigna's algorithm-based denials and from the Senate Finance Committee's 2023 investigation into Medicare Advantage — that AI tools are being used not to improve care but to generate faster denials with a veneer of clinical legitimacy. If HHS removes barriers to AI adoption broadly without first establishing hard limits on how AI can be used in coverage determinations, you are going to accelerate exactly that. Hospitals will be told their AI-assisted documentation is more efficient. Insurers will quietly expand algorithm-driven denials. Patients will have less recourse than they do now, not more, because the decision will appear to have come from a process rather than a person.⏎⏎The accountability issue is the one that worries me the most, honestly. When something goes wrong with an AI-assisted diagnosis or an AI-generated treatment recommendation, who is responsible? The clinician who followed the suggestion? The hospital that deployed the tool? The vendor who trained the model on data they won't fully disclose? Right now there is no satisfying answer to that question, and I do not think HHS should be in the business of normalizing the technology before that answer exists. Once these systems are embedded in workflows, once hospitals have restructured their staffing around the assumption that the AI is handling certain tasks, reversing course becomes politically and economically difficult even if the evidence of harm is clear.⏎⏎I am not opposed to technology in medicine as a general matter. I understand that there are narrow applications — certain kinds of image analysis, medication interaction flags — where the case is more straightforward. But the RFI as written seems to treat AI adoption as a goal in itself, and I think that's the wrong orientation. The goal is better patient outcomes. Sometimes technology serves that. Sometimes it gets in the way by creating the illusion of thoroughness while actually thinning out the human attention that patients need.⏎⏎There are nursing homes in rural areas right now being pitched AI monitoring systems as a substitute for adequate staffing ratios. That is not a hypothetical. CMS should be aware of at least several active pilots along those lines in facilities that are already understaffed by any reasonable standard, and the pitch to administrators is almost always framed in terms of cost savings rather than care quality.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0056♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Rivera♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Emory University Hospital in Atlanta in March of 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that her primary care doctor had initially missed. What saved her, eventually, was a hospitalist who sat with our family for forty minutes, listened to everything we described about how she'd been acting at home in the weeks before, and put together a picture that none of the labs or scans had shown clearly on their own. That doctor used judgment. He used context. He used the fact that my mother kept saying "something feels wrong" even when she couldn't describe it. I don't know how you feed that into a model.⏎⏎The question I want to address in this comment is the one HHS is asking about what actions the department can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. My answer is: please slow down, and think harder about what you might be destroying in the process of trying to modernize.⏎⏎Medicine is not primarily an information problem. I know that's probably an unpopular thing to say to a technology-focused office, but I think it's true. Patients are scared. They're in pain. They describe their symptoms badly. They leave things out because they're embarrassed or because they don't think a detail is relevant. A good clinician reads the room, reads the patient, reads the family. That is not something that gets captured in an eHR and it's not something an AI system trained on clinical notes is going to learn to do, at least not in any way I would trust with my mother's life.⏎⏎There's also the question of what happens when these systems are wrong. And they will be wrong sometimes - every tool is. But when a human doctor is wrong, there is a person accountable. You can file a complaint, you can sue, you can at least look someone in the eye. When an AI system gives a bad recommendation and a clinician follows it, who is responsible? The vendor will say the clinician should have overridden it. The hospital will say they deployed it according to specifications. The clinician will say the tool told them something different than their instinct and they were trained to trust the tool. I've seen this kind of diffusion of responsibility in other industries and it's genuinely dangerous, but in healthcare the stakes are not a delayed package or a bad credit score. They are permanent injuries and deaths.⏎⏎What worries me most practically is the insurance and prior authorization side. There are already AI systems being used to deny claims automatically, and there have been lawsuits about this - UnitedHealth Group faced significant litigation in 2023 and 2024 over an algorithm called nH Predict that was alleged to be denying post-acute care claims at rates far higher than human reviewers would have approved. If HHS accelerates AI adoption broadly, I think that's the place where the most harm will happen fastest, because insurers have every financial incentive to use AI to say no more efficiently and very little incentive to use it to say yes more accurately.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the workforce piece because I don't think it gets enough attention in these kinds of discussions. There are people who do medical coding, prior auth review, care coordination, and similar work. Many of them have years of experience and genuinely understand the human context behind the cases they handle. Replacing those roles with AI doesn't just eliminate jobs, it eliminates a layer of human judgment that exists in the system right now. Whether that judgment is always good is a separate question. But removing it entirely and replacing it with an optimization algorithm seems like a decision that deserves a lot more public debate than it's getting in this RFI process.⏎⏎I understand HHS is under pressure from multiple directions to show that it's modernizing. I'm not saying AI has zero place in healthcare administration or research. But there's a difference between using computational tools to support human decision-making and using them to replace it, and I think this RFI is oriented too heavily toward acceleration without enough attention to what the brakes are and who gets to use them.⏎⏎One more thing I keep coming back to: once these systems are embedded in clinical workflows and billing systems and insurance platforms, they will be very hard to remove. Vendors will have contracts. Institutions will have built their processes around the tools. The people who knew how to do things the old way will have retired or moved on. Reversibility should be a first-order concern in any policy HHS develops here, and I don't see it mentioned prominently anywhere in the questions being asked.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0057♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Johnson♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔National Council of Asian Pacific Islander Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Piedmont Atlanta Hospital in February 2023, and during that time I watched a rotating cast of hospitalists try to make sense of her records, her medications, her history. It was chaotic and imperfect and deeply human. What I keep thinking about now, reading about this push to put AI into clinical decisions, is whether any of that would have been better if a machine had been involved, or whether it would have just felt more organized while actually being worse.⏎⏎The question HHS seems to be asking is basically: how do we speed this up? And I want to push back on the premise that speed is the right goal here.⏎⏎Medicine is not an information retrieval problem. I know that sounds simple but I think it keeps getting lost in these conversations. When my mother's care team made decisions, they were reading her face, listening to how she described pain, noticing that she got confused and scared in the evenings and needed someone to actually sit with her. None of that is in a dataset. And the worry I have is that AI systems create this impression of rigor, this veneer of objectivity, that makes everyone feel like the hard judgment calls are being handled, when really what's happening is that the messy human stuff is just being ignored because it can't be quantified.⏎⏎There's also the bias issue, which I don't think gets taken seriously enough. These systems are trained on historical data, and historical data in American medicine reflects decades of unequal treatment. Studies going back to at least 2019, including work by Obermeyer et al. published in Science, showed that a widely-used commercial algorithm was systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health, and Black patients had been given less care historically so they cost less. That's not a bug that got fixed and went away. That's the kind of thing that can be baked into any system trained on real-world data, and it can be much harder to spot when the output looks precise and authoritative.⏎⏎I'm also worried about what happens to accountability. Right now, if a doctor makes a wrong call, there is a person who made that call. There is a license, a name, a conversation that can happen. If an AI system flags something incorrectly or misses something, who answers for it? The vendor will say the hospital deployed it wrong. The hospital will say the clinician should have overridden it. The clinician will say they were trained to defer to it. That diffusion of blame is not theoretical, it's already happening in other sectors and there's no reason to think medicine will be different.⏎⏎I understand the argument about burnout. I have heard from nurses who are exhausted, who are doing documentation at midnight, who feel like they spend more time in the EHR than with patients. I don't dismiss that. But I don't think the solution to a broken system is to automate the broken system. The documentation burden exists partly because of how billing and liability work in this country, and an AI that fills out notes faster doesn't fix any of that, it just makes the underlying dysfunction run more efficiently.⏎⏎What I am asking HHS to do, at minimum, is slow down. Require rigorous post-market surveillance before these tools get reimbursed or required. Don't let vendors self-certify. Make hospitals publish their outcomes data broken down by race, age, insurance status, before and after AI implementation. And please, do not let the reimbursement structure push smaller or rural hospitals into buying systems they don't have the technical staff to evaluate or monitor.⏎⏎There is a real risk that "AI-powered care" becomes a marketing phrase that means thinner staffing and more automated denials, and the patients who will suffer most from that are exactly the patients who are already getting the worst care. I keep thinking about what my mother's hospitalization would have looked like if the nurses had been cut because an algorithm was supposed to monitor her vitals remotely, and she had needed someone to actually notice that she was frightened and♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0058♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Jones♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔Association of American Indian Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Pittsburgh in February of 2023, and I watched what good medicine actually looks like up close. It is not pattern matching. Her attending physician, Dr. Reyes, spent forty minutes with our family on a Tuesday night explaining why he was ignoring what the risk-scoring software was suggesting and going with his gut on her fluid management. He was right. She went home. I keep thinking about what happens in that same situation if Dr. Reyes is looking at a screen that says "high confidence recommendation" and feels institutional pressure to follow it.⏎⏎That is the thing nobody in this RFI seems to want to say plainly: AI tools in clinical settings do not just assist judgment, they compete with it. And the competition is not fair because the tool has the aura of objectivity and the clinician has the vulnerability of being second-guessed if they deviate. So even a technically optional AI recommendation becomes practically mandatory in a lot of hospital cultures, especially for younger residents or nurses who don't yet have the standing to push back.⏎⏎HHS is asking about regulatory actions to accelerate adoption. I want to argue for the opposite, or at least for a serious slowdown while we figure out what we're actually doing.⏎⏎The safety case here is not hypothetical. Sepsis prediction algorithms have been deployed at dozens of hospital systems and the evidence on whether they actually reduce mortality is genuinely mixed. The Epic Sepsis Model was studied in a 2021 paper in JAMA Internal Medicine and the findings were pretty damning in terms of both sensitivity and the volume of false positives it generated. That tool was already embedded in workflows at hundreds of institutions before that kind of rigorous external evaluation was done. That is the pattern. Deploy first, study later, and by the time the evidence comes in, the system is so integrated that nobody wants to pull it out. Hospitals have sunk costs in the vendor contract, the staff have been trained around the workflow, and the patients are never told any of this is happening.⏎⏎The question I most want HHS to address, and I don't see it asked directly in the RFI, is what the liability structure looks like when an AI-assisted decision leads to harm. Right now it is genuinely unclear. The clinician signed off, so legally it may fall on them, but they were working within an institutional system that told them to use the tool, and the tool was built by a vendor whose contract almost certainly includes indemnification clauses that shift responsibility back toward the hospital or the user. Patients have no idea this negotiation is happening. And when something goes wrong they have no way of knowing whether the AI played a role because they were never informed it was being used.⏎⏎I am also worried about the administrative side of this, which I think people underestimate. The RFI mentions AI for prior authorization and billing. Insurance companies are already using automated systems to deny claims, and there is substantial evidence, including the congressional investigation into UnitedHealth's nH Predict algorithm that came out in 2023, that these systems are denying care at rates that human reviewers would not match. Calling that "AI in clinical care" might be a stretch but it absolutely affects clinical outcomes. People don't get the rehab they need. They leave the hospital too early. They skip the follow-up MRI. And there is no human being they can look in the eye and demand an explanation from.⏎⏎I understand there are genuine benefits being claimed for AI in radiology screening, in medication reconcilliation, in documentation burden reduction for clinicians. I am not saying none of that is real. But the framing of this RFI, which is about accelerating adoption, assumes that the main obstacle is regulatory friction rather than the possibility that the technology is not yet safe or equitable enough to deploy at scale. That assumption should be questioned before HHS commits to a posture of acceleration.⏎⏎The populations most likely to be harmed by poorly validated AI tools are also the populations least likely to be submitting comments to this docket.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0059♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔White♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔Nuance Communications♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Froedtert Hospital in Milwaukee in October 2022, and during that time I watched a rotating cast of nurses, hospitalists, and specialists piece together what was happening to her not from any single data source but from watching how she held her face when she was in pain, from talking to my father about what she'd eaten the week before, from noticing that she was embarrassed to admit certain symptoms. She recovered. I think about what that process actually looked like and I genuinely cannot map it onto what people mean when they talk about AI-assisted clinical decision making.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to AI adoption in clinical care, and I want to push back on the framing of that question itself. The question assumes that adoption is the goal and that barriers are the problem. But what if slower adoption is actually the appropriate response to genuine uncertainty about whether these tools work safely across the full range of patients who will actually receive care, not just the patients who appeared in whatever training dataset a vendor used?⏎⏎Here is what concerns me most concretely. When an AI system gets something wrong in a clinical setting, who is accountable? This is not a hypothetical. The FDA has cleared hundreds of AI-based clinical decision support tools and the post-market surveillance for many of them is essentially voluntary. A clinician who follows an AI recommendation and the patient is harmed can say they were following the system. The hospital can say the vendor's product was cleared by FDA. The vendor can say the clinician used it outside its validated indication or that the hospital's data environment differed from the training environment. I have read enough about the ProPublica investigation into the Optum risk-scoring algorithm from 2019 to know that these systems can operate at massive scale, shaping care for millions of people, while encoding racial bias that took years to become visible and that the vendor did not voluntarily disclose. By the time the problem was documented publicly the algorithm had already been used by hospitals serving approximately 200 million people. That is not a reason to fine-tune our approach to AI governance. That is a reason to be extremely cautious about embedding these tools deeper into clinical workflows before accountability structures actually exist.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the administrative side because the RFI mentions it and I think people underestimate the harm there. Prior authorization algorithms are already being used by insurers to deny care, and there have been multiple lawsuits and at minimum one Senate investigation into whether those denials are being generated by systems that don't actually review individual cases. When a nurse at a community health clinic in rural Wisconsin spends forty minutes on the phone trying to reverse an AI-generated denial for a patient who needs a home health aide, that is not an efficiency gain. That is the cost of the efficiency gain being externalized onto clinicians and patients who have less power than the insurer. The people who will be harmed most by bad AI in healthcare are not the people who show up in demonstrations of the technology working well.⏎⏎HHS and ONC should require, before any further acceleration of adoption, mandatory prospective performance reporting broken down by race, age, insurance status, and clinical setting for any AI tool used in coverage determinations or clinical decision support. Not voluntary reporting. Not post-market attestation. Actual auditable data, publicly accessible, updated at minimum annually, with clear consequences for tools that show disparate performance.⏎⏎I am genuinely uncertain whether that alone would be enough. It might not be. But the current approach of asking how to speed things up while treating safety and equity as secondary concerns feels backwards to me, and I think the people who will pay the price for moving fast are the same people who already have the hardest time getting good care.⏎⏎One more thing I keep coming back to: the RFI mentions reducing clinician burnout as a rationale for AI adoption. I understand why that argument is appealing. Burnout is real and the consequences are serious. But the causes of burnout include documentation burden, staffing shortages, administrative overhead, and the feeling of losing control over clinical judgement. If the response to those causes is to layer in AI systems that clinicians don't fully understand, that generate recommendations they're expected to oversee but can't always verify, and that may be used by hospital administrators to justify not hiring the additional staff that would actually help, then I am not sure AI adoption addresses burnout so much as it changes its texture while potentially adding new sources of it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0060♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Lee♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔Abbott Laboratories♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Regions Hospital in St. Paul in February 2023, and I was there almost every one of those days. What I remember most is not the monitors or the medications. It's the resident who sat with her at 2am and figured out she was confused not because of her illness but because she was terrified about her cat at home. No algorithm was going to find that. No pattern-matching system was going to notice the way she kept looking toward the window and asking about something that didn't make sense until you understood who she was as a person.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. The framing of this RFI bothers me. "Accelerating adoption" presupposes that faster is better. It doesn't ask whether we should slow down. It doesn't ask what we might lose. It treats the question of AI in medicine as settled in principle and only open in terms of implementation details. That's the wrong starting point.⏎⏎The question I want HHS to actually grapple with is: what happens when AI-generated recommendations become the default, and clinicians stop developing the judgment to override them? There's research on this in aviation, where pilots who rely too heavily on autopilot lose proficiency in manual flight. Medicine is not aviation, and I know that comparison only goes so far, but the basic concern about skill atrophy and over-reliance on systems that look authoritative even when they're wrong seems genuinely applicable. I'm not a researcher. I'm a retired social worker with 28 years of experience working with elderly patients in the Twin Cities metro area, and what I know from that is that the people most likely to be harmed by a system that works well "on average" are the people who have always been outside the average. My clients were poor, many had limited English, many had complicated histories with the medical system going back to experiences with forced institutionalization or neglect. When I think about an AI trained on electronic health records recommending care for someone like Mrs. T., a 79-year-old Hmong woman I worked with who had documented her own symptoms inaccurately for years because she didn't trust the intake process, I genuinely don't know how that goes well.⏎⏎HHS asks specifically about barriers to AI adoption and what actions the department could take to remove them. I want to answer that question honestly: some of those barriers exist for good reasons. Clinician skepticism is not ignorance. Regulatory caution is not obstruction. The fact that we don't yet have reliable, agreed-upon methods for auditing AI tools for bias in real-world clinical settings is not a bureaucratic delay, it's a real gap that will cause real harm if ignored in the rush to scale these systems up. The FDA's process for clearing AI-based clinical decision support tools has been widely criticized, including by people inside the agency, for being too permissive and too slow to catch post-market problems. Clearing a tool based on retrospective data from one hospital system and then deploying it nationally is not a conservative approach to safety. It's a gamble.⏎⏎What I'd actually want to see is the opposite of acceleration. I'd want to see HHS fund independent, post-deployment audits of AI tools currently in use, mandate that vendors disclose the demographic composition of training datasets before any Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement is tied to AI-assisted decisions, and create a formal mechanism for frontline workers, including nurses, medical assistants, social workers, and home health aides, to report concerns about AI recommendations without fear of retaliation. Those workers are the ones who will notice when something is wrong. Right now there's no real infrastructure for capturing that knowledge.⏎⏎I'm also worried about the insurance side of this, which the RFI touches on somewhat obliquely. UnitedHealth Group's use of an AI model called nH Predict to deny post-acute care claims has already been the subject of litigation and a Senate investigation. That situation did not emerge from nowhere. It emerged from a regulatory environment that allowed automated decision-making to expand into consequential coverage decisions without adequate oversight. Speeding up AI adoption without first resolving that situation seems backwards.⏎⏎I don't think technology is the enemy. I've watched electronic records improve coordination of care in ways that genuinely helped my clients. But there's a difference between tools that support human judgment and tools that replace it, and I don't think HHS has been clear enough about which one it's trying to encourage. The RFI language about "augmenting" clinicians sounds reassuring, but augmentation can slide into replacement very quickly once financial incentives are aligned that way, and in American healthcare the financial incentives almost always point toward doing things cheaper and faster with fewer staff.⏎⏎The staffing piece is something I feel strongly about and don't think gets enough attention in these policy conversations. If a hospital can demonstrate that an AI triage tool reduces the need for one RN per shift, there is enormous pressure to make that cut. The fact that the AI might be wrong in ways the nurse would have caught doesn't show up in the quarterly numbers until something goes very bad. And then the question of who is accountable is genuinely unresolved. The nurse who wasn't hired? The vendor who sold the tool? The administrator who decided to rely on it?⏎⏎I'd rather HHS spend the next two years getting those accountability questions right before it does anything to accelerate adoption further.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0061♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Walker♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023 after a series of small strokes, and what I remember most is how much we depended on the night nurses and the attending physician to actually look at her, talk to her, notice that she was frightened and getting more confused, not just read numbers off a screen. One of the nurses caught something the monitors missed because she noticed my mother was holding her breath slightly when she shifted position. That kind of observation is not in a dataset anywhere.⏎⏎So when I see HHS asking how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, my honest reaction is: slow down. Not because I'm against technology in some general way, but because I think the framing of this whole RFI gets the problem backwards. The question isn't how fast we can get AI into hospitals. The question is whether we've even begun to understand what we're giving up when we do.⏎⏎The thing I keep coming back to is accountability. Right now, if a doctor makes a wrong call, there is a person. You can ask that person why. They have to answer to a licensing board, to a family, to their own conscience. When a hospital deploys an AI system to flag deteriorating patients or recommend treatment pathways or, and this is the part that really worries me, help insurers decide what gets approved, where does accountability go? I've read a little about how these systems work and the honest answer seems to be: nowhere clean. The hospital says they followed the vendor's protocol. The vendor says the model performed within specifications. The clinician says they were told to defer to the alert system because liability was structured that way. My mother can't sue an algorithm.⏎⏎I understand there are real problems in healthcare that people hope AI will help with. Physician burnout is real. Administrative burden is real. But I'm skeptical that AI deployment as it's actually happening in practice is primarily about any of that. A lot of it seems to be about cost reduction dressed up in the language of innovation. When United Healthcare was using an AI model to deny post-acute care claims at a rate that internal documents later showed was something like 90 percent denial in certain categories, and their own staff were reportedly overriding it at low rates because of how the workflow was structured, that's not a burnout solution. That's a denial machine with a machine learning label on it.⏎⏎The RFI asks specifically about what regulatory actions might accelerate adoption and I want to answer that directly: I don't think acceleration is the right goal. I think the right goal is making sure that any AI used in a clinical or coverage decision context has been tested in the specific population and setting where it will actually be used, not just validated on a benchmark dataset from a different hospital system in different demographic conditions. I think there should be a clear requirement that patients be told when AI played a role in a decision about their care or coverage. And I think there need to be penalties with teeth, not voluntary guidelines, when a system causes harm.⏎⏎What I don't see in this RFI is much acknowledgment that the people most likely to be harmed by poorly deployed AI are the same people who already get worse care: elderly patients who don't fit the training data well, people with multiple chronic conditions whose cases are genuinely complicated, patients in rural or underserved areas where the hospitals buying these tools may have less capacity to audit whether they're working. A system that performs great on average can still be quietly catastrophic for specific groups, and the catastrophe won't show up in the vendor's press release.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the administrative side because I think it gets overlooked. The argument for AI in scheduling, billing, prior authorization, that kind of thing, sounds more benign than clinical AI, but the people doing those jobs are not just processing paperwork. A good patient services coordinator at a small clinic knows that the elderly man who always reschedules is probably having transportation problems and needs a social work referral. That context doesn't live in a structured field.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0062♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Johnson♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔American Urological Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023, and what got her through it was not an algorithm. It was a night nurse named Patricia who noticed something was off about how she was breathing and called the attending before the monitors flagged anything. That is the kind of judgment you cannot train into a system on historical data, and I think about it every time I read something like this RFI.⏎⏎The question HHS is really asking here is how to accelerate adoption, not whether to. That framing already concedes too much. Before you figure out how to speed something up, you might want to ask whether it should be moving in that direction at all, or at least moving this fast.⏎⏎What bothers me most about the clinical AI push is the safety argument, or rather the way safety gets used to justify adoption rather than slow it down. You will hear constantly that AI catches things doctors miss, that it reduces errors, that it will save lives. Some of that may be true in controlled studies. But healthcare is not a controlled study. A system validated at a large academic medical center in Boston is going to be deployed in a rural critical access hospital in eastern Kentucky with different patient populations, different documentation practices, different staffing ratios, and completely different patterns of incomplete data. The validation does not travel with the tool. The risk does. And when something goes wrong, nobody is quite responsible. The clinician can say the tool recommended it. The hospital can say they implemented it according to vendor specs. The vendor can say it was used outside the intended population. That diffusion of accountability is genuinely dangerous in a context where errors can kill people, and I do not see the RFI grappling with it seriously.⏎⏎There is also a workforce dimension that I think gets brushed aside too quickly. The argument that AI will reduce clinician burnout is plausible in a narrow sense. If AI handles documentation, maybe a physician spends less time typing. Fine. But that same efficiency argument is also used by health systems to justify cutting support staff, to increase patient loads, to reduce the number of humans in the room. A hospital administrator looking at an AI tool does not see a way to give nurses more time with patients. They see a way to run the floor with fewer nurses. I have no way to prove that will happen everywhere but it is already happening in some billing and prior authorization contexts, where automation has not made the process more humane, it has made it faster and colder and harder to appeal.⏎⏎The prior authorization piece is the one I keep coming back to. Insurers are already using algorithmic tools to generate denials at scale, and patients and physicians are fighting those denials with almost no visibility into how the decisions were made. Cigna's 2023 controversy over automated claim reviews was not an isolated incident. If HHS accelerates AI adoption without building in hard requirements for explainability, human review, and real accountability when automated decisions cause harm, you are just handing more power to the entities that already have too much of it.⏎⏎I understand there are potential benefits. I am not saying AI has zero place in medicine. But the question of whether to accelerate adoption in clinical care right now, without stronger safety frameworks, without resolved questions about bias in training data, without clear liability rules, without any serious reckoning with what happens to the workers whose roles get hollowed out, that is a question that deserves a slower and more cautious answer than this RFI seems to be looking for.⏎⏎One more thing I would ask the Department to consider: what does it mean for a patient to consent to AI-assisted care? Right now most people have no idea when an algorithm is influencing their diagnosis, their treatment recommendation, or their insurance decision. That seems like a basic transparency problem that should be solved before we worry about accelerating anything.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0063♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Hernandez♔♔Kansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February of 2023, and I watched her care team make decisions that no algorithm could have made. One of her nurses noticed something was off about how she was breathing even though her numbers looked fine, and pushed for a second look that caught a developing pneumothorax before it became a crisis. That nurse had twenty years of experience reading patients. She wasn't reading a dashboard.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read about how HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings. The framing bothers me. "Accelerate" is doing a lot of work in that sentence. It assumes the direction is correct and the only question is speed. I don't accept that.⏎⏎What concerns me most is the question of what happens when these tools fail. Not in a demo environment. In an actual hospital at 2am with an understaffed floor and a patient who doesn't fit the training data because she's 79 years old, takes fourteen medications, and has a history that was documented across four different health systems with inconsistent records. AI systems that performed well in controlled validation studies have repeatedly shown brittleness when deployed in real clinical environments, and the problem is that the failures are not always obvious. A system can be wrong in ways that look confident. That's worse than being wrong in ways that look uncertain, because clinicians and patients may not push back.⏎⏎There's also the bias question, which I don't think gets taken seriously enough in conversations about "accelerating" adoption. A lot of the historical clinical data that these systems are trained on reflects decades of unequal treatment. Black patients have been systematically undertreated for pain. Women presenting with cardiac symptoms have been misdiagnosed at higher rates than men. If you train a model on that data and then deploy it at scale, you're not just inheriting those biases, you're encoding them and making them harder to see because they're now coming out of a machine that feels objective.⏎⏎I want to say something about the administrative side too, because I think HHS is underestimating how much context gets lost when you automate functions that seem purely clerical. Insurance prior authorization is one example. People think of it as paperwork, but the nurses and case managers who do it understand the patient's situation in ways that a model reviewing structured claims data simply won't. When insurers use AI to automate denials, they're not just making things faster, they're removing people who had actual knowledge of the case from the decision. That matters. Someone's coverage for a home health aide or a specialized drug isn't a bureaucratic abstraction.⏎⏎I understand there are real pressures here. Clinician burnout is genuine. Documentation burden is genuinely terrible. But I am skeptical that AI deployment will actually address those problems rather than just shifting the work or introducing new kinds of burden. There's a difference between tools that help clinicians do their jobs and tools that let administrators reduce headcount while telling clinicians the algorithm has it covered.⏎⏎The RFI asks about what regulatory actions HHS can take to accelerate adoption. I'd ask HHS to consider instead what regulatory actions would ensure that any AI tool deployed in clinical care has been rigorously tested in populations representative of where it will actually be used, with ongoing post-deployment monitoring that is independent of the vendor, and with clear liability frameworks so that when something goes wrong there is an identifiable human being or institution that is legally responsible. Right now those frameworks mostly don't exist or are very underdeveloped.⏎⏎One more thing I keep coming back to: the framing of AI as a solution to healthcare's problems implicitly accepts a view of medicine as primarily an information processing task. I don't think that's right, and I don't think most patients think that's right either. When people are sick and scared they want someone who will be answerable to them, not a system that will route their concerns through♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0064♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Mitchell♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔Veracyte♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that was initially missed on two separate ER visits. Both times, she was sent home. When I asked the hospitalist what had changed on the third visit — what finally caught it — she said a nurse noticed my mother was "just not right" compared to how she'd looked the day before. Not a lab value. Not a scan. A nurse who had seen her enough times to know something had shifted. I keep thinking about that when I read materials like this RFI talking about accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings.⏎⏎The question I'd like HHS to actually sit with, rather than just gather input on, is whether deploying AI tools in clinical care might systematically devalue exactly the kind of judgment that saved my mother's life. I'm not saying it definitely would. But I don't think the Department has seriously grappled with what gets lost when you instrument every workflow, when nurses are spending more time confirming or overriding system flags and less time just watching patients. There is research suggesting that alert fatigue from existing clinical decision support tools already causes clinicians to ignore warnings at very high rates — some studies have put override rates above 90 percent for certain alert categories. If that's already happening with the tools we have, what happens when AI generates even more signals, more recommendations, more documentation prompts?⏎⏎What really worries me is the accountability problem and I don't think the RFI takes it seriously enough. When an AI-assisted decision goes wrong, who is responsible? The clinician who accepted the recommendation? The hospital that licensed the software? The vendor who trained the model? Right now there's no clear answer to that and I suspect the lack of a clear answer is commercially convenient for everyone except the patient. Vendors will write contracts that disclaim liability and hospitals will point to vendors and clinicians will say they were following what the system suggested. I've talked to medical malpractice attorneys who are already dreading this because the causal chains will be extremely difficult to unravel.⏎⏎I also think HHS should be honest about the economic incentives at play here and I say this without assuming bad faith from anyone in particular. AI is being sold to health systems in part on the promise of efficiency gains, reduced headcount, faster throughput. Those aren't necessarily bad things in the abstract. But in practice, if a hospital deploys an AI triage tool and then uses it as justification to reduce nursing staff, and then something goes wrong because the staffing was too thin to catch what the AI missed, that's a patient safety crisis dressed up as a technology success story. The acceleration framing in this RFI concerns me because acceleration tends to mean deployment outpaces evaluation.⏎⏎I'd ask HHS to focus specifically on the question of post-market surveillance for AI tools that are already deployed. The premarket clearance or approval process, whatever pathway FDA uses for a given tool, captures a moment in time. Models drift. Hospital populations change. A sepsis prediction algorithm validated on data from a large academic medical center in 2020 may behave very differently in a rural critical access hospital in 2025 with a different patient mix and different documentation practices. I don't see any robust mechanism right now for detecting that kind of degredation in the field, and the RFI doesn't propose one either.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0065♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Garcia♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔National Organization for Rare Disorders♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the ICU at UPMC Presbyterian in Pittsburgh for eleven days in February of 2023, and I want to talk about what actually happened there before I get into the broader question this RFI is asking.⏎⏎She had a sepsis presentation that was, according to her attending, "textbook" on paper. Her labs looked a certain way, her vitals tracked a certain pattern, and I was told later that if a clinical decision support tool had been running on her chart it would have flagged her as moderate-risk and suggested a standard protocol. What it would not have known is that she had been refusing to eat for three weeks before admission because she was grieving, that she had a history of medication sensitivity that was documented in a different health system entirely and not in the UPMC record, and that she was terrified of needles in a way that made her blood pressure readings unreliable whenever someone came at her with a cuff. The nurse figured that last part out by watching her. Just watching her. That is not something a model trained on retrospective claims data is going to replicate.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read materials like this RFI that talk about "accelerating adoption." Accelerating toward what, exactly? I understand the argument that AI can help overstretched clinicians catch things they might miss. I'm not dismissing that entirely. But the framing of this request concerns me because it is organized almost entirely around removing barriers and speeding things up, and there is almost no acknowledgment that the barriers might sometimes exist for good reasons, or that speed itself can be a form of harm in medicine.⏎⏎The question I want to focus on is the one about what regulatory actions HHS could take to support broader deployment. My honest answer is that before HHS does anything to encourage broader deployment, it should require much more rigorous post-market surveillance of tools that are already deployed. The FDA has cleared hundreds of AI/ML-based devices under 510(k) pathways that involved minimal clinical validation in the populations where the tools are actually being used now. There's a 2021 study from researchers at MIT and Harvard that looked at the demographics represented in training data for FDA-cleared AI diagnostic tools and found severe underrepresentation of Black patients, elderly patients, and patients from rural settings. Those tools are already in hospitals. They are already influencing care. And we are talking about accelerating more of the same.⏎⏎There's also the insurance side of this, which I think gets buried in conversations that focus on clinical AI. Insurers are using algorithmic tools to make prior authorization decisions, and the patients and physicians affected by those decisions often have no meaningful way to know an algorithm was involved, let alone challenge it. I've read about the litigation around UnitedHealth's nH Predict tool, which was allegedly denying post-acute care to elderly patients at rates that exceeded what their own physicians recommended. That case is ongoing as of when I'm writing this. That is not a hypothetical risk. That is a system already operating at scale, already causing harm, and HHS has not meaningfully constrained it.⏎⏎The accountability problem here is real and I don't think HHS has grappled with it seriously. When a physician makes a bad call, there is a named person, a license, a malpractice framework, a peer review process. When an algorithm makes a bad call, the hospital says they were following the vendor's recommendations, the vendor says the hospital implemented it incorrectly or used it outside its indicated population, and the patient or their family is left with nothing. I have talked to a medical malpractice attorney about this and she said it is genuinely one of the harder liability questions she has encountered in twenty years of practice, specifically because the diffusion of responsibility is so complete.⏎⏎I'm also worried about what happens to nursing and lower-level clinical staff. Not just job displacement in the abstract, but something more immediate: the de-skilling that happens when people stop doing tasks because a machine does them. If a nurse spends two years relying on an AI tool to flag deteriorating patients, does she still have the observational instincts she had before? There is research on automation bias in aviation that suggests the answer is probably no, and healthcare hasn't really confronted that question at all.⏎⏎One more thing and then I'll stop. The RFI mentions administrative burden reduction as one of the areas where AI could help, and I want to be careful about dismissing that entirely because I know documentation burden is a real problem. But a lot of administrative work in healthcare exists because it encodes human judgment about edge cases, exceptions, and context. Prior auth processes are terrible and I would not defend them. But the solution to a bad human process is not necessarily an automated version of the same bad process running faster and at higher volume with less ability to appeal. There needs to be some mechanism for a real person to intervene, and I'm not confident the current regulatory structure requires that in any consistent way.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0066♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Lee♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔Anthem Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Riverside University Health System in Moreno Valley in 2022 after a routine knee replacement went wrong. What saved her, honestly, was a night-shift nurse named Delores who noticed something was off about how my mother was breathing and pushed back against what the attending had ordered. Delores had been doing that job for nineteen years. She knew my mother was scared and wasn't telling anyone the full story of her symptoms because she didn't want to be a bother. No algorithm would have caught that. No algorithm was going to sit on the edge of the bed and figure out that this particular 71-year-old woman from Perris, California was minimizing her pain because her husband had died in a hospital and she associated complaining with dying there.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read about AI being "accelerated" into clinical care. The framing of this RFI bothers me. It takes as given that the goal is to speed up adoption, and then asks how. But the more basic question of whether and in what settings AI should be making or influencing clinical decisions doesn't really get asked, or it gets buried under assumptions about efficiency and innovation.⏎⏎The question I actually want HHS to answer is who is responsible when an AI-assisted decision causes harm. Not in a theoretical sense. In the practical, legal, and moral sense. If a hospital deploys a sepsis prediction tool that flags a patient as low risk and that patient dies six hours later, and it later comes out the tool was validated on a patient population that didn't look much like the patient who died, what happens? The clinician can say they followed the recommendation. The hospital can say it was FDA-cleared. The vendor can say it was used outside the intended population. I have watched enough medical malpractice cases as a legal observer to know that diffuse responsibility is not the same as no harm having occurred. It just means the family gets nothing and nobody learns anything.⏎⏎There is also something I don't see discussed enough, which is what happens to the Deloreses of the world when AI gets normalized as the standard of care. If the default becomes "the algorithm said X, and the nurse or the resident or the PA who said otherwise is now the one who has to justify themselves," that changes the culture of clinical decision-making in ways that are hard to reverse. The pressure to defer to the system won't be written down anywhere. It will just be how things work. And the people most likely to get overruled by a machine recommendation are probably the same people who are already lower in the institutional hierarchy, which is not reassuring.⏎⏎I understand there are AI tools that do genuinely useful things, like flagging imaging findings that get missed on a busy overnight shift. I'm not saying none of this has any value. But the question of whether HHS should be actively accelerating broad adoption across clinical care settings is different from whether specific, narrow, well-validated tools have a role. The RFI kind of collapses that distinction.⏎⏎On the reimbursement side specifically, I would ask HHS to be very careful about creating financial incentives that push hospitals toward AI adoption before there's real evidence of benefit in their specific patient populations. The history of healthcare IT is not encouraging here. Hospitals spent billions on EHR systems in the 2010s partly because the incentive structures made it hard not to, and clinicians are still complaining about the documentation burden those systems created. Creating similar incentives for AI tools before the evidence base is solid seems like a good way to repeat that mistake at scale.⏎⏎One more thing that I haven't seen addressed anywhere in the materials I've read about this RFI: what happens to the workers whose jobs get partially or fully automated? Medical coders, prior authorization specialists, the people who do utilization review. These are not glamorous jobs but they are jobs held by real people, often women, often people of color, often in communities that don't have a lot of other options. If HHS is going to accelerate AI adoption it should at minimum be honest that this will eliminate positions and have a plan for what happens to those workers, rather than leaving that as someone else's problem to figure out later.⏎⏎The comment period closes February 4, 2025 and I hope♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0067♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Anderson♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔National Disability Rights Network♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Advocate Christ Medical Center in Oak Lawn, Illinois in February of 2023. She had sepsis, and it got complicated. What I remember from those eleven days is not the monitors or the labs or the imaging. What I remember is a night-shift nurse named Darnell who noticed she was holding her jaw differently and figured out she was in pain before she could tell anyone herself. That is not a pattern-matching problem. That is a human being paying attention to another human being.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS wants to "accelerate" AI adoption in clinical care, my honest reaction is: slow down and explain why this is actually good for patients, because I am not convinced.⏎⏎I want to focus on one thing the RFI asks about, which is how AI can support clinical decision-making. My concern is that the framing of the question already assumes the answer. It asks how to accelerate adoption, not whether to. That is a real problem with how this process is being set up. The department is essentially asking the public to help it go faster, when what it should be asking is whether going fast is safe at all.⏎⏎Here is what worries me specifically. AI tools trained on historical medical data will reflect historical patterns of care, and those patterns are not neutral. There is well-documented research showing that some widely used clinical algorithms underestimated pain and illness severity in Black patients because the training data reflected years of undertreated Black patients. An AI system doesn't fix that problem just by being new. It can actually lock it in and make it harder to see, because the output looks authoritative. A number on a screen feels more objective than a human judgment even when it isn't.⏎⏎I also think there is something being glossed over about accountability. When a doctor makes a wrong call, there is a person who made that call and who can be questioned and held responsible. When an AI makes a wrong recommendation and a clinician follows it, who exactly is accountable? The hospital will say the clinician should have caught the error. The vendor will say the clinician misused the tool. The clinician will say the tool told them to do it. I have watched enough hospital billing disputes to know that institutions are very good at diffusing responsibility when it suits them, and AI gives them a whole new layer of diffusion to hide behind.⏎⏎I want to be fair here. I don't think everyone pushing AI in healthcare is acting in bad faith. Some of it probably does help. I've read that some radiology AI tools catch certain findings that get missed on busy overnight shifts. I'm not saying the technology is worthless. What I'm saying is that "some tools help in some settings" is a very different claim than "we should accelerate broad AI adoption across clinical care," and HHS seems to be moving from the first to the second without really making the argument.⏎⏎The administrative side of this concerns me too, maybe more than the clinical side if I'm honest. Insurance companies using AI to process prior authorizations and claims denials is already happening, and it is already generating horror stories. A 2023 ProPublica investigation found that some insurers were using AI tools to deny claims at rates that no individual physician reviewer could have achieved, which sort of tells you that the AI wasn't doing careful case-by-case review. It was doing fast bulk denial. HHS should be asking hard questions about that before it encourages more of it.⏎⏎What I would actually want HHS to do is require any AI tool used in a clinical or coverage decision to be subject to mandatory post-market surveillance, bias audits by independent third parties, and clear disclosure to patients when AI was used in their care or in a decision about their coverage. Not voluntary disclosure. Mandatory. And the audit results should be public, not just submitted to regulators who may or may not act on them.⏎⏎I keep coming back to Darnell noticing my mother's jaw. There is no dataset for that.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0068♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Clark♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023, and I can tell you that what kept her alive was not any algorithm. It was a night-shift nurse named Darnell who noticed something was off about the way she was breathing at 2am and called the attending before the monitors flagged anything. That kind of judgment does not come from pattern matching on a training dataset. It comes from years of watching sick people and learning what normal looks like in a way that is almost impossible to put into words, let alone code.⏎⏎I want to focus my comment on the question of what it actually means to "accelerate adoption" of AI in clinical care, because I think HHS is framing this wrong. The assumption baked into this RFI seems to be that speed of adoption is itself a good, and that regulatory barriers are the main problem standing between patients and better care. But that framing ignores the possibility that caution is appropriate here, and that some of the friction in the system is doing important work.⏎⏎Medicine is not primarily an information problem. I know that sounds almost obvious, but I think people who design these systems sometimes forget it. A patient sitting in an exam room is not a set of inputs waiting to be processed. They are scared, often inarticulate, sometimes lying to their doctor about how much they drink or whether they've been taking their medication. Good clinicians know this and they navigate it. They ask the same question three different ways. They watch someone's face when they mention their spouse. That interpretive work happens in a relationship, and a relationship requires a human being who is actually accountable for the outcome.⏎⏎What worries me about the direction HHS seems to be heading is that AI tools can create the impression of objectivity and rigor while actually just laundering existing biases through a more authoritative-looking interface. There is a lot of published research on this, including the 2019 Obermeyer et al. study in Science that found a widely used commercial algorithm was systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health status. And that tool had gone through whatever validation process the vendor used before selling it. The problem is that biases in historical data look like signal to a model that doesn't know anything about the social structures that produced the data. Deploying more of these systems faster is not obviously going to fix that problem. It might make it worse, precisely because the systems look so authoritative.⏎⏎There is also an employment and professional autonomy dimension to this that I don't think HHS is taking seriously enough. The pitch is usually that AI will reduce clinician burnout by handling administrative tasks. Maybe. But hospitals and insurers have very strong financial incentives to use these tools to justify staffing reductions or to automate prior authorization denials in ways that are harder for patients and doctors to challenge. A human reviewer who denies a claim can be questioned. They can be asked to explain themselves. An algorithmic denial is much harder to push back on because the logic is often opaque and the vendor treats it as proprietary. That is not a hypothetical concern, that is already happening with tools used by insurers for post-acute care decisions, and the ProPublica reporting on Cigna's algorithm-assisted review process in 2023 showed pretty clearly how those systems can be structured to minimize meaningful human review.⏎⏎I understand that there are areas where AI tools might genuinely help, particularly in diagnostic imaging where the research base is more solid and the task is more constrained. I am not saying technology has no role. But there is a difference between careful, evidence-based deployment in specific narrow contexts with strong oversight, and what this RFI seems to be asking for, which is a broad push to remove friction from adoption across clinical settings generally.⏎⏎The accountability question is the one that keeps me up at night, honestly. When an AI-assisted decision leads to a bad outcome, who is responsible? The clinician who followed the recommendation? The hospital that deployed the tool? The vendor who built it? In practice, responsibility tends to get diffused across all of them in ways that make it hard for any single party to be held accountable, and in my experience that diffusion is not an accident. It is a feature that vendors and institutions find very convenient.⏎⏎HHS should be asking whether deployment of these tools is outpacing our ability to evaluate them, audit them, and correct them when they fail. The current answer to that question is almost certainly yes, and I don't think the right response is to accelerate further.⏎⏎One specific thing I would ask HHS to consider: before removing any regulatory barriers to AI adoption in clinical settings, please require mandatory post-market surveillance and public reporting of adverse events attributable to AI tool recommendations, similar to the FDA's MedWatch system but specifically designed for software-driven clinical decision support. Right now there is no systematic way to know how often these tools are contributing to diagnostic errors or inappropriate treatment decisions at the population level, which means we are essentially running an uncontrolled experiment on patients without their knowledge or consent.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0069♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Garcia♔♔California♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's Medical Center in Houston in March of 2023, and I watched a rotating cast of nurses, hospitalists, and specialists piece together what was happening with her through conversations, contradictory chart notes, and a fair amount of educated guessing. It was messy and human and occasionally frightening. But there was always someone in the room whose face I could read, someone who would stop and explain, someone whose career was on the line if they got it wrong. That is what I keep thinking about when I read proposals like this one.⏎⏎The question I'd want HHS to actually sit with, rather than just solicit comments about, is who ends up responsible when an AI system gets it wrong. Not philosophically. Practically. If a clinical decision support tool flags my mother as low risk for sepsis and she deteriorates overnight because the nursing team was taught to trust the algorithm, who do I sue? Who loses their license? Who calls me to explain? My guess is nobody, or rather, the answer gets so diffuse that it amounts to nobody. The hospital points to the vendor. The vendor says the clinician retained final authority. The clinician says the system showed a green indicator. And my mother is still dead.⏎⏎I understand there are people who will say this is exactly why we need oversight frameworks and post-market surveillance and transparency requirements and so on. Maybe. But I am deeply skeptical that we can regulate our way to accountability in a system where the tool itself is a black box and the institution deploying it has a financial incentive to rely on it more, not less, because it costs less than hiring another nurse. That's not a hypothetical. Hospitals have been using AI-based tools for prior authorization and patient triage decisions since at least 2020, and the staffing levels have not gone up. The mortality reviews have not become more rigorous. What has happened is that the documentation burden on nurses has increased while actual bedside time has decreased, and now some of those nurses are being told the AI will help them manage more patients simultaneously.⏎⏎That should terrify anyone who has actually been a patient recently.⏎⏎The RFI asks specifically about regulatory or reimbursement actions HHS could take to accelerate adoption. I'd like to suggest the opposite framing: what actions could HHS take to ensure that any AI tool used in clinical settings has demonstrated actual clinical benefit in a population that looks like the patients it will be used on, before it gets reimbursed or required? Not accuracy on a validation dataset. Actual outcomes. Because a tool can be 94% accurate on average and still be systematically wrong about elderly Black women with atypical presentations of cardiac events, which is exactly the kind of patient who is already underserved.⏎⏎The bias problem in training data is not a technical detail to be corrected later. It is a structural feature of a healthcare system that has historically collected worse data on marginalized populations because it provided worse care to marginalized populations. You cannot launder that history through a model and get equity out the other end.⏎⏎I'm also genuinely worried about what this does to clinical training over time. If residents and new nurses spend their formative years deferring to algorithmic recommendations, what happens to the diagnostic reasoning skills that take years to develop? The muscle memory of reading a patient who can't tell you what's wrong? I don't have a study to cite on this, I'm just a person who spent a lot of time in hospitals this decade and has watched how much clinical judgment still matters in moments where the chart says one thing and the patient in front of you says something else entirely with their body and their eyes and the way they're breathing.⏎⏎None of this is to say that every use of any AI tool is automatically harmful. I can imagine genuinely useful applications in radiology image review or flagging medication interactions, where the stakes of a single decision are bounded and the clinician is still clearly in charge. But "accelerating adoption" as a policy goal, without a prior commitment to proving benefit and assigning liability, seems like the wrong starting point entirely. It starts from the assumption that more AI in healthcare is good and asks how to get there faster, when the question we should be asking is♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0070♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Lewis♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔Privia Health♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Francis Hospital in Hartford in March of 2023, and what I remember most clearly is not any particular test or medication. It's the night nurse who sat with her for twenty minutes at 2am because my mother was confused and frightened and kept asking where her husband was (he died in 2009). That nurse didn't consult a protocol. She just stayed. I don't know how you train an algorithm to do that or why we would want to pretend we could.⏎⏎I want to comment specifically on the question of what barriers HHS sees as worth removing to speed up AI adoption, because I think the framing of that question is already pointed in the wrong direction. The assumption seems to be that adoption is being held back by outdated rules or slow reimbursement structures, and that the job of this RFI is to figure out how to get out of the way. But some of those barriers exist because people thought carefully about what medicine requires. Removing them to accelerate deployment of AI tools is not automatically progress.⏎⏎My concern isn't really about one specific product. It's more about the direction this is all heading. I've read enough about how some of the insurance-related AI tools work — there was substantial reporting in 2023 about UnitedHealth's nH Predict system and the extremely high denial rates it was producing for post-acute care — to know that "AI-assisted decisions" can very quickly become a way to automate cost-cutting while giving it a technical veneer that makes it harder to appeal. A human reviewer who denies a claim is at least theoretically accountable to another human. When a system does it, the accountability gets very muddy very fast.⏎⏎The depersonalization issue is real too, and I don't think it gets taken seriously enough in these conversations. When a clinician is looking at a dashboard of AI recommendations, there's a psychological shift that happens. You start evaluating the machine's output rather than the patient in front of you. I've talked to nurses and physicians who describe this already with current EHR systems, where the documentation burden has made them feel more like data entry clerks than caregivers. Adding AI recommendations on top of that workflow doesn't obviously make it more human. It might make it less.⏎⏎I'm also just skeptical of the safety claims. The validation studies for many of these tools are done in controlled settings with complete records and specific patient populations. Deploy the same tool at a safety-net hospital with incomplete records, high patient turnover, and staff who haven't had adequate training, and the performance profile is probably completely different. I don't think HHS has a good answer for how to ensure that a tool validated at Mass General performs the same way at a rural critical access hospital in Mississippi. That's not a hypothetical gap, that's a documented problem with how medical devices and software get deployed across disparate settings.⏎⏎The employment angle matters too and I feel like it gets dismissed as just being resistant to change. Healthcare jobs aren't just jobs. The person who does intake, the patient advocate, the medical coder who knows when something doesn't look right — these roles carry institutional knowledge and human judgment that doesn't get captured anywhere in a training dataset. If AI tools allow hospitals to justify cutting those positions, the loss is real even if the cost savings show up nicely in a quarterly report.⏎⏎I don't think HHS should be trying to accelerate AI adoption right now. There isn't adequate post-market surveillance, there isn't meaningful patient consent in most of these deployments, and there's no clear standard for when a tool has failed badly enough that it needs to be pulled. Those gaps should be closed before the Department starts removing whatever regulatory friction is currently slowing things down.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0071♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Jackson♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔American Psychiatric Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Riverside University Health System in Moreno Valley in the fall of 2022, and what kept her alive was not a pattern-matching algorithm. It was a night nurse named Delores who noticed that my mother's breathing had changed in a way that didn't show up on any monitor, who went and got the attending at 2 in the morning because something felt wrong. That kind of judgment is not a data point. I don't know how you would even begin to train a model on it.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS is looking at ways to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my first reaction is honestly just dread. Not because I think technology is bad in some general sense, but because I've watched what happens when hospitals get excited about efficiency tools. The tool gets deployed, the administrators feel good about it, and somewhere down the line the Deloreses of the world are working three more patients per shift because the system flagged everyone as stable.⏎⏎The question I keep returning to is: who is this actually for? When a hospital buys an AI triage tool or a predictive deterioration score or whatever the latest product is, they are making a business decision. They may genuinely believe it will help patients. They may also be looking at staffing budgets. Both things can be true at once, and I think HHS is being a little naive if it doesn't reckon with that tension directly.⏎⏎There's also a bias problem that I don't think gets taken seriously enough in these conversations. A lot of these systems are trained on data from academic medical centers or large hospital networks that already skew toward certain populations. A 2019 study published in Science found that a widely used commercial algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for need, and Black patients had historically been given less care and therefore generated lower costs. The algorithm looked objective. It was not. And the scary part is that most of the clinicians using it had no idea that was happening. If HHS accelerates adoption without mandatory, ongoing, post-deployment bias auditing, you are going to scale that kind of error across the entire health system.⏎⏎I want to be honest that I'm not a researcher or a doctor. I'm a social worker who has spent about fifteen years helping elderly and disabled clients navigate the healthcare system in Southern California, and a lot of what I see from the ground level is that my clients already feel like they are being processed rather than cared for. Adding AI into that experience without serious guardrails is not going to fix that feeling. It is going to make it worse. An 80-year-old woman with limited English who is trying to understand why her prior authorization was denied does not benefit from that denial having been generated faster by a machine. She benefits from a human being who can explain it, who can be held accountable, who she can appeal to in a language she understands.⏎⏎On the specific question of administrative AI, which I notice the RFI asks about, I want to push back on the assumption that administrative functions are somehow lower stakes. Prior authorization decisions affect whether people get surgery, whether they get the right medication, whether they stay in the hospital long enough to actually recover. These are clinical decisions wearing administrative clothing. Using AI to make them faster while reducing the number of people available to review appeals is not a neutral efficiency gain. I've had clients wait six weeks to get a prior auth denial reviewed by an actual person at their insurer, and that was before any of this AI acceleration happened. I can only imagine what that timeline looks like when the denial was generated automatically and the appeals queue is handled by a system that can't read the handwritten notes from my client's doctor in Riverside.⏎⏎HHS asks what barriers exist to AI adoption and how the department can remove them. I'd ask a different question: what barriers exist to accountability when AI causes harm, and are those barriers going up or coming down? Right now if a clinician makes a mistake there is a chain of responsibility. Licensing boards, malpractice, peer review, credentialing. When an AI system makes a mistake the vendor says it's a tool not a decision-maker, the hospital says the clinician should have caught it, and the clinician says they were just following the recommendation. My clients don't have lawyers. They can't navigate that.⏎⏎I'm also not sure the evidence base for most of these tools is as strong as the sales pitches suggest. The FDA cleared a lot of AI devices through the 510(k) pathway which doesn't require clinical trial evidence, just substantial equivalence to a predicate device. That is a very low bar for something that might be influencing whether your cardiologist orders a certain test or whether your insurer approves your hip replacement.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0072♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Scott♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔Foundation Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in January 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that her primary care doctor almost missed because the clinic had recently rolled out an AI-assisted triage tool that flagged her as low-risk twice before a nurse overrode it. She survived. But I have spent the time since then thinking very hard about what it means when a machine is wrong and nobody is quite sure who is responsible for that wrongness.⏎⏎The question HHS is asking here seems to be mostly about acceleration. How do we move faster? What barriers are in the way? I want to offer the opposite perspective, which is that the barriers may be doing important work.⏎⏎Medicine is not primarily an information-processing problem. I know that sounds obvious but the entire logic of AI adoption in clinical care treats it as one. You have symptoms, you have records, you have lab values, and the machine finds the pattern. But my mother was not a pattern. She was a 71-year-old woman who had stopped eating, who was scared and minimizing her symptoms when she talked to the triage intake person, whose blood pressure was slightly elevated but not dramatically so, and who had a history of anxiety that made it easy to attribute her distress to something psychological. The nurse who finally pushed back on the AI's low-risk classification did so because she noticed something in my mother's affect, something that is not in any training dataset I am aware of. That is not a small thing. That is the thing.⏎⏎What concerns me about this RFI is that the framing assumes the main obstacle to AI adoption is regulatory friction. But there is another possibility, which is that the caution is warranted. The FDA has cleared hundreds of AI-enabled medical devices in the last several years, and the post-market surveillance on many of them is thin. We do not have good data on how these tools perform outside of the academic medical centers and well-resourced health systems where they were validated. A tool that works well at Mass General may behave very differently at a rural critical access hospital with incomplete EHR data, different patient demographics, and staff who had two hours of training on the system.⏎⏎I am also worried about what happens to the question of accountability when AI is in the loop. If the sepsis tool had been wrong and my mother had died, who would have been responsible? The nurse who relied on it? The hospital that deployed it without adequate training? The vendor who sold a tool validated on a dataset that did not look like my mother? This is not a hypothetical problem. It is already happening and the answer is usually that nobody is quite responsible in a way that leads to any consequences or any change.⏎⏎There is also a labor question that I think gets glossed over in these discussions. Hospitals are already using AI tools in prior authorization and claims review in ways that appear designed to reduce the number of human reviewers, not to assist them. UnitedHealth's use of an AI model called nH Predict to deny post-acute care claims, which led to a class action lawsuit filed in 2023, is the clearest recent example of what happens when efficiency becomes the governing value. The people most harmed by those denials were elderly Medicare Advantage patients who did not have the resources or the health to fight back.⏎⏎I am not saying AI has no place anywhere in medicine. I am saying that HHS should be asking different questions than the ones in this RFI. Not how do we accelerate adoption, but where has adoption already caused harm that we have not fully documented, and what accountability structures need to exist before we go further.⏎⏎The question of who audits these systems after deployment, and with what authority, and with what consequences for vendors whose tools cause harm, seems like the thing that should come before any acceleration.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0073♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Flores♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February 2023, and during that time I watched nurses and doctors make decisions about her care that I genuinely do not think any software could have made. Not because the information was complicated in some abstract sense, but because she was terrified and lying about her pain levels, and her doctor figured that out by watching her face when she thought nobody was looking. That is not a data point. I don't know how you train a model on that.⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the question HHS is asking about what actions the Department can take to accelerate adoption, because I think the framing of the question itself is part of the problem. The assumption baked into "how do we accelerate this" is that more AI in clinical care is self-evidently good and the only obstacle is regulatory friction. I'd ask HHS to sit with a different question for a moment: what if slower is actually the right speed here?⏎⏎The safety argument against rushing this is not hypothetical. There is published research showing that some widely used clinical AI tools perform significantly worse on Black patients than white patients because they were trained on datasets that didn't represent those populations adequately. The pulse oximetry problem is a version of this, where a device that was considered reliable for decades turned out to be systematically less accurate for patients with darker skin. That wasn't caught quickly. It caused real harm to real people. AI systems are more opaque than a pulse ox, and when they fail, the failure mode is harder to see precisely because the output looks authoritative. A number on a screen that says "73% probability of sepsis" carries an air of objectivity that a nurse's gut feeling doesn't, even if the nurse is right and the model is wrong in that specific case.⏎⏎What concerns me most is the employment and accountability piece, though. I keep coming back to this. Hospitals are under enormous financial pressure and they will use any tool available to justify cutting staff. "The AI handles that now" is a cost-cutting argument waiting to happen, and it will hit the roles that are least glamorous but often most important: the care coordinator who knows which patients won't follow up unless someone calls them, the billing advocate who understands which denials are worth fighting. When those jobs get automated or eliminated, the people who needed that human attention will just fall through. They won't show up in any vendor's outcome metrics.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has zero place in medicine. I genuinely don't know where the line is, and I'd be lying if I said the question is simple. But I think HHS should be asking much harder questions about liability before any of this goes further. Right now if an AI system recommends a treatment and the clinician follows it and the patient is harmed, it's not obvious who is responsible. The vendor will say the clinician should have overridden it. The clinician will say they reasonably relied on a validated tool. The hospital will say they followed the vendor's implementation guidelines. The patient or their family is left holding the consequences of a decision that nobody actually made.⏎⏎Before the Department does anything to accelerate adoption, I'd want to see a clear legal framework that places responsibility somewhere specific. Not a voluntary commitment from vendors to be transparent. An actual binding rule that says: if your AI system is used in a clinical decision and that decision causes harm, here is who is liable and here is what the remedy looks like. Until that exists, "accelerating adoption" just means accelerating the deployment of systems whose failure modes we haven't fully mapped, into a liability vacuum, in a field where the people being harmed are often already vulnerable.⏎⏎I also think the reimbursement question deserves more skepticism than it's getting. If CMS creates billing codes that reward the use of AI-assisted diagnosis or AI-generated treatment plans, you will immediately create a financial incentive for overuse that has nothing to do with patient outcomes. We have seen this before with other technologies and procedures. The incentive structure shapes the behavior, and right now nobody seems to be thinking carefully enough about what happens when there's money attached to running a patient through an algorithm.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0074♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Ramirez♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔American Academy of Ophthalmology♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that was initially missed on her first ER visit. What saved her, eventually, was a night-shift nurse who noticed she was moving her lips oddly and called the attending back in at 2am. No algorithm flagged that. A person noticed something that wasn't in any dropdown menu or vitals field.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read about HHS wanting to "accelerate" AI adoption in clinical care. Accelerate toward what, exactly.⏎⏎The question I want to address is the one about what regulatory actions HHS should take to support AI adoption - because my answer is that HHS should pump the brakes, not hit the gas, and I want to explain why that's not just fear of new technology.⏎⏎Medicine has edge cases everywhere. Not unusual ones either - just ordinary patients who don't fit the training data. My mother is 74, speaks some English as a second language, has an unusual medication history because she immigrated and had different care in Poland for decades, and has a body weight that puts her in a category that's consistently underrepresented in clinical datasets. She is not a rare patient. She is an extremely common type of patient in this country. And AI systems trained on American hospital records from the last twenty years will have systematic gaps for people like her. The 2019 ProPublica reporting on the COMPAS recidivism algorithm showed how hard it is to detect bias in a system that produces confident-looking outputs. Healthcare AI has the same problem and the stakes are a lot higher than a parole recommendation.⏎⏎What worries me almost as much as the safety question is the accountability question. Right now if a clinician makes a wrong call, there is a person who made that call. They can explain their reasoning, face a board, be sued, lose their license. I'm not saying that system is perfect - it obviously isn't. But when an AI tool is involved, the hospital says the vendor is responsible, the vendor says the clinician had final sign-off, and the patient's family spends three years trying to figure out who to even depose. That diffusion of responsibility isn't a theoretical risk. It's already happening in prior authorization disputes where insurers use automated denial systems and then claim a physician reviewed the case, when in practice that review is a two-minute rubber stamp on a machine output.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require that any AI system used in a clinical decision or insurance determination be disclosed to the patient in plain language, that the institution using it maintain full liability for its outputs, and that there be a mandatory human review process with documented reasoning before any AI-assisted denial or treatment recommendation is finalized. Not a checkbox. Actual documented reasoning from a named clinician.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the employment piece because I don't think it gets taken seriously enough. The argument that AI will "reduce clinician burnout" is being made by the same industry that has spent thirty years cutting nursing staff ratios to the legal minimum and fighting every attempt to mandate safe staffing levels. I have no reason to believe that AI tools will be used to give nurses more time with patients rather than to justify having fewer nurses on the floor. The incentive structure points entirely in the wrong direction. A hospital that adopts an AI monitoring system doesn't get credit for keeping the same number of nurses - it gets credit for maintaining outcomes with fewer. That's the business case and everyone knows it.⏎⏎The RFI asks about reimbursement mechanisms to support adoption. I'd ask HHS to think very carefully about whether reimbursement incentives for AI adoption might function the same way that some pay-for-performance metrics have - rewarding the appearance of quality while creating perverse incentives that harm actual patients. Tying Medicare reimbursement to AI adoption before we have long-term outcome data from diverse patient populations seems like exactly the kind of thing that looks responsible in a press release and creates problems five years later that are very hard to untangle.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0075♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Flores♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in October 2022 after a sepsis diagnosis that was nearly missed because the initial triage system flagged her as "low acuity." She is alive because a night-shift nurse named Darlene overrode what the computer said and pushed for a blood culture. I think about that a lot when I read about efforts to put more AI into clinical settings, not less.⏎⏎The question I want to address is the one about what HHS can do to "accelerate adoption" in clinical care. My honest answer is: slow down. I don't think that makes me anti-technology. I have a smartphone, I use online banking, I'm not a luddite. But medicine is different and I don't think the people pushing this have really grappled with how different.⏎⏎The core problem is that clinical judgment is not a pattern-matching exercise. When Darlene looked at my mother she was reading something that no algorithm trained on discharge summaries would capture: the particular color of her skin, the way she kept plucking at her blanket, the fact that her husband of 52 years said she just didn't look right. That's not mysticism, it's the accumulated interpretive skill of someone who has spent years at bedsides. An AI system that performs well on a validation dataset does not have that. And the danger isn't just that the AI will be wrong. It's that it will be wrong in a way that looks authoritative, and that clinicians under pressure, rushing between 14 patients, will defer to it rather than trust themselves.⏎⏎There are real numbers on this. A 2019 study in JAMA found that a commercial sepsis prediction algorithm used at the University of Michigan missed nearly two thirds of sepsis cases that human clinicians identified. That's not a fringe result, that's a widely cited finding about a condition that kills 270,000 Americans per year. And yet hospitals keep buying these systems because the vendors are aggressive and the procurement process doesn't require the same level of evidence that the FDA would require for a drug.⏎⏎What I want HHS to do is require that any AI clinical decision support tool demonstrate real-world effectiveness in the specific patient population where it will be deployed before it gets used, not just performance on a test set from a different hospital system in a different state. That seems obvious. Apparently it isn't, because right now it mostly doesn't happen.⏎⏎I'm also worried about something that the RFI doesn't really address directly, which is what happens to nurses and other clinical staff when these systems come in. I've talked to hospital workers in my county and what they describe is not AI freeing them up for the human parts of the job. It's AI generating alerts that they have to document responses to, creating more charting burden, more checkbox compliance, less time for the Darlenes of the world to stand at the bedside and notice something. There is a real possibility that accelerating AI adoption in clinical care makes care worse for vulnerable patients while making the quarterly reports look better.⏎⏎I understand there are things AI can probably do well. Radiology image screening, maybe. Drug interaction flagging. Scheduling. I'm not saying zero AI ever. But the framing of this RFI treats adoption as the goal and asks how to get there faster. I think the goal should be patient outcomes and the question should be whether AI helps or hurts those, case by case, setting by setting, population by population. Those are not the same question and the difference matters a lot depending on which hospital you end up in and whether your night nurse is overworked and has twelve other patients and an alert fatigue problem from a system that cried wolf forty times that shift already.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0076♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Flores♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔Pfizer Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the hospital for six weeks in 2019 after a stroke, and what I remember most is not the technology — it's the nurse who noticed she seemed more confused than usual on a Tuesday afternoon and flagged it before it showed up in any test. That kind of judgment doesn't come from a pattern-matching system trained on hospital records. It comes from someone who has been doing this work for twenty years and knows what "off" looks like on a patient they've been watching for days.⏎⏎That's what worries me about the direction this RFI seems to be pointing. The framing assumes that more AI adoption is inherently good and that the job of regulators is mostly to smooth the path. I'd push back hard on that assumption.⏎⏎HHS asks about what actions would accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. I want to argue that slowing down, or being very selective, would actually produce better outcomes for patients. The pressure to adopt AI tools in hospitals right now is coming more from vendor marketing and cost-cutting ambitions than from demonstrated patient benefit. I don't have a citation for that in the way a researcher might, but I've been following this space for a while and I keep seeing the same pattern: a hospital announces an AI partnership, the press release is full of words like "revolutionary" and "predictive," and then two years later there's a quiet study showing the system performed worse on Black patients or low-income patients than on the populations it was originally tested on. The sepsis prediction algorithm that was deployed at University of Michigan and later analyzed in critical papers is one example. Epic's sepsis model had a sensitivity rate cited around 63% in independent validation, which means it was missing more than a third of cases. That's not a tool you want clinicians deferring to.⏎⏎The RFI also asks about reimbursement changes that might support AI use. I'd urge HHS not to create financial incentives that push facilities toward AI adoption before there are meaningful standards for what "validated" means in a deployed clinical environment. Validation on a training dataset in a research hospital is not the same as performance in a rural critical access hospital with different patient demographics, different staffing levels, and different EHR systems. Right now those distinctions are blurry enough that a vendor can claim FDA clearance and hospitals assume that means something more comprehensive than it does.⏎⏎There's also something that doesn't get talked about enough in these policy conversations, which is what happens to the people doing the work. When a hospital installs an AI tool for prior authorization review, that's not just automation — it's a restructuring of who has power over the decision. The reviewer whose judgment used to be the final word is now being asked to override a system, which is psychologically and professionally a very different position to be in. Override rates tend to drop over time as workers feel pressure not to challenge the machine. That's documented in behavioral research on automation bias going back to aviation studies in the 1990s. Healthcare is now walking into a problem aviation spent decades trying to correct.⏎⏎I'm not saying there's no role for any computational tool in medicine. I'm saying that HHS should not treat acceleration as the goal. The questions worth asking are whether a specific tool demonstrably improves outcomes for the actual patient population it will be used on, who is accountable when it fails, and whether the clinician using it still has genuine autonomy to override it without institutional penalty. If the answer to any of those is unclear, that tool should not be in clinical use yet, and federal reimbursement policy should not be designed in a way that rewards facilities for deploying it anyway.⏎⏎I'd also add — and I realize this is a separate issue but it bothers me — that some of the administrative AI applications being discussed, things like scheduling and documentation summarization, sound harmless but they're also how institutions get comfortable with the idea of AI making decisions. It normalizes the technology in lower-stakes contexts and then the logic becomes "well we're already using it for notes, why not triage." That creep is worth paying attention to before♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0077♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Nguyen♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔Disability Rights Advocates♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Advocate Christ Medical Center in Oak Lawn, Illinois in February of 2023. I was there almost every day. What I remember most is not the machines or the monitors — it's the pulmonologist who sat down next to her bed on day four, didn't look at a screen, and just talked to her. He asked about her dogs. He asked if she was frightened. He adjusted her treatment that afternoon in a way that, later, a different doctor told me had probably saved her life. I don't know how to put that into a dataset.⏎⏎That's the thing I keep coming back to when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings. Accelerate it toward what, exactly?⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to AI adoption, which I think frames the whole question wrong. The question should be what we lose when we push AI into more and more clinical decisions, and whether those losses are acceptable. I think HHS is treating the spread of AI in healthcare as something like a natural force that just needs to be cleared of regulatory underbrush, rather than a choice with real costs that fall disproportionately on the most vulnerable patients.⏎⏎The safety problems are real and they don't get talked about honestly. AI tools trained on historical health data inherit everything that was wrong with historical health care — the undertreated Black patients, the dismissed women with chronic pain, the poor people who only showed up in records when things had already gotten very bad. A system trained on that data will reproduce those patterns and call it medicine. And because the output comes from a machine, it can actually feel more authoritative than a human judgment that you could at least argue with. I've read about the sepsis prediction algorithm at University of Michigan hospitals that generated so many false alarms that nurses started ignoring the alerts altogether. That's not a hypothetical. That's what happens when you deploy tools in real environments versus controlled pilots.⏎⏎I'm also genuinely worried about what this does to the clinician's role over time. If a nurse practitioner is working in a system where an AI flags everything and recommends treatment paths and documents the encounter, at what point does she stop trusting her own judgment? At what point does the institution stop valuing her judgment, because the AI is cheaper and doesn't take sick days? I don't think that's paranoid. Staffing decisions follow cost structures, and if AI can be used to justify thinner staffing, it will be, regardless of whether the quality of care actually holds up.⏎⏎The administrative AI piece gets treated as obviously benign but I'm not sure it is. Prior authorization is already a nightmare, and there is strong evidence that insurers use algorithmic tools to deny claims at scale in ways that individual reviewers never would. The ProPublica investigation into Cigna in 2023 described physicians spending an average of 1.2 seconds per claim review. Once you automate that process further and call it AI, accountability basically disappears. The patient gets a denial letter, the insurer points to the algorithm, and nobody is responsible.⏎⏎HHS should be asking whether any of this should be slowed down rather than how to remove barriers. Embedding these systems creates dependencies that are very hard to reverse — vendors get contracts, institutions retrain workflows, and in five years it will be nearly impossible to ask whether the original decision was right. I think that's a real governance problem and I don't see it addressed anywhere in this RFI.⏎⏎One more thing — the framing of AI as a solution to clinician burnout troubles me. Burnout is caused by understaffing, insurance bureaucracy, documentation requirements, and moral injury from being asked to do too much with too little. Adding an AI layer on top of that system doesn't fix any of those things. It might make the documentation faster while leaving all the underlying conditions exactly in place, or it might just add a new layer of cognitive overhead as clinicians learn to second-guess or work around the recommendations they're getting from tools they had no role in choosing and don't fully understand.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0078♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Hernandez♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔Leidos Health♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Piedmont Atlanta Hospital in February of 2023 after a series of medication errors that her care team initially could not explain. She is 74, has lupus, and was also being treated for a UTI at the time. What eventually came out was that a clinical decision support tool had flagged one of her medications as low-risk based on her chart data, but the chart data was incomplete because she had received care at two different systems that didn't share records properly. Nobody caught it for three days. I am not saying AI caused that error, but I am saying that the attending physician told my sister, almost offhandedly, that the tool "would have caught it if the records were there." That comment has stayed with me. The assumption was that the tool was right and the inputs were the problem. Not that maybe someone should have looked harder at a 74-year-old woman with an autoimmune condition and an active infection.⏎⏎That is what worries me about this whole push to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. The framing is almost always that the tools are basically sound and the environment just needs to catch up. More data sharing, better training, cleaner records. But healthcare is not a data problem that happens to involve sick people. The incompleteness, the ambiguity, the patient who can't explain her symptoms clearly because she's scared and in pain and her daughter is crying in the corner -- that is not a bug in the input. That is the actual condition of medicine.⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the question HHS is asking about what barriers are slowing AI adoption and what the department can do to remove them. My answer is that some of those barriers exist for good reasons and removing them would be a mistake.⏎⏎One barrier is clinician skepticism. When nurses and doctors push back on AI tools, the instinct in policy circles seems to be that this is resistance to change that needs to be managed. But clinicians are often skeptical because they are the ones who see what happens when the tool is wrong. They are the ones who have to look the patient in the eye. If a recommendation system tells a physician that a patient is low-risk for sepsis and the physician has a gut feeling that something is off, that gut feeling is not irrational noise. It is pattern recognition built from years of experience. Policies that treat clinician override rates as a problem to be minimized are getting this exactly backwards.⏎⏎Another barrier is liability uncertainty. Vendors and hospitals want clearer legal frameworks so they know who is responsible when an AI tool contributes to a bad outcome. I understand why they want that, but I think the public interest runs in the opposite direction. Diffuse liability is a warning sign, not a regulatory gap to be filled. When a system is designed in a way that makes it genuinely hard to say who is responsible for a decision, maybe that system should not be making that decision. Making it easier for vendors to deploy tools without clear accountability does not protect patients.⏎⏎There is also a real concern that I have not seen addressed adequately anywhere in this RFI's framing: the use of AI in insurance and prior authorization. Administrative AI is often treated as lower-stakes than clinical AI, but that is wrong. A system that automatically denies coverage for a procedure, or flags a claim for review, or decides that a patient's hospitalization was not medically necessary, is making a clinical judgment. It is just making that judgment from a remove, without seeing the patient, and with a financial interest baked into the training objective. The ProPublica and HHS OIG reporting on algorithmic claim denials at major insurers in 2023 documented denial rates that no individual physician reviewer could plausibly have achieved, which means these systems were not reviewing cases, they were processing them. That distinction matters enormously and I do not think HHS has grappled with it seriously in how this RFI is structured.⏎⏎I am not saying technology has no place in medicine. I am saying that acceleration is the wrong goal right now. The question should not be how do we remove barriers to adoption, but what harms are already occurring and who is being harmed. The populations most likely to be hurt by brittle, biased, or misapplied AI tools are the same populations that already get worse care: elderly patients, patients with multiple chronic conditions, patients with limited English, patients in under-resourced hospitals that cannot afford the infrastructure to deploy these tools safely but will deploy them anyway because they are marketed as cost-saving.⏎⏎My mother is fine, by the way. She went home after eleven days and is doing reasonably well. But I think about those three days a lot.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0079♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Miller♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the ICU at St. Vincent's in Indianapolis for eleven days in February 2023, and I watched what happened when the staff was stretched thin. Nurses were running between rooms. The attending would see her for maybe four minutes at a time. What actually helped her was a night-shift nurse named Delores who noticed she was holding her jaw funny and figured out she was in pain but couldn't communicate it because of the oxygen mask. That's not in any algorithm. That's a person who knew how to look at another person.⏎⏎So when I hear that HHS wants to "accelerate AI adoption in clinical care," I honestly feel my stomach drop. Not because I'm against technology in general, but because I keep thinking about what that framing erases. The word "accelerate" is doing a lot of work. It assumes the direction is already correct and the only question is speed. I'd push back on that assumption pretty hard.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to AI adoption and what HHS can do to overcome them. I want to suggest a different question: whether some of those barriers exist for good reasons and whether the Department should be protecting them rather than removing them.⏎⏎One thing I keep coming back to is the accountability problem. When an AI system recommends a course of treatment or flags a patient as low-priority or helps an insurer decide to deny a claim, and something goes wrong, who is responsible? Right now I don't think there's a clear answer to that, and the absence of a clear answer isn't a paperwork problem, it's a patient safety problem. If a physician makes a wrong call, there are consequences, there are review boards, there is malpractice. If an algorithm makes a wrong call and the physician just went along with it because the interface made the recommendation look authoritative, the accountability is suddenly murky. The physician might say they were following clinical decision support. The hospital might say the vendor certified the product. The vendor might say the hospital deployed it outside its intended use case. I've read enough about cases like Optum's algorithm, which was found in 2019 to systematically underestimate care needs for Black patients compared to white patients with the same level of illness, to know this isn't hypothetical.⏎⏎And that example matters because it shows the bias can be invisible and still be doing damage. The system wasn't broken. It was working exactly as designed based on healthcare cost data that already reflected decades of unequal access. Accelerating the adoption of tools like that isn't progress.⏎⏎I understand the argument about burnout and administrative burden, and I'm not dismissing it. Clinicians are drowning in documentation. I've seen that too. But there's a real difference between using software to reduce paperwork and using AI to make or heavily influence clinical decisions, and I worry the RFI treats those as part of one continuum when they're actually quite different in terms of risk. The further AI moves toward actual decision support, the more the accountability problem I described above becomes serious.⏎⏎What I'd ask HHS to actually do is slow down rather than accelerate. Require that any AI tool used in clinical care or insurance decisions be publicly registered with the specific population it was validated on and the conditions under which it was tested. Require that any denial of coverage or clinical recommendation that was AI-assisted be disclosed to the patient as such. And honestly, I'd want a moratorium on AI in insurance prior-authorization decisions until there's a clear legal framework for liability, because right now the incentives are pointing entirely toward using these tools to cut costs and the patients are the ones who will absorb that risk.⏎⏎My mother got better because of Delores. She got better because of a doctor who sat down one afternoon and actually talked to her for twenty minutes about what she was afraid of. I don't know how you put that in a training set.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0080♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Nguyen♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔National Association of Social Workers♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Froedtert Hospital in Milwaukee in March of 2023 and what I remember most is that the nurses and one particular hospitalist, Dr. Anand, actually looked at her when they walked into the room. They noticed she was holding her arm differently. They noticed she seemed more confused after a medication change. None of that was in a data field. I keep thinking about what happens to those eleven days if the clinician's first job is reviewing what a system has already decided.⏎⏎I want to address specifically the question HHS is asking about barriers to adoption and what the department can do to accelerate use of AI in clinical settings. My answer is that I think HHS is asking the wrong question. The question should be whether adoption should be accelerated at all, and I don't think the department has made a serious case that it should.⏎⏎Medicine is not fundamentally an information retrieval problem. Even when it involves a lot of data, what clinicians actually do is interpret, and that interpretation depends on things that don't get captured: the patient who minimizes symptoms because they're afraid of a diagnosis, the family member who contradicts the patient's self-report, the way someone's breathing sounds versus how it looks on a chart. An AI system trained on historical records is going to reflect the patterns of who got diagnosed with what, and those patterns in American healthcare are not neutral. There's well-documented research, including work published in Science in 2019 by Obermeyer et al., showing that a widely-used commercial algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health, and Black patients had historically received less care even when sicker. That kind of bias doesn't announce itself. The output just looks like a number.⏎⏎What worries me about this RFI is that it frames the question as how to remove obstacles to AI adoption rather than how to protect patients from premature or inappropriate deployment. Regulatory action to "accelerate" adoption could easily mean weakening the scrutiny that currently exists, making it easier for hospitals to deploy tools that have been validated in academic medical centers on populations that don't look like the patients in rural community hospitals or safety net facilities. Those institutions often have the least capacity to audit what the tools are actually doing.⏎⏎There's also the insurance side of this, which I think deserves a lot more attention than it's getting. AI-driven prior authorization systems are already being used to deny care, and there have been multiple lawsuits and congressional inquiries into companies like UnitedHealth for using algorithmic tools to issue denials at a rate that human reviewers couldn't possibly sustain. Accelerating AI adoption in that context doesn't help patients, it just makes the denial pipeline faster and harder to appeal because you can't really cross-examine an algorithm.⏎⏎I understand there are people who believe AI will genuinely help with diagnostics, with early detection, with reducing the burden of documentation on overworked clinicians. I'm not completely dismissing that. But the question of whether a technology can help is different from whether deploying it widely and quickly, under commercial incentives, in under-resourced settings, with diffuse accountability, will actually produce those benefits at scale. I don't think HHS has answered that second question. I'm not sure it has seriously asked it.⏎⏎The accountability issue is the one I keep coming back to. When my mother's care team made decisions, there were humans who could be asked why. The hospitalist could be asked why he changed the medication. The nurse could be asked what she observed. If something had gone wrong, there were people who bore responsibility. I don't know how that works when the recommendation came from a system that the hospital licensed from a vendor who will say the clinician should have overridden it if it was wrong, while the clinician will say they were trained to trust it, and the hospital will say it met all regulatory requirements at the time of deployment.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require that any AI tool used in clinical decision-making be subject to ongoing post-market surveillance requirements equivalent to what FDA applies to high-risk medical devices, with mandatory reporting of adverse events and public disclosure of validation data including demographic breakdowns. That doesn't exist in any comprehensive way right now and the RFI doesn't really explain how accelerating adoption without that infrastructure in place is supposed to be safe.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0081♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Garcia♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023, and I sat with her through most of it. What got her through was not any particular test result or algorithm. It was a night nurse named Darnell who noticed she was holding her breath every time a certain resident came in the room, figured out she was terrified of him specifically because he reminded her of a doctor who had dismissed her years ago, and quietly rearranged the rotation. No system is going to catch that. No system is even looking for it.⏎⏎That's where I want to start, because I think HHS is framing this whole question wrong. The RFI asks how to accelerate AI adoption, which assumes acceleration is the goal. I'd like to push back on that assumption pretty hard.⏎⏎Medicine involves a lot of things that look like information problems but aren't. A patient who says "I feel fine" might mean they feel fine, or they might mean they've given up, or they might mean they're scared of what you'll do if they say otherwise. Clinicians learn to hear the difference over years of practice. That kind of interpretive work is not pattern matching on historical data. And I worry that the more we dress up AI recommendations in authoritative-looking interfaces, the more we train clinicians to stop trusting that interpretive instinct and start deferring to the machine. That shift will not show up in any accuracy metric. It will show up years later in outcomes we can't trace back to a cause.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem, which I don't think the field has come close to solving. Pulse oximeters were found to be less accurate on darker skin tones, and that was a physical device with a simple measurement task and it still took decades to get meaningful attention. AI systems trained on historical clinical data are inheriting every inequity baked into that history, who got admitted, who got believed, who got the aggressive treatment and who got sent home with ibuprofen. When those systems are wrong in ways that track race or income or disability status, the error looks like an objective output. That is genuinely more dangerous than a human bias because at least with a human you can argue back.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the employment question, because I think it keeps getting brushed aside. Radiology techs, medical coders, prior authorization reviewers, clinical documentation specialists, these are real jobs held by real people, many of whom spent years and real money on training and certification. The pitch that AI will free clinicians from administrative burden is probably accurate in some narrow sense, but "administrative burden" is somebody else's actual job. When a hospital cuts its coding department by 40 percent because a vendor promises their system can handle it, those aren't inefficiencies being eliminated. Those are livelihoods. And the accountability that goes with those human roles doesn't automatically transfer to the software that replaces them.⏎⏎What I keep coming back to is the question of who's responsible when something goes wrong. Right now if a doctor misses something, there is a person, a license, a malpractice framework, a professional relationship. If an AI system misses something and a hospital deployed it and a vendor sold it and the training data came from somewhere else entirely, the responsibility gets spread so thin it basically disappears. I've read some of the arguments that liability frameworks will evolve to handle this, and maybe they will, but "liability frameworks will evolve" is not a reassurance you'd accept in any other safety-critical domain.⏎⏎I'm not saying every AI application in healthcare is worthless. Scheduling software is probably fine. Flagging potential drug interactions in a pharmacy system, maybe. But the RFI doesn't distinguish between those relatively low-stakes administrative uses and things like diagnostic support, treatment recommendations, or insurance coverage determinations, all of which carry consequences that are serious enough to require a human being who is actually accountable in a meaningful way.⏎⏎On that last point about insurance specifically: the use of AI in prior authorization and claims denial decisions should not be lumped in with clinical care tools as if they're the same category of thing. Cigna made news in 2023 for a system that was reportedly denying claims at a rate of tens of thousands per hour with physicians spending an average of around 1.2 seconds per review. That's not AI assisting a human decision. That's a human providing legal cover for an automated denial. HHS has authority to address that and I think it should, but the answer is not to build better AI for it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0082♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Hernandez♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔Society of Hospital Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the ICU at Brigham and Women's Hospital for eleven days in February 2023, and I watched what it actually looks like when a human clinician makes a hard call. Her attending physician sat with us for forty minutes going over things that didn't fit neatly into any category -- her history with a particular medication, the way she described her chest pain, the fact that she'd been under enormous stress since my father died. None of that was in the chart. None of it would have been in any dataset. And I keep thinking about what would have happened if the recommendation had come from a system that only knew what was documented.⏎⏎I'm responding specifically to the questions in this RFI about what actions HHS can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, because I think the framing itself is the problem. The question assumes acceleration is the goal. I'd argue the department should be asking something harder: under what conditions, if any, should AI be making or influencing clinical recommendations at all, and who bears responsibility when it's wrong?⏎⏎The safety case against moving fast here is not abstract. AI systems trained on historical health data inherit the biases in that data. There's well-documented evidence -- the 2019 Obermeyer study in Science being probably the most cited -- that a widely used commercial algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, and Black patients had historically received less care. That's one study about one algorithm. We don't know how many systems like that are already deployed, because the transparency requirements don't exist yet, and this RFI seems more interested in getting more AI into clinics than in auditing what's already there.⏎⏎And I do understand the argument that AI could reduce burnout, catch things clinicians miss, help with documentation burden. I'm not dismissing that entirely. But there's a version of this that goes very badly, where hospitals use "AI-assisted care" as cover for cutting nursing staff, where insurers use algorithmic prior authorization to deny claims faster and with less human review, where the clinician becomes someone who rubber-stamps machine outputs because they're seeing thirty patients a day and there's no time to override. That's not a hypothetical. Prior authorization denial rates have been rising for years, and insurers have already begun deploying AI tools to manage that process. UnitedHealth's use of an AI model called nH Predict to deny post-acute care claims was the subject of a federal lawsuit in 2023. The model allegedly had a 90% error rate on appeal. That happened without HHS accelerating anything.⏎⏎The accountability problem is the thing I can't get past. When an AI system contributes to a bad outcome, the liability structure is genuinely unclear. The clinician can say they followed a recommendation, the hospital can say the vendor certified the tool, the vendor can say the clinician had final authority. That diffusion of responsibility is not a technical problem that better regulation can fully solve. It's built into the model. Medicine has always worked because there is a named human being who is accountable to you. Your doctor. Your surgeon. Someone you can look at and who has to look back at you.⏎⏎HHS should not be in the business of accelerating the erosion of that.⏎⏎What I'd actually want to see, if this comment accomplishes anything, is a requirement that any AI tool used in clinical decision-making or insurance determinations be subject to mandatory prospective auditing in the specific population where it's deployed, not just the population where it was validated. And the results of those audits should be public. That's the minimum. Before we talk about acceleration, we should talk about whether the systems already in use are doing what their vendors claim, and for which patients they are quietly failing.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0083♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Torres♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔Paige AI♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Riverside University Health System in Moreno Valley in March of 2023. She has a rare clotting disorder, speaks limited English, and was admitted after a fall that turned out to involve a subdural hematoma. I watched what it took for her care team to get that right. Her attending physician sat with us for forty minutes on the second day, not reviewing labs, not entering orders, just talking. Trying to understand what her baseline was, what she was afraid of, what she could and couldn't tell him about her symptoms. There is no way to overstate how much of that conversation was irreducibly human. She kept saying her head felt "full of water" and he knew, from context, from watching her face, from asking follow-up questions that weren't in any protocol, that she meant pressure, not dizziness. That distinction mattered clinically. An AI parsing her language would have had no idea what to do with that.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read about accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings. The word "accelerating" is doing a lot of work in this RFI. It assumes the direction is correct and the only question is speed. I don't think that's right.⏎⏎To the extent this RFI is asking what HHS should do to support AI in clinical care, my answer is: slow down considerably, and be honest about what isn't known yet. I want to focus on two things.⏎⏎First, the safety question is not settled and the current frameworks for evaluating these tools are not adequate. When a hospital deploys an AI-assisted diagnostic tool, there is often no requirement that it be validated on a population that resembles the patients actually being treated. A system trained predominantly on records from large academic medical centers in the Northeast may perform very differently at a rural critical access hospital in Mississippi or a safety-net hospital serving recent immigrants. We already know from published research, including the 2019 Obermeyer et al. study in Science on a widely used commercial algorithm, that these tools can embed racial bias in ways that aren't visible from the outside. That study showed a specific commercial health management algorithm was systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients. That wasn't a hypothetical risk. That was deployed, in real hospitals, affecting real people. And the vendor's response was essentially to update the tool quietly. There was no recall, no mandatory reporting, no clear accountability.⏎⏎HHS should not be accelerating adoption into that regulatory vacuum. Before any new incentives or reimbursement pathways are created for AI clinical tools, there should be mandatory prospective validation requirements, demographic breakdown of performance data, and a clear post-market surveillance mechanism with teeth. The FDA's existing frameworks for software as a medical device are not keeping pace with how quickly these tools are being deployed and updated, and ONC's certification program was not designed with this in mind.⏎⏎Second, and honestly I'm not sure this gets discussed enough in the official framing, there's the question of what AI does to the clinician-patient relationship over time even when it technically works. If a physician knows that an AI flagged something before they did, or that the AI's recommendation is sitting in the chart, that changes how they think. There's research on automation bias going back decades. Clinicians defer to systems even when they have good reason not to. A study published in JAMA Network Open in 2021 found that clinician decision-making shifted measurably when AI recommendations were present, even when those recommendations were wrong. That's not a bug that gets patched. That's a cognitive dynamic that becomes baked into clinical culture.⏎⏎The reimbursement angle worries me too. If CMS creates billing codes or quality incentives tied to AI tool use, hospitals will adopt those tools regardless of whether they improve care, because the financial signal will push them to. We've seen this pattern before with electronic health records. The incentive structure drove adoption faster than the evidence supported and we are still dealing with the usability and burnout consequences of that.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in medicine ever. Radiology screening, certain genomic analyses, some administrative functions, maybe. But the framing of this RFI treats hesitation as an obstacle to overcome rather than a reasonable response to genuine uncertainty. And the populations most likely to be harmed by poorly validated AI tools are the same populations who already have the least power to push back when something goes wrong, elderly patients in nursing facilities, people with disabilities, people who don't speak English as a first language, people whose medical histories are thin because they've had limited access to care throughout their lives and whose data is therefore underrepresented in every training set these tools are built on.⏎⏎My mother's doctor doesn't use any AI-assisted diagnostic tools that I know of. He's been practicing for about twenty-six years. I don't think that's a coincidence.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0084♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔White♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Regions Hospital in St. Paul in February of 2023, and not once during that time did any machine tell us what was really happening. It was a night-shift nurse named Darnell who noticed she was frightened rather than confused, and that distinction changed her treatment. I don't know how you train an algorithm to see that. I'm not sure you can.⏎⏎That's the thing that keeps coming back to me when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings. The word "accelerate" is doing a lot of work in that framing. Accelerate toward what, exactly? Toward a system that processes patients faster? I'm not against efficiency but medicine isn't a throughput problem, and I worry that framing it that way, even implicitly, does real damage to what people actually need when they're sick and scared.⏎⏎The question I want to address in this comment is the one about what barriers exist to AI adoption, because I think HHS is asking that question backwards. The barriers aren't the problem. Some of the barriers are there for good reasons and removing them without understanding why they exist seems reckless.⏎⏎One barrier is clinician skepticism, and I'd argue that skepticism is rational. There's a study from 2021, I believe it was published in JAMA Internal Medicine, that looked at a sepsis prediction algorithm deployed at University of Michigan and found that the tool flagged so many false positives that nurses were largely ignoring it within months of deployment. The tool was technically "accurate" in some statistical sense but it was creating alert fatigue, which is itself a patient safety problem. So you have a situation where an AI system designed to save lives may have made the environment slightly more dangerous, and the response from the vendor was not to withdraw the product but to say the hospital needed better "implementation." That kind of thing is why frontline workers don't trust these systems. And they shouldn't have to trust them just because the vendor's slides look impressive.⏎⏎What concerns me almost as much as the direct safety issues is what AI does to the structure of accountability. When a physician makes a bad call, there is a person who made that call. They can be questioned, retrained, disciplined, sued. When an AI system contributes to a bad outcome, that chain of responsibility gets very murky very fast. The hospital says they followed the vendor's guidance, the vendor says the hospital deployed it wrong, the physician says they were just acting on the recommendation. Patients and families get left with no clear answer about what happened or why. That isn't a hypothetical, it's already happening in prior authorization contexts where insurers are using AI tools to deny claims and nobody can explain the reasoning in human terms.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the employment angle because I think it gets dismissed too quickly as just being about protecting jobs. It's not only about that. When you reduce the number of skilled humans involved in care decisions, you reduce the number of people who are in a position to notice something is wrong and say so. A hospital that has replaced its utilization review nurses with an automated system has also eliminated the people who might catch a systematic error in that system. There's a kind of institutional knowledge that lives in people, not in data, and once you've cut those positions you don't easily get that back.⏎⏎The administrative functions question is one where I'd push back especially hard on HHS's apparent assumption that AI is neutral or beneficial. Prior authorization is a good example. The argument for automating it is that it's time-consuming and expensive. That's true. But prior authorization also requires judgment about individual circumstances, and the history of insurance companies using automated tools in this space is not encouraging. In 2023, a ProPublica investigation found that Cigna's automated denial system was being used to reject claims without physician review, sometimes in under a minute per case. That's not efficiency. That's a company using the appearance of technological sophistication to avoid the cost of actually evaluating claims.⏎⏎I understand there are people who genuinely believe AI will improve care and I'm not saying they're all wrong about everything. Some narrow applications probably do help. But HHS is asking how to accelerate adoption broadly, and I think the honest answer is that the pace of adoption should be determined by evidence of benefit and by the capacity of oversight systems to catch problems, not by vendor enthusiasm or by the fact that some hospitals have already bought the tools and want to justify the purchase.⏎⏎The regulatory infrastructure for evaluating these tools in real-world clinical settings is honestly pretty thin right now. The FDA's framework for software as a medical device is still being worked out, post-market surveillance for AI tools is not well established, and there's no consistent requirement that hospitals publicly report adverse events linked to AI-assisted decisions. Before HHS does anything to remove barriers to adoption it should probably fix that situation first, because right now we are deploying systems at scale without the monitoring infrastructure to know whether they're helping or♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0085♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Martin♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔Center for Science in the Public Interest♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Emory University Hospital in February of 2023, and what kept her alive was not an algorithm. It was a night-shift nurse named Darnell who noticed she was breathing slightly differently at 2am and called the attending before the monitors showed anything worth flagging. That kind of judgment does not come from pattern matching on historical data. I don't know how you would even begin to train a model on what Darnell knew in that moment.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS is looking for ways to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, my honest reaction is: slow down. Please. Not because I'm against technology in general, but because I think the department is asking the wrong question. The question isn't how do we speed this up. The question is whether any particular use of AI actually makes care better for the patient in front of the clinician, not better on average across a dataset, not more efficient from a billing standpoint, better for that specific scared person in that specific room.⏎⏎What concerns me most about this RFI is what it doesn't ask. It doesn't ask what happens when AI gets it wrong. It doesn't ask who is liable. It doesn't ask how a patient is supposed to know that a recommendation their doctor just made was generated or filtered through a commercial AI product that the hospital licensed from a vendor with no particular accountability to that patient. There are already documented cases of AI-based tools used in prior authorization decisions that have denial rates far above what any individual physician reviewer would approve. ProPublica and others have reported on UnitedHealth's use of an algorithm called nH Predict that apparently generated 90-day post-acute care limits that physicians and patients couldn't effectively challenge. That is not a hypothetical risk. That is already happening.⏎⏎And I think that's where the administrative uses of AI deserve more scrutiny than they're getting. I know the argument is that automating prior auths and billing and scheduling frees up clinicians to focus on patients. Maybe. But those administrative functions, as tedious as they are, involve human beings making judgment calls about individual circumstances. A biller who has worked a department for ten years knows things that are not in the structured fields of a claim form. An intake coordinator knows that a particular patient is homeless and that the address on file is a shelter and that the discharge plan needs to account for that. I'm genuinely not sure an AI system picks that up, and I'm even less sure that the hospitals deploying these systems are asking whether it does.⏎⏎There's also a workforce question that I don't think the department is taking seriously enough. If AI reduces the need for medical coders, for care coordinators, for certain categories of nursing support staff, where do those people go? These are often jobs held by women, by people of color, by workers without four-year degrees who found stable employment with benefits in healthcare. The efficiency gains from AI will accrue to health systems and vendors. The job losses will fall on specific people in specific communities. That tradeoff deserves at minimum an honest accounting, and this RFI doesn't really ask for one.⏎⏎I want to be fair: I'm not saying there's no role for AI tools in medicine ever. If a radiologist can catch a tumor she might otherwise have missed because a model flagged something in a scan, that seems worth exploring carefully. But "exploring carefully" and "accelerating adoption" are not the same thing, and right now HHS seems much more interested in the latter. The incentives here are not neutral. Vendors want to sell products. Health systems want to cut costs. And the patients and workers who bear the risk of getting this wrong don't have nearly as much voice in this process as the people who stand to profit from it.⏎⏎I'd ask the department specifically to consider requiring pre-deployment audits for any AI system used in coverage or denial decisions, with results that are publicly available and broken down by race, age, insurance type, and diagnosis category. Not after the fact. Before the system goes live. And I'd ask that any comment process like this one be extended and actively publicized to patient advocacy groups, labor unions representing healthcare workers, and community health organizations, because right now the responses to RFIs like this one skew heavily toward industry and academic institutions that already have grant relationships with the department.⏎⏎I'm not sure that recommendation fits neatly into any of the specific questions HHS posed, but I'd rather say something that matters than answer the questions as written.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0086♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Jackson♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔National Council on Aging♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Advocate Lutheran General Hospital in Park Ridge, Illinois in February of 2023, and what saved her was not an algorithm. It was a night-shift nurse named Darnell who noticed she was holding her jaw funny and kept asking questions nobody had thought to ask yet. That is the thing I keep coming back to whenever I read materials like this one.⏎⏎The question HHS is really asking here, even if it is not phrased this way, is how fast and how far should we go. And I think that framing already concedes too much. The whole document is tilted toward acceleration. The assumption built into every question is that adoption is good and the only problem is removing friction. I would ask HHS to sit with a different possibility, which is that the friction is sometimes the point.⏎⏎What worries me most is the question about reimbursement incentives. If Medicare and Medicaid start paying more for AI-assisted diagnoses or AI-assisted prior authorizations, you are not just encouraging a tool, you are restructuring the economic logic of medicine around it. Hospitals that are already running on thin margins will not use those incentives to add staff. They will use them to justify cutting staff. I have seen nothing in this RFI that seriously grapples with that. There is language about workforce considerations but it reads like a footnote.⏎⏎The bias problem also does not get enough weight here. Training data in healthcare is not neutral. It reflects decades of unequal access, underdiagnosis in certain populations, overdiagnosis in others. A system trained on that data will reproduce those patterns and do it confidently, which is worse than a human doing it because at least a human can be questioned and can feel shame. I am not sure an AI system deployed at scale in a rural critical access hospital in, say, eastern Kentucky is going to perform the way it performed in the academic medical center where it was validated. Those are not the same populations and they are not the same conditions.⏎⏎I do want to be honest that I am not opposed to every possible use of AI in healthcare. I think there are narrow administrative applications that probably do not raise these concerns in the same way. Scheduling. Maybe transcription of notes if the clinician retains full control and review. But the moment we start talking about AI informing clinical decisions, recommending treatments, or flagging insurance denials, we are in a different category entirely and I think HHS is treating all of this as a spectrum when it should be drawing much sharper lines.⏎⏎The accountability problem is the one I keep circling back to. When Darnell made a judgment call about my mother, he was accountable. His name was in the chart. His license was on the line. His supervisor could have been called. If an AI system had flagged or not flagged the same thing, who answers for that? The vendor will say it depends how the hospital configured it. The hospital will say it depends on the clinician's override options. The clinician will say they were following the system's recommendation. I have read enough about the Epic deterioration model and the controversy around its sepsis predictions to know this is not hypothetical hand-wringing. There have already been cases where these tools were deployed widely before anyone really knew how they worked in practice, and the institutions involved were not forthcoming about the error rates.⏎⏎HHS should not be in the business of accelerating adoption of tools that have not resolved these accountability questions. I would strongly urge the Department to consider a moratorium on new reimbursement incentives tied to AI clinical decision support until there is a mandatory independent auditing framework in place, with results that are public and searchable by hospital name.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0087♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Ramirez♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023. She was 74, and her presentation was confusing enough that the team spent the first 48 hours running down possibilities before a senior hospitalist sat with her for about forty minutes, asked questions nobody had thought to ask, and figured out what was actually going on. It wasn't in her chart. It wasn't in any data. It came out in conversation because she trusted this particular doctor and finally told him something she'd been embarrassed to mention. I think about that constantly when I read materials like this RFI.⏎⏎The framing of this request for information bothers me. The question isn't really "should we accelerate AI adoption" — it's structured so that the answer is already assumed to be yes, and the public is just being asked how fast and in what ways. That kind of framing closes off the more important question, which is whether clinical care is even the right place for these tools at all, at least in their current state and deployment context.⏎⏎What I keep coming back to is the accountability problem. When my mother's care went sideways initially, there was a doctor accountable for that. A person. Someone who could be questioned, who felt responsible, who in fact stayed late to correct course. If an AI triage tool or a diagnostic support system had flagged the wrong condition at the outset and the team had anchored on that — which is a well-documented cognitive risk, anchoring bias doesn't go away just because the initial suggestion came from a computer — who answers for it? The vendor says the clinician had final authority. The clinician says the tool said X and they followed standard protocol. The hospital says the tool was FDA cleared. Nobody is actually responsible. That diffusion of accountability is genuinely dangerous and I don't think this RFI takes it seriously enough.⏎⏎There's also something that gets almost no attention in these conversations, which is what happens to the clinician's judgment over time when they are routinely prompted by AI recommendations. There's research going back to automation bias studies in aviation — pilots who flew with increasingly automated systems showed degraded manual flying skills when those systems failed. Medicine isn't the same as aviation but the principle isn't crazy. If a nurse practitioner spends years deferring to a risk-stratification algorithm for every patient, what happens to her ability to recognize the patient who doesn't fit the model? That's not hypothetical. I genuinely don't know the answer, but I am suspicious that we're about to run a massive uncontrolled experiment on an entire clinical workforce and call it progress.⏎⏎The RFI asks about regulatory and reimbursement actions HHS can take to accelerate adoption. My answer to that specific question is: don't. Or at least, stop. Before creating pathways that make it easier to bill for AI-assisted care or that create incentives for health systems to deploy these tools widely, there needs to be a much more rigorous pre-deployment evaluation requirement. Not post-market surveillance after the tool is already embedded in two hundred hospitals. Actual prospective evidence that the tool performs as claimed in the population and setting where it will actually be used, not just in the academic medical centers where it was validated.⏎⏎The bias issue deserves its own paragraph because I think people underestimate how hard it is to detect. A 2019 study published in Science found that a widely used commercial algorithm used to allocate healthcare resources systematically underestimated the needs of Black patients, not because anyone programmed it to be discriminatory, but because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients historically received less care. That algorithm was running for years before anyone caught it. HHS knows about this study. It's been cited extensively. And yet the RFI reads as though the main challenge is just getting more data and moving faster. I'd argue the lesson is almost exactly the opposite.⏎⏎I'm also skeptical of the administrative AI argument, the idea that even if clinical AI is complicated, surely we can agree that AI handling prior authorizations or scheduling is fine. But prior authorization decisions affect whether people get care. The humans who currently do that work, whatever you think of the insurance industry, are at least reachable, trainable, and subject to state insurance commissioner oversight. An automated denial system sitting inside a black box managed by a vendor under a proprietary license is none of those things. And there are already lawsuits — UnitedHealth's nH Predict system, which used a 90% error rate (according to a Senate investigation) to deny post-acute care claims — that suggest this is not a theoretical risk.⏎⏎I don't think I'm anti-technology. I'm writing this on a computer. My mother's imaging was read partly with computer-assisted detection and I'm glad it was. But there's a difference between a tool that assists a human who retains full ownership of the decision and a system that is making recommendations that clinicians, under time pressure and cognitive load, are going to follow most of the time regardless of what the consent forms or the liability waivers say about "final clinical judgment."⏎⏎HHS should require, at minimum, that any AI tool used in clinical decision support or insurance determination be subject to independent third-party auditing for both accuracy and bias, with results publicly posted. Not vendor-conducted validation. Not post-market reporting that takes three years to surface problems. And there should be an explicit right for patients to request human review of any AI-influenced decision, and that right needs to be real and not buried in a portal that requires three logins and a♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0088♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Clark♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔Booz Allen Hamilton♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's Hospital in Kansas City in February 2023, and what kept her alive was not a pattern-matching algorithm. It was a night-shift nurse named Danielle who noticed that my mother was holding her breath slightly when she answered questions, a behavioral cue that told Danielle something was wrong with the pain management even though my mother kept saying she was fine. You cannot train a dataset on that. You cannot label it. That kind of perception is built from years of being in rooms with sick people and caring enough to keep watching.⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the question HHS is asking about what regulatory actions could accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, because I think the question itself is framed wrong. The Department is asking how to go faster. I think the more responsible question is whether going faster serves patients or serves the vendors and health systems that have already made large capital investments in these tools and need utilization to justify them.⏎⏎The safety argument against rushing this is pretty straightforward. AI systems trained on historical health records inherit all the inequities baked into who got diagnosed with what, who got taken seriously, who got sent home too early. There is documented evidence going back to a 2019 paper in Science by Obermeyer and colleagues showing that a widely used commercial algorithm systematically underestimated illness severity in Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients had historically received less care. That is not a fringe concern. That is a peer-reviewed finding about a tool that was already deployed at scale before anyone caught it. And that was a relatively simple algorithm. The systems being discussed now are far more complex and far harder to audit.⏎⏎What worries me about the framing of this RFI is that it treats AI adoption as inherently good and asks only about removing obstacles. But some of those "obstacles" are actually safeguards. Clinician skepticism is not a barrier to be overcome through better marketing or training programs. It might be appropriate skepticism based on direct experience with tools that don't work as advertised in real clinical conditions. The fact that a tool performs well in a controlled study or a well-resourced academic medical center does not mean it will work in a rural critical access hospital with incomplete EHR data and staff who are already stretched thin.⏎⏎There is also something that I think gets glossed over in these discussions, which is what happens to accountability when AI is in the loop. Right now, if a physician misses something, there is a chain of responsibility. It is not a perfect system, obviously, but it exists. When an AI system recommends against a treatment and a clinician defers to it and the patient is harmed, who is responsible? The clinician who deferred? The hospital that deployed the system? The vendor who trained the model? I have not seen a satisfactory answer to this from anyone promoting these tools, and the fact that HHS is considering accelerating adoption before that question is settled is genuinely alarming.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the administrative side of this because I think it gets underdiscussed. There is a lot of enthusiasm about using AI for prior authorization and claims review, which sounds like it would reduce administrative burden. But if you look at how insurers have already deployed automated decision-making in prior auth, the results are not good. ProPublica and other outlets have reported on UnitedHealth's nH Predict tool, which was used to deny post-acute care to Medicare Advantage patients based on algorithmic predictions, and there are active lawsuits about this. That is what AI-assisted administrative decision-making looks like in practice when the incentives are to deny rather than approve. Speeding up that process does not help patients.⏎⏎I am not saying technology has no place in medicine. I use a continuous glucose monitor, and it has changed my life. But there is a difference between a tool that gives a patient more information about their own body and a tool that intermediates between a patient and the clinical judgment of another human being. HHS should be asking much harder questions about where that line is before writing policy that pushes institutions toward faster adoption on the assumption that more AI is categorically better.⏎⏎The question of who benefits economically from accelerated adoption is one I wish this RFI had asked more directly.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0089♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Lopez♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in March of 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that her primary care doctor almost missed. What saved her, honestly, was a nurse named Patricia who noticed something was off about how my mother was holding her arm and pushed back on the attending's initial read. Not a test. Not a score. A person who had been doing this for twenty years and had learned to notice things that don't show up in a chart. I keep thinking about that when I read about AI being used to flag sepsis risk, which sounds good in theory, but what happens to Patricia's role when there's a sepsis algorithm running and everyone assumes the machine is already on top of it?⏎⏎That's really the question I want HHS to sit with before it does anything to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings. The word "accelerate" is doing a lot of work in this request for information. It presupposes that faster adoption is better, and I don't think that's been established at all.⏎⏎On the specific question of what regulatory actions HHS should take: I'd argue the burden of proof has to be much higher than it currently seems to be. Right now, a hospital can deploy a clinical decision support tool and if it's positioned in a certain way, it doesn't even require FDA clearance. The result is that hospitals are buying products that were validated in one population, in one health system, sometimes years ago, and using them on patients who look nothing like the training data. There was a widely reported study from 2019 looking at a commercial algorithm used by health systems across the country that systematically underestimated the care needs of Black patients because it used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need. That kind of bias doesn't announce itself. The clinician sees a score, the score seems authoritative, and the disparity gets baked in deeper than it was before.⏎⏎HHS should not be looking for ways to reduce regulatory friction for these tools. If anything, the post-market surveillance requirements are too weak and the pre-deployment validation standards are inconsistent. I understand there's pressure from the vendor community and from health systems that have already bought these products to make the regulatory environment friendlier, but that pressure shouldn't drive federal policy.⏎⏎The insurance side of this worries me even more than the clinical side, actually. AI-driven prior authorization denials are already a serious problem and the volume is only going up. When a clinician recommends a treatment and an automated system denies it with no explanation that a real person can engage with, that's not efficiency, that's a power shift. The clinician loses standing. The patient loses their advocate. And when they appeal, they're often appealing to a process that's also partly automated or staffed by people who have no authority to deviate from whatever the algorithm decided.⏎⏎I don't think HHS can or should try to ban AI entirely and I'm not sure that's a coherent position anyway. But "accelerating adoption" as a policy goal, without first having much stronger standards for what adoption means, is the wrong framing. The question should be something more like: under what conditions is AI safe to use in a given clinical context, and who is accountable when it's wrong?⏎⏎I'd also want to flag the workforce piece, which I don't think gets enough attention in these RFI processes. Radiologists, pathologists, medical coders, prior auth coordinators, these are real careers with real training pipelines. The assumption that AI will "augment" rather than replace workers in these roles is mostly coming from the people selling the AI. The economic incentives point clearly toward replacement wherever possible, and health systems that are already operating on thin margins will follow those incentives. Whether that produces better care for patients is a separate question that nobody seems to be seriously asking.⏎⏎Patricia still works at Mercy General as far as I know. I hope she still has a job that lets her notice things.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0090♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Hernandez♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔Johns Hopkins Malone Center for Engineering in Healthcare♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the ICU at St. Francis Medical Center in Peoria for eleven days in February 2023. During that time, I watched nurses and doctors make decisions that no algorithm could have made, not because the data was missing but because the data wasn't the point. She couldn't describe her pain clearly. She was frightened and sometimes combative. Her history was complicated in ways that her chart didn't capture. What kept her alive and what eventually got her home wasn't pattern matching. It was a pulmonologist who sat with her for forty minutes and listened to things that weren't in any database. I keep thinking about what her care would have looked like if that doctor had been nudged, prompted, second-guessed, or time-pressured by a system telling him what the most likely diagnosis was.⏎⏎That's the thing I want HHS to sit with before it accelerates anything. The word "accelerate" in this RFI worries me as much as anything else in it. There is no clinical emergency requiring us to move faster on AI adoption. The emergency, if there is one, runs in the other direction: we are at risk of deploying systems whose failure modes we don't understand into settings where errors can kill people, and we are doing it because vendors are selling hard and hospital administrators are looking for ways to cut costs while calling it innovation.⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the question about what barriers exist to AI adoption, because I think the framing is backwards. The question treats caution as a problem to be removed. But some of those barriers are there for good reasons. Clinicians who are skeptical of AI recommendations are not being obstructionist. They are doing their jobs. A nurse who overrides an alert because she knows her patient isn't like the population the model was trained on is exercising exactly the kind of judgment that makes care safe. If HHS's goal is to reduce that friction, it should explain very carefully why, because from where I sit, that friction is often the last line of defense.⏎⏎There's also something that doesn't get discussed enough, which is what happens when these tools are wrong in ways that look right. An AI system that confidently produces a plausible-looking but incorrect recommendation is more dangerous than one that obviously fails. And the history of clinical decision support tools, even before the current generation of machine learning, is full of alert fatigue, ignored recommendations, and systems that were validated in one context and deployed in twenty others. There's a 2019 study from Vanderbilt that found clinicians overrode drug-interaction alerts at a rate above 90 percent, not because the clinicians were reckless but because the alerts were so poorly calibrated that ignoring them had become rational behavior. I'm not sure the newer AI tools are fundamentally different in that respect.⏎⏎The employment and labor dimension also deserves a direct answer from HHS rather than being treated as a side issue. If AI tools are adopted in administrative roles, in prior authorization, in coding, in care coordination, those are jobs held by real people who have built expertise over years. The argument that AI will free up workers for higher-value tasks has been made about every automation wave in history and it has been wrong often enough that it deserves skepticism, not assumption. I haven't seen HHS ask how many healthcare workers might lose their jobs or have their roles degraded as a result of the adoption it wants to accelerate, and that omission is noticeable.⏎⏎The accountability question is the one I find hardest to let go of. When something goes wrong in medicine, there has to be someone responsible. Not a system, not a vendor, not a hospital policy that says the clinician should have caught it. A person. AI makes that harder in ways that I don't think have been solved, and I'm not sure they can be solved with disclosure requirements or audit logs. If a physician makes a decision partly because an AI told him to, and the patient is harmed, the legal and moral accountability becomes genuinely murky in ways that could take years of litigation to sort out, and the patient or their family bears the cost of that murkiness in the meantime.⏎⏎I recognize that AI tools might help in some narrow, well-defined, carefully monitored circumstances. I'm not saying there's no possible application anywhere. But the question HHS is asking is how to accelerate adoption broadly, and that's a different question, and the answer to that one is: slowly, with much more rigorous pre-deployment evaluation than currently exists, with mandatory post-market surveillance that has real teeth, and with explicit protections for clinicians who decline to follow AI recommendations when their clinical judgment says otherwise. There's currently no federal standard requiring that a clinician be able to override an AI recommendation without it being flagged as a deviation from protocol or used against them in a productivity review, and that gap alone should give HHS pause before it does anything to make deployment faster.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0091♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Martinez♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔American Society of Anesthesiologists♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Piedmont Atlanta Hospital in February 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that got missed for almost 36 hours. Part of what delayed the diagnosis was that the clinical decision support tool flagged her presentation as low-risk. The nurses at bedside were suspicious. They kept saying something was wrong. But the tool said otherwise and the attending on duty deferred to it. I am not saying AI killed my mother — she survived, thank God — but I am saying that what I saw in that hospital was not a system where the machine assisted human judgment. It was a system where human judgment was waiting for permission from the machine.⏎⏎That experience is why I am responding to this RFI, specifically to the questions about what regulatory steps HHS should take to accelerate adoption. My answer is: please slow down instead.⏎⏎I understand the argument for AI in healthcare. I have heard it many times. Burnout is real, physician shortages are real, diagnostic errors happen constantly and some of them could theoretically be caught by pattern recognition tools. I do not think everyone who wants to use AI in clinical settings is acting in bad faith. But I think the framing of this RFI, which is built around the word "accelerate," starts from an assumption that more AI faster is obviously the right direction, and I don't think that assumption has been earned.⏎⏎Medicine is not primarily an information processing problem. When my mother was in the ICU, the nurses who were worried about her were picking up on things that no dataset captures well — the way she was breathing, the look on her face, a gut feeling built from years of watching sick people. That is not mysticism. That is clinical expertise. And what concerns me about embedding AI deeper into clinical workflows is that over time, that kind of expertise gets devalued. Clinicians start to second-guess themselves when the tool disagrees. Hospitals start to see clinicians who trust their own judgment as liabilities rather than assets.⏎⏎There is also an accountability problem that I do not think HHS is taking seriously enough in how this RFI is framed. When the tool is wrong, who is responsible? The vendor will say the hospital misused the product. The hospital will say the clinician should have overridden it. The clinician will say they were trained to trust it. I watched something like this play out firsthand. No one was clearly responsible for the 36-hour delay. Everyone had a piece of the process, and the AI tool was sort of floating above all of it, not really accountable to anyone.⏎⏎The economic piece matters too and I don't think it gets discussed honestly. Hospitals and insurers are not going to use AI primarily to make care better. They are going to use it to cut costs. We already see this with prior authorization — automated denial systems that reject claims without meaningful clinical review. Patients with chronic conditions are spending hours on the phone fighting decisions made by algorithms. That is not hypothetical. The American Medical Association put out data in 2023 showing that 94 percent of physicians reported prior authorization delays causing care delays for patients. AI is going to make that worse, not better, because it scales the denial capacity without scaling the appeals process or the human oversight.⏎⏎I keep coming back to the question of what we are actually optimizing for. If the answer is throughput and cost per encounter, then yes, AI probably helps. But if the answer is whether patients feel that a human being is genuinely responsible for their care and will fight for them when something goes wrong, I am not sure AI helps at all. It might actively make that worse.⏎⏎The specific regulatory action I would urge HHS to take is a moratorium on the use of AI in insurance coverage decisions until there is a functioning, independent audit process with real enforcement teeth — not voluntary best practices, not self-reported vendor metrics. And for clinical settings, any deployment should require prospective validation in the specific patient population where it will actually be used, not just aggregate performance data from a training set that may not look anything like the community hospital in rural Georgia where the tool eventually gets installed.⏎⏎There is probably more to say about the liability framework and how CMS reimbursement structures create perverse incentives for hospitals to adopt tools before they're ready, but I don't have the regulatory expertise to lay that out in detail. What I have is the experience of sitting in an ICU waiting room while my mother got sicker because a computer said she was fine, and I don't think♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0092♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Nguyen♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Regions Hospital in St. Paul in February 2023, and I sat with her through most of it. What I watched the nurses and doctors do was not pattern matching. It was something else entirely. The attending physician, an older woman who had been practicing for probably thirty years, spent twenty minutes one afternoon just watching my mother breathe, not looking at a screen, not consulting anything, just watching. She changed a medication order after that. I don't know how to put that into a dataset.⏎⏎I understand that HHS is trying to figure out how to speed up AI adoption in clinical settings, and I want to respond specifically to the question about what barriers to adoption exist and whether those barriers should be removed. My answer is that some of those barriers exist for very good reasons, and the framing of this RFI, which treats adoption speed as a goal in itself, worries me.⏎⏎Here is what I keep coming back to: the safety record for AI diagnostic and decision-support tools is genuinely uneven, and the studies showing good performance are often done in controlled conditions that don't look much like the hospitals where most people actually receive care. A 2021 analysis published in The Lancet Digital Health looked at something like 130 published clinical AI algorithms and found that only a small fraction had been tested in real deployment conditions rather than retrospective data. That's not a minor technical footnote. That's a fundamental problem with knowing whether any of this actually works when a tired nurse at 3am in a short-staffed unit is the one supposed to be catching what the algorithm misses.⏎⏎The insurance piece is maybe even more concerning to me than the clinical piece, though they're connected. Automated prior authorization systems already deny care at rates that human reviewers wouldn't, and when those denials get appealed, they're overturned the majority of the time, which tells you something. UnitedHealth Group's use of an AI model called nH Predict to deny post-acute care claims was the subject of a federal lawsuit filed in 2023, where the allegation was that the system had a 90 percent overturn rate on appeal but continued to be used anyway. If HHS accelerates AI adoption in administrative functions without first requiring that these systems meet some minimum standard of accuracy and explainability, we are just going to see more of that, at larger scale, faster.⏎⏎I'm not someone who thinks all technology is bad. I use electronic health records every day in my work, I'm not a Luddite. But there is a difference between tools that support clinical judgment and tools that replace it or that get used to justify cost cutting dressed up as efficiency. The hospitals and insurers that are most eager to deploy AI are often not the ones most concerned about outcomes for low-income or elderly patients. They're concerned about margin.⏎⏎What I would ask HHS to consider is whether the right question right now is really how to accelerate adoption, or whether it's how to make sure that whatever gets adopted actually works, doesn't embed existing disparities into automated systems that are much harder to challenge than a human reviewer, and preserves some meaningful form of accountability when things go wrong. Right now if a physician makes a mistake there is a process, imperfect as it is, for understanding what happened and holding someone responsible. When an algorithm makes a mistake the vendor says it was used off-label, the hospital says it was following the vendor's guidance, and the clinician says they were just following the recommendation. I've talked to patient advocates who deal with exactly this situation and they describe it as hitting a wall.⏎⏎The question of who is actually accountable when an AI system contributes to a bad outcome has not been answered. I don't think adoption should be accelerated before it is.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0093♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Gonzalez♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔Society for Women's Health Research♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Riverside University Health System in Moreno Valley in October 2023, and what kept her alive was not an algorithm. It was a night-shift nurse named Darlene who noticed something was off about how my mother was breathing and flagged the attending at 2 in the morning before any monitor alarmed. That kind of judgment is not a data pattern. I don't know how you would even begin to train a model on it.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS is looking for ways to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my first reaction is honestly fear. Not fear of technology in general, I use technology constantly, but fear of what happens when hospitals and insurers get ahold of a tool that lets them justify decisions that used to require a human being to be accountable for.⏎⏎The question I want to address is the one about what barriers exist to AI adoption and whether HHS should act to remove them. My answer is that some of those barriers are there for good reasons, and the Department should be very careful before treating every obstacle as a problem to be solved.⏎⏎Take prior authorization. Insurers are already using automated systems to deny claims at enormous scale, and there is documented evidence, including a 2023 Senate investigation into UnitedHealth Group's nH Predict tool, that these systems deny post-acute care at rates that a physician reviewer would not replicate. When that gets called an AI efficiency gain, what it actually is is a transfer of decision-making power away from clinicians and patients and toward a system that nobody can really interrogate. The clinician can appeal, technically, but the appeals process is designed to exhaust people. Making that faster and more automated does not improve care. It just makes the denial faster.⏎⏎I keep thinking about what it means for a patient to feel that a human being is responsible for their care. When things go wrong in medicine, and they do go wrong, what matters enormously is that there is a person who is answerable. A doctor who makes a mistake can be questioned, disciplined, sued, can feel guilt, can learn. What happens when the recommendation came from a vendor's model trained on data from a different hospital system in a different region, deployed under a license agreement that limits liability? The hospital says the vendor made the tool. The vendor says the clinician should have overridden it. The clinician says they were trained to defer to the system's outputs under the hospital's protocol. Nobody is responsible. That is not a hypothetical, there are already cases working through state courts with exactly that structure.⏎⏎And I do want to say something about the workforce piece because I think it gets dismissed too quickly. The argument that AI will free nurses and doctors from administrative burdens so they can focus on patients sounds good. But hospitals are businesses. If an AI system allows a hospital to process the same number of patients with fewer billing coders, fewer intake coordinators, fewer case managers, the likely outcome is not that those workers get reassigned to patient care. The likely outcome is that those positions are eliminated. The people who do those jobs are disproportionately women, disproportionately people of color, and they are often the people who have the most sustained contact with patients navigating a confusing system. Losing them is not a neutral administrative efficiency.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require that any AI tool used in clinical or insurance decision-making be subject to ongoing post-deployment auditing with results made publicly available, not just submitted to the vendor. The FDA's current framework for software as a medical device is not adequate for this, and the gap between what gets validated in a controlled study and what gets deployed in a community hospital in a rural county is enormous. I've read some of the ONC interoperability work and I understand there are certification pathways being discussed, but certification at the point of deployment tells you very little about how a system performs six months later on a different patient population.⏎⏎There is also the question of what happens to patients who can't navigate an AI-mediated system. My mother speaks primarily Spanish and has limited health literacy. When I wasn't there, she had real difficulty communicating with the care team even with human interpreters available. An AI triage tool or a chatbot-based symptom checker is going to fail her in ways that are hard to measure and easy to ignore in aggregate accuracy statistics.⏎⏎I am not saying no AI ever in any context. I'm saying the framing of this RFI, which treats acceleration as the goal and barriers as friction to be cleared, has the logic backwards. The question should be which uses of AI genuinely improve outcomes for patients, particularly vulnerable patients, and what protections need to be in place before deployment, not after a harm has already occurred. Right now the incentive structure rewards vendors for selling and hospitals for buying, and neither of them bears the cost when the system fails on an edge case that wasn't in the training data.⏎⏎The auditing requirement I mentioned above should also apply retroactively to systems already in use. Many hospitals deployed ambient documentation tools and clinical decision support tools during and after the pandemic under emergency conditions and those tools have never been formally evaluated in the populations where they're now being used every day.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0094♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Sanchez♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔Cigna♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in 2022 after a sepsis diagnosis that was initially missed. What saved her wasn't a better algorithm. It was a night-shift nurse named Theresa who noticed something was off about how my mother was holding her arm and pushed back on the attending until he ordered a second blood culture. I keep thinking about that when I read materials like this RFI, which seems to assume that the main problem with American healthcare is that there isn't enough computational power involved.⏎⏎The question HHS is essentially asking is how to accelerate AI adoption, which already embeds an assumption I'd push back on hard. The question isn't how fast we can deploy these tools. The question is whether deploying them at scale actually improves patient outcomes, and for which patients, and under what conditions, and who is accountable when it goes wrong. Those questions are getting skipped.⏎⏎On the safety and effectiveness piece specifically: I am genuinely skeptical that the current evidence base justifies the enthusiasm here. A lot of the published validation studies for clinical AI tools are done by the same institutions or vendors that developed the tools, on patient populations that look nothing like a rural Medicaid patient in Mississippi or an elderly monolingual Spanish speaker in a busy urban ED. The FDA has cleared hundreds of AI-enabled devices over the past several years and the post-market surveillance requirements are, by most accounts I've read, pretty thin. Systems get validated in one hospital system and then sold to fifty others where the patient mix, the EHR configuration, and the workflow are all different. That's not a minor technical concern. That's how you get a tool that performs fine in the demo and then quietly produces bad recommendations in real deployment, and nobody catches it because the clinicians have been trained to trust the output.⏎⏎There's also something that bothers me about the framing of AI as a solution to clinician burnout. I've talked to enough nurses and hospitalists to know that what's burning them out is documentation burden, staffing ratios, and the feeling that they have no control over their work environment. AI tools often add to documentation burden, not reduce it, because now you have to document your interaction with the AI recommendation on top of everything else. And if a hospital uses "we have AI now" as a justification for cutting staff or reducing the number of physicians per unit, that's not burnout relief. That's a labor substitution strategy dressed up in patient-safety language.⏎⏎The reimbursement angle matters too. If CMS and commercial insurers start reimbursing AI-assisted care at higher rates, or if AI-generated prior authorization decisions become normalized, you are essentially building a financial architecture that locks in these tools before anyone has done the long-term outcome studies. That's backwards. Reimbursement should follow demonstrated clinical benefit, not precede it.⏎⏎I'm not saying there are zero legitimate uses. Radiology screening tools in settings with genuine radiologist shortages, that's a different conversation. But that's a narrow, specific case. What concerns me is the broad push to get AI into clinical decision support, into insurance determinations, into administrative functions that currently require humans who have actual accountability for their decisions. When a prior auth is denied by a human reviewer, there's a person you can appeal to, a paper trail, a licensed professional whose name is on the decision. When it's denied by an algorithm run by a vendor under contract, the diffusion of responsibility is real and the patient is the one who absorbs the cost of that confusion.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require prospective, independent clinical validation before any AI system is used in patient-facing decisions, mandatory incident reporting when AI recommendations are overridden or contribute to adverse events, and clear rules about who bears liability. I'd also want to see explicit restrictions on using AI outputs as the sole or primary basis for insurance coverage denials, which is already happening at some large commercial insurers regardless of what the policies technically say.⏎⏎I realize I haven't addressed every section of the RFI. Honestly the document is long and a lot of the questions assume a level of enthusiasm for rapid deployment that I don't share. The question about what barriers HHS should remove to accelerate adoption assumes removal of barriers is the right goal. Some of those barriers exist because people got hurt.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0095♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Martin♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔American Association of Neurological Surgeons♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the hospital for six weeks in 2021 with a complicated post-surgical infection, and what got her through it was not any piece of technology. It was a night nurse named Denise who noticed something was off about how my mother was holding her arm, something that wasn't in any chart, and who stayed an extra half hour asking questions until she figured out there was a secondary site of inflammation the imaging had missed. I don't know how you code that into a training dataset.⏎⏎That's what I keep coming back to when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. The framing of this RFI assumes that faster adoption is the goal and that the question is just how to remove obstacles. I'd like to push back on that assumption pretty directly.⏎⏎Medicine is not primarily an information retrieval problem. I understand why it can look like one from the outside, especially to people who work in technology policy or health informatics, where the problems that are visible are the ones involving data flow and interoperability and documentation burden. Those are real problems. But the solution to documentation burden is not to hand decision support over to a system trained on historical records that already contain decades of documented racial and socioeconomic bias. There's published research, including work from Ziad Obermeyer and colleagues going back to 2019, showing that a widely used commercial algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients had historically received less care, so the algorithm essentially learned to replicate that disparity. That's not an edge case. That's what happens when you treat medicine as an optimization problem without asking what you're optimizing for or whose experience is encoded in the training data.⏎⏎I'm also worried about what AI does to accountability structures. When a clinician makes a mistake, there is a person who is answerable. Licensing boards, malpractice law, hospital credentialing, peer review, these are imperfect systems but they exist and they name a responsible human. When an AI system contributes to a bad outcome, the accountability becomes genuinely murky. The clinician says they were following the tool. The hospital says the vendor validated it. The vendor says the hospital deployed it outside the approved use case. I've watched this dynamic play out in other regulated industries and it is not hypothetical.⏎⏎The RFI asks specifically about reimbursement pathways and regulatory changes that could accelerate adoption. My honest answer is that the current pace of adoption is already faster than the evidence base supports. The FDA's regulatory framework for software as a medical device is still catching up to the products already on the market. Creating new reimbursement incentives before the oversight infrastructure is mature would be putting the cart about three miles ahead of the horse.⏎⏎There's also an employment issue that I don't think gets taken seriously enough. When hospitals talk about AI reducing clinician burnout, what they often mean in practice is that AI will allow them to justify thinner staffing ratios. The productivity gains get captured by the institution, not the worker. Radiology techs, medical coders, prior authorization staff, these are real jobs held by people with specific training and expertise, and there is a pretty direct line between AI deployment in those functions and pressure to reduce headcount. The argument that new jobs will appear to replace them is the same argument that has been made about every automation wave, and it has a mixed record at best.⏎⏎I want to be clear that I'm not saying technology has no place in healthcare. Electronic records, telemedicine access in rural areas, better imaging analysis as a second-check tool, there are specific applications where the evidence is reasonable and the oversight is workable. But that is very different from the broad acceleration agenda this RFI seems to be building toward. The question HHS should be asking is not "how do we remove barriers to adoption" but "what evidence standard should be required before a clinical AI tool can be reimbursed, and who is legally responsible when it fails."⏎⏎On the question of insurance and prior authorization specifically, I'd urge HHS to look very carefully at what has already happened. Several large insurers have deployed AI-assisted denial tools that have generated denial rates far above historical averages for certain claim types, and at least one class action lawsuit filed in 2023 against Cigna alleged that physicians were spending an average of♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0096♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Rodriguez♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023, and what kept her alive wasn't an algorithm. It was a night nurse named Darnell who noticed she was breathing slightly differently at 3am and called the attending before the monitors flagged anything. I've been thinking about that a lot while reading about what HHS wants to do here.⏎⏎The question I want to address is really the framing of this whole request, which assumes that accelerating AI adoption is the goal and that the only thing left to figure out is how to remove obstacles. That's the wrong starting point. HHS should be asking whether AI belongs in clinical care at all, at least in anything like the scope being contemplated, before building regulatory infrastructure to speed it up.⏎⏎Here's what bothers me most. Medicine is not primarily an information problem. I know that sounds like a strange thing to say in 2024 but I genuinely believe it. When my mother couldn't describe her symptoms clearly because she was frightened and her english gets worse when she's scared, Darnell figured it out anyway by watching her, by knowing what she looked like the day before, by asking her son questions. No model trained on clinical notes from a hospital system in a different city, with different patient demographics and different documentation habits, was going to replicate that. And the danger isn't just that AI would have missed it. The danger is that an AI system would have produced some output, some risk score or recommendation, that looked authoritative and might have given the team a false sense that the situation was being monitored.⏎⏎There's actually published research on this. A 2019 study in Science by Obermeyer et al. found that a widely used commercial algorithm for identifying high-risk patients was systematically underestimating the severity of illness in Black patients because it used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, and Black patients had historically been given less care so their costs were lower even when they were sicker. The algorithm looked like it was working. It wasn't. And the people using it had no particular reason to suspect anything was wrong because the outputs seemed reasonable and the vendor wasn't volunteering information about how it worked.⏎⏎That's the thing that I don't think this RFI takes seriously enough. These systems can be wrong in ways that are invisible to the people relying on them. And in healthcare, invisible wrongness gets people hurt or killed before anyone figures out the pattern. There's a reason we have clinical trials and post-market surveillance for drugs and devices. We don't say "this medication looks promising, let's accelerate adoption and figure out safety as we go." But that is essentially what accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings means.⏎⏎I'm also genuinely worried about what this does to clinicians. Not just job losses, though that's real and I don't want to brush past it. I mean what it does to the people who stay. If a doctor's job increasingly becomes reviewing AI outputs and either accepting or overriding them, there's a lot of research on automation bias suggesting they will override less and less over time even when they should. Their skills atrophy. Their confidence in their own judgment erodes. And then when the system fails in some unusual edge case, the person nominally responsible for catching it hasn't been exercising the judgment muscles needed to catch it.⏎⏎HHS should not be in the business of accelerating this. HHS should be asking what guardrails would need to exist before any clinical AI tool is deployed on real patients, and whether those guardrails are even achievable given how these systems work. At a minimum I'd want to see mandatory prospective validation studies in the specific deployment context, not just retrospective performance on historical data. I'd want to see real auditing requirements with teeth, not voluntary transparency frameworks. And I'd want to see liability rules that don't let vendors hide behind "the clinician should have caught it."⏎⏎I realize this puts me on the skeptical end of this conversation. I'm not saying there's zero application for computational tools in medicine. But the specific framing of accelerating adoption as a policy goal, the idea that the regulatory job is to get out of the way faster, worries me quite a bit. The people who will bear the cost of getting this wrong are patients who are already in vulnerable situations, and most of them are not going to be submitting comments to this docket.⏎⏎One more thing I want to flag, and I'm not sure where it fits exactly in the RFI questions: the insurance and prior authorization piece. AI is already being used by insurers to deny claims, and there are active lawsuits about this. UnitedHealth's nH Predict tool was the subject of litigation in 2023 alleging that it was systematically terminating coverage for post-acute care patients before they had recovered, with a denial rate reportedly much higher than human reviewers would have produced. That's not a hypothetical risk. That's already happening. Whatever HHS does on the clinical care side, it should not be creating an environment where that kind of deployment gets easier or more normalized.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0097♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Martin♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔Stanford Center for Biomedical Informatics Research♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in March of 2023, and what got her through it was not any algorithm. It was a night nurse named Patricia who noticed something was off about how my mother was breathing at 2am and called the attending before the monitors flagged anything. I don't know how to put that into a dataset.⏎⏎That's where I keep getting stuck when I read about this push to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. The word "accelerate" is doing a lot of work in this RFI and I don't think HHS has really grappled with what it means to speed up the deployment of systems that, by the agency's own framing, are still being figured out. You don't accelerate into uncertainty in medicine. You're supposed to be cautious.⏎⏎On the specific question of what regulatory actions HHS can take: my honest answer is that the most responsible thing the department can do right now is slow down, not speed up. I understand that's not the answer the RFI is looking for, but the framing of the question itself troubles me. The RFI asks how to "accelerate adoption" as though adoption is the goal rather than patient outcomes. These are not the same thing. A hospital can adopt an AI-powered prior authorization tool tomorrow and adoption goes up while actual care gets worse, because now a nurse has to argue with a machine to get a patient's medication approved instead of making a phone call to someone with discretion.⏎⏎That prior authorization example isn't hypothetical. UnitedHealth's use of the nH Predict algorithm to deny post-acute care claims was the subject of a federal lawsuit filed in 2023. The complaint alleged the system had a 90% overturn rate on appeal, meaning the AI was wrong nine times out of ten when humans actually reviewed the decisions. And yet the system kept running because it was cheaper to let it run and let some percentage of patients not bother to appeal. That is what AI adoption in healthcare actually looks like in practice, not the demos, not the whitepapers.⏎⏎I'm also genuinely worried about what happens to the people doing this work. Radiology techs, medical coders, prior auth nurses, even some diagnostic roles -- these are real jobs held by people with real training, and the case for AI in those spaces is often just a case for doing the same work with fewer people. I don't think HHS should be in the business of subsidizing that through reimbursement incentives or certification pathways that make it easier for health systems to replace workers with tools that haven't been validated in their specific patient populations.⏎⏎The validation point matters a lot and I don't think it gets enough attention. A model trained on data from a large academic medical center in Boston is not the same as a model deployed in a rural critical access hospital in eastern Kentucky. The patient populations are different, the documentation practices are different, the staffing ratios are different. When the model fails, and some will fail, the question of who is responsible becomes genuinely unanswerable. The clinician says they were following the tool's recommendation. The hospital says they implemented it according to vendor specs. The vendor says the hospital used it outside the indicated population. This is not a hypothetical liability puzzle, it's already happening and there isn't a clear regulatory framework for it.⏎⏎I think HHS should require, at minimum, prospective outcome studies before any AI clinical decision support tool can be eligible for reimbursement incentives. Not retrospective validation on historical data, actual prospective studies in the deployment setting. That would slow things down considerably and that is the point.⏎⏎One more thing and I realize this is somewhat separate from the regulatory question but it keeps coming back to me: there is something lost when patients know or suspect that their care is being shaped by a system rather than a person. I've talked to older patients, my mother included, who feel a kind of abandonment when they sense that the human in the room is really just confirming what a screen already decided. That feeling isn't irrational and it isn't just a communication problem that better UI design can fix. Trust in medicine is built on the belief that someone is actually thinking about you specifically, and that belief is harder to sustain the more the process looks like pattern matching.⏎⏎I don't know what the right threshold is for when AI assistance becomes AI replacement, and I'm not sure anyone does yet, which is itself a reason for caution rather than acceleration. The RFI asks about removing barriers to adoption and I'd ask HHS to consider whether some of those barriers exist for good reasons that just aren't visible yet because we haven't had enough time with these systems in real clinical environments to see what goes wrong at scale.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0098♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Martinez♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February 2023, and during that time I watched three different physicians come to her bedside, look at her, talk to her, ask her questions that weren't on any checklist, and change their clinical approach based on things that I genuinely don't think would show up in any dataset. One of them noticed she was gripping the blanket a certain way and figured out she was in more pain than she was reporting. That's not a data point. I don't know what you call it exactly, but it's not something a model trained on discharge summaries is going to replicate.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS is looking at ways to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my honest reaction is: slow down. A lot.⏎⏎What I want to address is the question about what regulatory actions HHS might take to speed this up, because I think the framing of the question itself is the problem. The assumption seems to be that adoption is the goal and the question is just how fast and how smoothly. But nobody seems to be asking whether the underlying technology is actually ready for the consequences of being wrong in a hospital setting, as opposed to being wrong in, say, a streaming recommendation algorithm where the stakes are whether someone watches a bad movie.⏎⏎There's published research showing that several widely-used clinical AI tools perform significantly worse on Black patients than on white patients, not marginally worse, significantly, because the training data reflects decades of undertreatment and different care patterns that the models just absorb and replicate. The pulse oximetry problem is maybe the most well-documented version of this, where devices were systematically less accurate on darker skin and that error propagated into clinical records for years before people started taking it seriously. If AI systems get trained on those records, they inherit those errors and then present them with a confidence score. That's not progress.⏎⏎What bothers me most though is the accountability question, and I keep coming back to this even when I try to think about it charitably. Right now if a physician makes a wrong call, there are malpractice standards, there's a licensing board, there's some mechanism for consequence. If an AI system contributes to a wrong call, who exactly is responsible? The vendor will say the hospital didn't implement it correctly. The hospital will say the clinician had the final call. The clinician will say the AI said it was fine. I've seen this dynamic play out in other regulated industries and it doesn't resolve cleanly, it just means patients or their families end up with nobody to hold accountable and a very expensive legal process that goes nowhere.⏎⏎I'm also genuinely skeptical of the argument that AI will reduce clinician burnout. Every technology introduced into healthcare in the last twenty years has been sold partly on that promise and most of it has made documentation worse, not better. The EHR transition was supposed to free up clinician time. I think most physicians would laugh at that claim now. I'm not saying AI is the same, but I am saying the burden of proof should be on the people making the promise, not on the people asking for evidence.⏎⏎If HHS wants to do something useful, it could start by requiring that any AI tool used in direct clinical decision support be validated prospectively on the actual patient population at the institution deploying it, not just on whatever dataset the vendor used for the original study. Retrospective validation on a homogeneous academic medical center dataset and then deployment at a safety-net hospital serving a very different population is not acceptable. And the validation data should be public, or at least available to independent reviewers, not just sitting in a vendor's internal files.⏎⏎I also think there needs to be a much more honest conversation about what AI in insurance prior authorization actually means. It means automated denials, faster and at higher volume. The companies using these tools have a financial incentive to deny and a tool that lets them do it at scale with a veneer of clinical legitimacy. UnitedHealth's use of an algorithm called nH Predict to make post-acute care coverage decisions resulted in a class action lawsuit in 2023 precisely because the denial rate was so high and so disconnected from individual patient circumstances. That's already happening. HHS accelerating adoption in that context isn't helping patients, it's helping payers.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0099♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Walker♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔American Association of Physician Assistants♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the hospital for six weeks in 2019 after a series of strokes, and what I remember most is how much it mattered that her neurologist actually knew her. Not her chart. Her. He knew she was a retired teacher who would downplay her symptoms because she didn't want to be a burden. He caught things that wouldn't have shown up in any pattern-matching system because they required understanding who she was as a person. That's the thing I keep coming back to when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care.⏎⏎I want to focus on two things specifically: the question of what problems AI is actually solving, and who bears the risk when it fails.⏎⏎On the first point - I genuinely don't understand what clinical problem AI is supposed to solve in a way that human judgment and adequate staffing couldn't solve better. When hospitals say they need AI to reduce clinician burnout, what they often mean is that they have understaffed their facilities to a point where clinicians are drowning in documentation and administrative tasks. The correct response to that is hiring more staff, not deploying software that monitors and nudges and second-guesses the remaining staff. There's a study from 2023 by researchers at UCSF looking at an AI sepsis alert tool that was supposed to catch early sepsis cases - and when they actually evaluated it in practice, nurses were ignoring the alerts at high rates because there were so many false positives. The tool didn't reduce burden. It added noise. And this was a well-resourced academic medical center with the capacity to study the thing. What happens at a rural hospital in Mississippi where nobody has time or expertise to evaluate whether the tool is working or not?⏎⏎That connects to my second concern about risk. Right now, if a doctor makes a wrong call, there is a person who is accountable. Their license is at stake. Their reputation. Their conscience. When an AI system makes a wrong call, the accountability evaporates. The hospital says the vendor is responsible, the vendor says the clinician should have overridden the recommendation, the clinician says they were following protocol. I've seen this play out already in the context of AI-driven prior authorization denials by insurers. Cigna and other large insurers have faced lawsuits and investigations over automated denial systems that were rejecting claims in bulk without meaningful individual review - a physician spending something like 1.2 seconds per case, according to reporting by ProPublica from 2023. That's not AI assisting human judgment. That's AI replacing human judgment while maintaining legal cover by having a human technically "in the loop."⏎⏎HHS should be asking whether the efficiency gains being promised are real or whether they're just a transfer of risk onto patients and workers. I'm not saying there's no possible application of machine learning that could ever help anyone in a clinical context. But the framing of this RFI - "accelerating adoption" - treats deployment as the goal rather than treating patient outcomes as the goal. Those aren't the same thing and the difference matters a lot.⏎⏎What I'd want to see instead is a moratorium on AI-driven insurance coverage decisions until there are actual audit mechanisms with teeth, not voluntary transparency commitments from vendors who have every incentive to obscure how their systems work. And I'd want any clinical decision support tool to be required to demonstrate performance in the specific population it's being deployed in, not just in whatever dataset the developer used for validation. A tool validated on data from a large urban health system may perform very differently in a federally qualified health center serving a predominantly Spanish-speaking population, and right now there's no requirement to check.⏎⏎I realize this comment doesn't engage with the full scope of the RFI. Honestly the scope is so broad that responding to all of it would take more than a public comment form allows. But I'd rather make two points clearly than gesture vaguely at everything.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0100♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Jackson♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔Suki AI♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Bakersfield in February 2023, and what got her through it was not an algorithm. It was a night nurse named Patricia who noticed something was off with her breathing at 2am and called the attending even though the monitors were technically fine. I don't know how to put that in a dataset.⏎⏎That's the thing I keep coming back to when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings. The word "accelerate" is doing a lot of work there. Accelerate toward what, exactly? The RFI asks about barriers to adoption and how to remove them, but I think the more honest question is whether some of those barriers exist for good reasons and whether we are making a serious mistake by treating them as problems to be solved rather than safeguards to be respected.⏎⏎Medicine is not primarily an information-processing problem. I know that's how it can look from the outside, especially to people who come to healthcare from tech or from insurance, where efficiency metrics make sense. But a lot of what a good clinician does is interpret things that are ambiguous, incomplete, or contradictory. Patients don't always tell the truth. They leave things out because they're embarrassed or scared or because they don't know what's relevant. They describe symptoms in ways that don't match textbook presentations. Their records are incomplete because they moved, or because a previous provider used a different system, or because they just never got diagnosed for something they've been quietly managing on their own for twenty years. An AI trained on historical data is going to perform well on patients who look like the patients in the training set. That's basically what these systems do. And the patients who don't fit that profile, the ones with unusual presentations, the ones from communities that were historically underserved and therefore underrepresented in the data, those are exactly the patients who are most likely to be harmed when the system gets it wrong.⏎⏎There's a study from 2019 by Obermeyer and colleagues in Science magazine that found a widely used commercial algorithm in hospital systems was significantly underestimating the health needs of Black patients compared to white patients with the same actual health status, because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients had historically had less access to care and therefore lower costs. The bias wasn't obvious. The system looked fine from the outside. That's the part that worries me most. These systems can be wrong in ways that are hard to see and hard to audit, especially once they're embedded in clinical workflows and everyone just assumes the output is reasonable.⏎⏎I want to be fair here. I don't think everyone pushing for AI in healthcare is acting in bad faith. Some of this technology probably does help in specific, narrow applications. Radiology image review, maybe. Certain kinds of pattern detection in labs. I'm not saying none of it is ever useful. But the RFI is asking HHS to think about how to speed up adoption broadly, across clinical care generally, and I think that framing gets things backward.⏎⏎What concerns me about the reimbursement angle specifically is that once Medicare and Medicaid start paying for AI-assisted care, and once hospitals realize they can justify thinner staffing because they have "AI support," the economic pressure becomes very hard to reverse. A hospital administrator in 2027 who is trying to cut costs is going to look at that reimbursement structure and see an opportunity, and the clinical staff who think the AI is wrong about a particular patient are going to be in a very difficult position arguing against a tool that the institution has financially committed to. That's not a hypothetical. That's just how institutional incentives work.⏎⏎Patricia, the night nurse, is probably already dealing with documentation software that takes time away from patient contact. Adding AI recommendations on top of that workflow doesn't automatically make her job easier or her patients safer. It adds another layer of things to second-guess or defer to, and the research on automation bias in clinical settings suggests that humans working alongside automated systems tend over time to over-rely on the system's outputs, especially under stress and time pressure. Which is most of nursing, most of the time.⏎⏎I don't think HHS should be in the business of accelerating this. I think HHS should be asking much harder questions about what safeguards need to exist before any of this scales, who is accountable when an AI-influenced decision causes harm, and whether the patients most likely to be affected by errors in these systems have any meaningful way to know that AI was involved in their care or to opt out of it.⏎⏎On that last point I haven't seen anything in this RFI that takes patient consent seriously as a real operational question rather than a checkbox.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
